/* Índice del buscador en inglés (generado por _src-publicaciones/ingles/indice_en.py, no editar a mano). */
window.CATTO_INDICE = (window.CATTO_INDICE || []).concat([{"t":"Further reading","d":"CMOS memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-cmos#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Bruce Jacob, Spencer W. Ng and David T. Wang. Memory Systems: Cache, DRAM, Disk. Morgan Kaufmann, 2008. The reference on how a computer's memories work inside, from the cell to the system. Jan M. Rabaey, Anantha Chandrakasan and Borivoje Nikolić. Digital Integrated Circuits: A Design Perspective. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2003. The memories chapter: cells, sense amplifiers and array organization. Rino Micheloni, Luca Crippa and Alessia Marelli. Inside NAND Flash Memories. Springer, 2010. The flash cell, its programming mechanisms and the reliability problems."},{"t":"What distinguishes one memory from another","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#clasificacion","x":"01 What distinguishes one memory from another A memory stores bits in cells, and each technology stores the bit in a different physical way: the state of a flip-flop, the charge of a capacitor, the charge trapped on an insulated gate, or the polarization of a material. All of its properties follow from that physical form: whether it loses the data when power is removed, how long it takes to read and write, how many times it can be rewritten, and how much area each bit takes. The technician program page presents the types; here we see why they are the way they are. Memory Cell Volatile? Rewrites Use SRAM 6 transistors (flip-flop) Yes Unlimited Registers, cache, microcontroller RAM DRAM 1 transistor and 1 capacitor Yes, and it must also be refreshed Unlimited Main memory of computers NOR flash Floating-gate transistor No About 100,000 Microcontroller program; executed in place NAND flash Floating gate or charge trap, in series No From 1000 to 100,000 depending on bits per cell Solid-state drives, SD cards EEPROM Floating gate with byte-wise erase No Around one million Configuration and calibration FRAM Ferroelectric capacitor No On the order of 10 14 Frequent data logging at low power"},{"t":"Internal organization","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#organizacion","x":"02 Internal organization The cells form a matrix of rows and columns. A row decoder activates an entire row, the sense amplifiers detect the small voltage differences on its columns, and a column multiplexer chooses which ones come out. A memory of \\( 2^A \\) words of \\( w \\) bits needs \\( A \\) address lines and \\( w \\) data lines: a 128 K × 8 one has 17 address lines and 8 data lines. In a system with several memories and peripherals, address decoding divides up the space: the high bits of the address choose which chip is enabled, through its select signal, and the low bits choose the word within the chip. The result is the system’s memory map."},{"t":"SRAM and DRAM","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#ram","x":"03 SRAM and DRAM The SRAM cell is a flip-flop of two cross-coupled inverters plus two access transistors: it holds the data as long as power is applied, is read without destroying it, and is fast, but takes six transistors per bit. The DRAM cell stores the bit as the charge on a capacitor of a few femtofarads, behind a transistor. It is much denser, but the charge leaks away and each cell must be refreshed, typically every 64 ms, and reading discharges the capacitor, so every read is followed by a rewrite. DRAMs multiplex the address: first the row, then the column. DDR memories transfer on both clock edges: a DDR4-3200 module makes 3200 million transfers of 64 bits per second, 25.6 GB/s."},{"t":"Nonvolatile memories","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#flash","x":"04 Nonvolatile memories In a flash cell, the bit is the charge trapped on a gate surrounded by oxide. That charge shifts the threshold of the transistor, and the read detects whether it conducts or not. To write and erase, charge is driven through the oxide with high fields, and each pass wears it down a little: that is why the number of rewrites is limited. NOR NAND Access Random by word, like a RAM By pages of kilobytes Erase By sectors By blocks of hundreds of pages Density and cost per bit Lower density, higher cost Maximum density, minimum cost Management Almost none Error correction, bad blocks and wear leveling Storing more than one bit per cell, with several charge levels, multiplies the capacity but narrows the margins between levels: a one-bit cell (SLC) withstands on the order of 100,000 erase cycles; a three-bit one (TLC), a few thousand."},{"t":"Errors and error-correcting codes","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#ecc","x":"05 Errors and error-correcting codes No memory is perfect. An alpha particle from the package or a neutron from cosmic rays can flip a bit in a DRAM or an SRAM (a soft error ), and in a flash the bits degrade with use. Systems that cannot fail store, along with each word, parity bits computed with a Hamming code. To correct one error in \\( m \\) data bits, \\( r \\) parity bits are needed such that: \\[ 2^r \\ge m + r + 1 \\] With \\( r \\) bits you can signal \\( 2^r \\) situations: no error, or an error in any of the \\( m + r \\) bits. With one more global parity bit, the code corrects one error and detects two (SECDED). For 64 data bits, 8 check bits are needed: ECC memory modules are 72 bits wide. In the Hamming code, the parity bits go in the positions that are powers of two, and each one covers the positions whose binary number has that bit set to 1. On reading, the parities are recomputed: the binary number formed by those that fail, the syndrome, is directly the position of the erroneous bit. Lab · a SECDED Hamming code Four data bits are encoded into eight: three Hamming parities in positions 1, 2 and 4, and a global parity in position 0. You choose the data, then click the stored bits to alter them, as a particle would, and the decoder responds. clear the errors"},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics System Memories it uses Microcontroller NOR flash for the program, SRAM for the data, sometimes EEPROM emulated in flash Server DRAM with 72-bit SECDED ECC Solid-state drive NAND with a controller that corrects errors and spreads the wear Satellite Radiation-hardened memories, with ECC and periodic rereading (scrubbing) Energy meter FRAM or EEPROM for the registers that change often"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Memory map Design the address decoding of a system with a 32 K × 8 EPROM, an 8 K × 8 SRAM and a peripheral with 16 registers on a 16-bit address bus. Draw the map. Activity 2 · Reading and writing an I²C EEPROM With a microcontroller, write a page to a serial EEPROM, measure the write time and verify the readback. Activity 3 · Hamming in firmware Program the SECDED encoding and decoding of a byte and inject errors to test the correction."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Writing to flash too often. A log written every second uses up 100,000 cycles in a little over a day. Forgetting that flash is erased in blocks. Changing one byte requires erasing and rewriting the entire block. Confusing capacity in bits and in bytes. An 8 Mbit chip has 1 MB. Believing that simple parity corrects. It only detects an odd number of errors."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment How many address lines does a 512 K × 16 memory have? 19, because \\( 2^{19} = 524\\,288 \\). How many parity bits does a Hamming code that corrects one error in 16 bits need? Five: \\( 2^5 = 32 \\ge 16 + 5 + 1 \\). With SECDED, six. In a Hamming (7,4) code, parities 1 and 4 fail and parity 2 does not. Which bit is wrong? The one in position 5 (101 in binary). Why does a DRAM need refresh and an SRAM does not? Because it stores the bit as charge on a capacitor that discharges through leakage; the SRAM stores it in a flip-flop that sustains itself as long as power is applied."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Electronic memories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/memorias-electronicas#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Bruce Jacob, Spencer W. Ng and David T. Wang. Memory Systems: Cache, DRAM, Disk. Morgan Kaufmann, 2008. The reference on the organization, timing and design of memory systems. Betty Prince. Semiconductor Memories: A Handbook of Design, Manufacture and Application. 2nd ed., Wiley, 1991. The cells and circuits of each type of memory, from inside the chip. Shu Lin and Daniel J. Costello. Error Control Coding. 2nd ed., Pearson, 2004. Hamming codes and their successors, with the complete theory."},{"t":"Translating a signal in frequency","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#conversion","x":"01 Translating a signal in frequency A mixer multiplies the input signal by that of a local oscillator. By the product-of-cosines identity, the sum and the difference of the frequencies appear at the output: \\[ \\cos\\omega_{RF}t \\cdot \\cos\\omega_{LO}t = \\tfrac12\\cos(\\omega_{RF} - \\omega_{LO})t \\] \\[ \\qquad + \\tfrac12\\cos(\\omega_{RF} + \\omega_{LO})t \\] A filter selects one of the two, the intermediate frequency (IF). It is the same spectrum translation as in linear modulation, used to bring the received signal down to a fixed frequency where it is easy to filter and amplify."},{"t":"The image frequency","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#imagen","x":"02 The image frequency The mixer cannot tell whether the input is above or below the local oscillator: two frequencies give the same IF. If the oscillator is above the desired signal (high-side injection), the other one is on the other side: \\[ f_{FI} = |f_{RF} - f_{LO}| \\] \\[ f_{imagen} = f_{RF} + 2 f_{FI} \\ \\ \\text{(high-side injection)} \\] In an FM receiver with a 10.7 MHz IF tuned to 100 MHz, the oscillator is at 110.7 MHz and the image at 121.4 MHz. A filter before the mixer has to attenuate it; that is why a higher IF makes image rejection easier, and a lower one, channel selectivity. That trade-off leads to double-conversion receivers, with a high first IF for the image and a low second one for selectivity, and to image-reject mixers, which combine two mixers in quadrature to cancel it, as will be seen in modulators and demodulators."},{"t":"Real mixers","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#tipos","x":"03 Real mixers Type How it works Feature A diode or transistor The nonlinearity generates the products Simple, with many unwanted products Diode double-balanced A ring of four diodes switched by the local oscillator Suppresses the oscillator and the RF at the output; conversion loss of about 6 dB Gilbert cell Switched differential pairs, as in the MC1496 Conversion gain, integrable Sampling or passive CMOS mixer Switches toggled at the oscillator frequency Good linearity in integrated receivers The specifications of a mixer are its conversion gain or loss, the noise figure, the isolation between ports and the linearity, with the third-order intercept point from amplifier measurements. The Gilbert cell is analyzed in the MC1496 publication."},{"t":"Spurious responses","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#espurias","x":"04 Spurious responses A real mixer does not only multiply: its nonlinearities generate all the products \\( m f_{LO} \\pm n f_{RF} \\). Each combination can convert to the IF a signal that is not the desired one. An input signal \\( f \\) produces output at the IF if: \\[ |m f_{LO} - n f| = f_{FI} \\] \\[ f = \\frac{m f_{LO} \\pm f_{FI}}{n} \\] \\( m + n \\) is the order of the response; the higher it is, the weaker. One of the most troublesome is the half-IF response, with m = n = 2, at \\( f_{FI}/2 \\) from the desired signal toward the oscillator: it is so close that the input filter barely attenuates it. Lab · image and spurious responses of a receiver A receiver tuned in the FM band. On the input frequency axis, from 50 to 200 MHz, the green bar is the desired signal; the red one, the image, and the gray ones, the spurious responses up to fourth order of each factor, with lower height the higher their order. Tuned frequency = 10.7 MHz IF 45 MHz IF Local oscillator above (high-side injection) Local oscillator below (low-side injection)"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Conversion Mix two generators with a double-balanced mixer and observe the output on the spectrum analyzer. Measure the conversion loss and the oscillator leakage. Activity 2 · Image In an FM radio, inject a signal at the image frequency and measure how much stronger it has to be than the desired one to be heard equally. Activity 3 · Half-IF Use a generator to find the half-IF response of the same receiver and measure its rejection."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the image when choosing the IF. With a low IF, the image is close and the input filter does not reject it. Driving the mixer with too little local oscillator. The diodes do not switch fully and loss and distortion increase. Ignoring the isolation between ports. The oscillator leaking out through the antenna interferes with other receivers. Checking only the image. Low-order spurs can fall in the band of interest."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment An AM receiver with a 455 kHz IF and high-side injection is tuned to 1 MHz. Where are the oscillator and the image? The oscillator at 1.455 MHz and the image at 1.91 MHz. Where does the half-IF response of the FM receiver tuned to 100 MHz fall? At 105.35 MHz, between the signal and the oscillator. What advantage does a double-balanced mixer have? It suppresses the local oscillator and the RF at the output, and reduces many unwanted products. Why do modern receivers use a high IF for the first conversion? To move the image away and be able to reject it with a fixed input filter."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Mixers","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mezcladores#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Stephen A. Maas. Microwave Mixers. 2nd ed., Artech House, 1993. The theory and design of diode and transistor mixers. Behzad Razavi. RF Microelectronics. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2011. Integrated mixers and image-reject receiver architectures. Wayne Tomasi. Electronic Communications Systems: Fundamentals Through Advanced. 4th ed., Prentice Hall, 2001 (Spanish edition: “Sistemas de comunicaciones electrónicas”, 4th ed., Pearson, 2003). The superheterodyne receiver, the image and the choice of the IF."},{"t":"The production function","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#produccion","x":"01 The production function The production function relates the maximum quantity that can be produced to the quantities of factors employed. In the short run some factor is fixed —the plant, the machines— and adding labor increases output less and less: this is the law of diminishing returns. The marginal product, what the last worker contributes, may first grow and then falls; when the marginal product is below the average, the average falls. In the long run all factors are variable, and as the scale increases there may be economies of scale —the average cost falls, as in semiconductor manufacturing— or diseconomies. The resulting costs were covered in costs."},{"t":"Supply, demand and equilibrium","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#mercado","x":"02 Supply, demand and equilibrium Demand relates the price to the quantity that buyers want to purchase: it falls as the price rises. Supply relates the price to the quantity that sellers want to sell: it rises with the price. Where they cross is the equilibrium. The elasticity measures how much the quantity responds to the price: \\[ \\varepsilon = -\\frac{\\Delta Q / Q}{\\Delta P / P} \\] If \\( \\varepsilon > 1 \\) demand is elastic: the price rises 1 % and the quantity falls by more than 1 %. Goods with close substitutes have elastic demand; necessities, inelastic. With linear curves \\( P = a - bQ \\), the elasticity at a point is \\( P/(bQ) \\). A per-unit tax separates the price paid by buyers from the one received by sellers. The quantity falls, the government collects revenue, and a surplus that nobody gains is lost: the deadweight loss. How the burden is shared depends on the elasticities: the side of the market that can escape the least pays more. Lab · market equilibrium with a tax Demand \\(P = a - bQ\\) (blue) and supply \\(P = 100 + Q\\) (green); with the tax, supply shifts upward (orange). The violet rectangle is the tax revenue and the red triangle, the deadweight loss. Demand level a = Demand slope b = Tax per unit t ="},{"t":"Types of market","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#mercados","x":"03 Types of market Market Sellers Product Power over price Perfect competition Many Identical None: the market sets the price Monopolistic competition Many Differentiated Little, thanks to the brand or the design Oligopoly Few Identical or differentiated Large, but each depends on what the others do Monopoly One No close substitutes Maximum, limited by demand and regulation The semiconductor industry, with enormous investments per plant, tends toward oligopoly; common passive components come close to competition. In all cases the firm maximizes its profit by producing where marginal revenue equals marginal cost; in perfect competition marginal revenue is the price."},{"t":"The decisions of the agents","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#agentes","x":"04 The decisions of the agents The consumer chooses, given income and prices —the budget constraint—, the combination of goods that gives the highest utility: spending so that the last peso yields the same in each good. The firm decides how much to produce and with what combination of factors, seeking the lowest cost for each level of output. The government intervenes with taxes, subsidies, price ceilings or floors and regulation of markets with dominant power; each intervention changes the incentives of the other agents."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Diminishing returns Measure how many boards are assembled in an hour with one, two, three and four students sharing a single soldering iron, and calculate the marginal product. Activity 2 · Elasticity With the prices of a component at different distributors and the quantities from the discount lists, estimate the elasticity. Activity 3 · Market structure Classify the markets for microcontrollers, resistors, phones and board fabrication services."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing a change in quantity demanded with a change in demand. The first is a movement along the curve; the second, a shift. Believing that the tax is paid by whoever remits it. The burden is shared according to the elasticities. Identifying the slope with the elasticity. On a straight line the slope is constant, but the elasticity changes at every point. Thinking that diminishing returns appear from the first worker. They appear when the fixed factor begins to limit."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is the equilibrium with \\(P = 1000 - 2Q\\) and \\(P = 100 + Q\\)? \\(Q = 300\\) and \\(P = 400\\). With a tax of ARS 90, how much do buyers pay and how much do sellers receive? Buyers pay ARS 460 and sellers receive ARS 370; 270 units are sold. How much does the government collect and how much efficiency is lost? It collects ARS 24,300 and the deadweight loss is ARS 1350. Where does a profit-maximizing firm produce? Where marginal revenue equals marginal cost."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Microeconomics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/microeconomia#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading N. Gregory Mankiw. Principles of Economics. Cengage. (Spanish edition: “Principios de economía”.) Supply, demand, elasticity and tax incidence. Robert S. Pindyck and Daniel L. Rubinfeld. Microeconomics. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Microeconomía”.) Production, costs and market structures in greater depth. Hal R. Varian. Intermediate Microeconomics: A Modern Approach. W. W. Norton. (Spanish edition: “Microeconomía intermedia: un enfoque actual”, Antoni Bosch.) Consumer and firm theory with mathematical tools."},{"t":"From the physical system to the equations","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#modelo","x":"01 From the physical system to the equations To design a controller you need a model of the plant: equations that describe how it responds to its inputs. It is obtained by applying the physical laws of each domain, Kirchhoff's laws in circuits, Newton's laws in mechanics, energy and mass balances in thermal and chemical processes, and linearizing around the operating point. The analogies between domains mean that the same type of equation describes very different systems: Domain Effort Flow Stores energy Dissipates Electrical Voltage Current Capacitance, inductance Resistance Rotational mechanical Torque Angular velocity Inertia, spring Viscous friction Thermal Temperature Heat flow Thermal capacitance Thermal resistance Hydraulic Pressure Flow rate Tank, fluid inertance Restriction"},{"t":"A complete example: the DC motor","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#motor","x":"02 A complete example: the DC motor The permanent-magnet DC motor couples an electrical circuit, with resistance \\( R \\) and inductance \\( L \\), and a mechanical load, with inertia \\( J \\) and friction \\( b \\). The back EMF is \\( K\\omega \\) and the torque is \\( Ki \\). With the state variables \\( i \\) and \\( \\omega \\): \\[ L \\frac{di}{dt} = v - R\\,i - K\\omega \\] \\[ J \\frac{d\\omega}{dt} = K\\,i - b\\,\\omega - T_L \\] Two first-order equations, one for each element that stores energy. \\( T_L \\) is the load torque. How the motor works was covered in DC machines."},{"t":"State space and transfer function","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#estados","x":"03 State space and transfer function The same model can be written in two ways. In state space, as a system of first-order equations in matrix form, which admits several inputs and outputs, initial conditions and nonlinearities, and is integrated numerically. With the Laplace transform and zero initial conditions, as a transfer function between one input and one output: \\[ \\dot{\\mathbf x} = A\\mathbf x + B u, \\quad y = C\\mathbf x + D u \\] \\[ \\frac{\\Omega(s)}{V(s)} = \\frac{K}{(Ls + R)(Js + b) + K^2} \\] The poles of the transfer function are the eigenvalues of \\( A \\). If the electrical constant \\( L/R \\) is much smaller than the mechanical one, the motor is approximated with a single pole: \\( \\Omega/V = K_m/(\\tau_m s + 1) \\), with \\( \\tau_m = RJ/(Rb + K^2) \\). Lab · the DC motor, full and approximate A motor with \\( R = 1\\ \\Omega \\), \\( K = 0.05\\text{ V}\\cdot\\text{s/rad} \\) and \\( b = 10^{-5}\\text{ N}\\cdot\\text{m}\\cdot\\text{s} \\), driven by a 12 V step. The blue curve is the speed of the full model, integrated with fourth-order Runge-Kutta in 4000 steps; the dotted one, that of the first-order model; below, the current. Inductance L = Inertia J ="},{"t":"Simulation","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#simulacion","x":"04 Simulation To simulate is to integrate the state equations numerically. Euler's method advances using the derivative at the start of the step; fourth-order Runge-Kutta averages four derivatives and is much more accurate with the same step. The step has to be considerably smaller than the fastest time constant of the system: if there are very different constants, the system is stiff and a variable-step or implicit integrator is preferable. The usual tools, such as Simulink, Xcos, Modelica or the Python libraries, do this automatically, but the model is written by the engineer."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Identify a motor Measure \\( R \\) with the motor stalled, \\( K \\) with the motor unloaded as a generator, and \\( \\tau_m \\) with the speed step response. Build the model and compare the simulation with the measurement. Activity 2 · Euler and Runge-Kutta Simulate the motor in Python with both methods and different steps, and compare with the exact solution. Activity 3 · A thermal process Model a heated block with its thermal capacitance and resistance and verify the time constant with a measurement."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using the model away from the point where it was linearized. With large excursions, the nonlinearities change the response. Neglecting the inductance without checking. It is only valid if the electrical constant is much smaller than the mechanical one. Simulating with a large step. The simulation can oscillate or diverge even if the system is stable. Confusing the transfer function with the system. It describes one input and one output with zero initial conditions."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What final speed does the lab motor reach with 12 V? \\( K V/(Rb + K^2) = 0.6/0.00251 \\approx 239 \\) rad/\\( s \\). What is \\( \\tau_m \\) with \\( J = 10^{-5}\\text{ kg}\\cdot\\text{m}^2 \\)? \\( 10^{-5}/0.00251 \\approx 4 \\) ms. How many state variables does a system with two independent capacitors and one inductor need? Three. What is the relationship between the poles of the transfer function and the matrix A? The poles are the eigenvalues of A."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Modeling and simulation of dynamic systems","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelado-sistemas#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Katsuhiko Ogata. System Dynamics. 4th ed., Pearson Prentice Hall, 2004 (Spanish edition: “Dinámica de sistemas”, Prentice Hall Hispanoamericana, 1987). The modeling of mechanical, electrical, thermal and hydraulic systems with the same method. Norman S. Nise. Control Systems Engineering. 8th ed., Wiley, 2019. State-space and transfer-function modeling with many examples. Gene F. Franklin, J. David Powell and Abbas Emami-Naeini. Feedback Control of Dynamic Systems. 8th ed., Pearson, 2019. Models of real systems, from the motor to the airplane, and their simulation."},{"t":"The atom that classical physics could not sustain","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#rutherford","x":"01 The atom that classical physics could not sustain Rutherford's scattering experiment (1911) —alpha particles against a gold foil, with some bouncing straight back— brought down Thomson's “plum pudding” model and left an atom with almost all of its mass and all of its positive charge concentrated in a tiny nucleus, with the electrons circling around it. The problem is that this atom, calculated with classical electromagnetism, cannot exist. An accelerating charge radiates energy —it is the same physics that makes an antenna work— and an electron in orbit accelerates all the time, even at constant speed, because it changes direction. Radiating energy, it would lose radius in a spiral until it fell into the nucleus. \\[ t_{\\text{collapse}} \\approx 1.6\\times10^{-11}\\text{ s} \\] The time an electron would take to fall into the nucleus according to classical electromagnetism, starting from the Bohr radius. Sixteen picoseconds: no atom should last longer than that, and yet the universe has 13.8 billion years of stable atoms."},{"t":"The Bohr atom","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#bohr","x":"02 The Bohr atom In 1913, Bohr solved the problem by decree: he postulated that the electron can only occupy certain orbits, those where its angular momentum is an integer multiple of \\( \\hbar \\), and that in those orbits —and only in those— it does not radiate. It emits light only when jumping from one orbit to another, and that light comes out as a photon with exactly the energy of the difference. \\[ L = n\\hbar \\qquad r_n = n^2 a_0 \\qquad E_n = -\\frac{13.6\\text{ eV}}{n^2}, \\quad n=1,2,3,\\ldots \\] \\( a_0 = 0.529 \\) Å is the Bohr radius. With \\( n=1 \\) the hydrogen electron is at exactly that distance from the nucleus, with \\( E_1 = -13.6 \\) eV: that is the ionization energy of hydrogen, measured in the laboratory with a precision of parts per million. The postulate had —in 1913— no fundamental justification; it was a rule that worked. It was justified by Schrödinger twelve years later, when the quantization of \\( L \\) came out by itself from requiring that the electron's wave function fit without discontinuities around the nucleus, with no further hypothesis. Lab · the hydrogen lines You choose the series and the starting level; the level diagram marks the transition and calculates the exact wavelength of the emitted photon. Lyman (ends at level 1) · ultraviolet Balmer (ends at level 2) · visible Paschen (ends at level 3) · infrared Starting level = 3"},{"t":"The Schrödinger atom: orbitals, not orbits","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#schrodinger","x":"03 The Schrödinger atom: orbitals, not orbits The Bohr model predicts hydrogen well and fails with any atom of more than one electron. Solving the Schrödinger equation in three dimensions for the hydrogen atom gives something different from an orbit: a probability cloud, \\( |\\psi|^2 \\), which describes where the electron is most likely to be found, without it having a definite trajectory. From that solution come, unasked, four quantum numbers. Quantum number What it fixes Possible values Principal, \\( n \\) The energy (approximately, as in Bohr) 1, 2, 3… Orbital, \\( l \\) The shape of the cloud: \\( l=0 \\) spherical (s), \\( l=1 \\) with two lobes (p)… 0 to \\( n-1 \\) Magnetic, \\( m_l \\) The spatial orientation of that shape \\( -l \\) to \\( l \\) Spin, \\( m_s \\) An internal degree of freedom of the electron, with no classical analog \\( +\\tfrac12 \\) or \\( -\\tfrac12 \\) Spin does not come out of the Schrödinger equation —Dirac's relativistic version is needed to derive it— but it has to be added by hand for the model to agree with what is observed: each orbital admits exactly two electrons, one of each spin."},{"t":"The Pauli exclusion principle","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#pauli","x":"04 The Pauli exclusion principle In 1925, Pauli stated the rule that organizes everything above: two electrons in the same atom can never have all four quantum numbers equal. As a consequence, each orbital —each combination of \\( n, l, m_l \\)— admits at most two electrons, and only if they have opposite spin. That single rule builds the entire periodic table: the electrons of an atom settle into shells and subshells, from the lowest energy upward, until the available electrons are used up. Why matter does not collapse Without the Pauli principle, all the electrons in an atom —in any atom, in the whole body— would fall to the lowest-energy state, the 1s orbital. Matter as it exists, with volume and with chemistry, depends on that being forbidden: electrons stack upward in energy because they have no other choice. The same idea, carried over to a crystal, is what in the next unit will explain why a metal has free electrons up to a well-defined energy —the Fermi level— and not a single one above it at zero temperature."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics When many atoms come together to form a crystal, the Pauli principle forces something remarkable: since no electron in the crystal can repeat the state of another, the single atomic level of each isolated atom —say, the 3s of silicon— splits into as many slightly different levels as there are atoms in the crystal. With \\( 10^{22} \\) atoms per cubic centimeter, those levels end up so close together that they form, for practical purposes, a continuous band of allowed energy. Concept from this topic Where it leads Discrete levels of an isolated atom Energy bands of a crystal (next two units) Electrons stacked by Pauli up to a certain energy Fermi level: the boundary between full and empty in a metal or semiconductor Valence shell of the atom (the outermost orbitals) Valence band; the four valence electrons of silicon build its four covalent bonds Empty orbitals of higher energy Conduction band: where an electron has to jump in order to conduct No concept of a semiconductor —band gap, Fermi level, charge carrier— makes sense without this topic behind it. It is the missing link between “an atom has electrons in shells” and “a crystal has electrons in bands”."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Reconstructing the hydrogen spectrum Calculate the first four wavelengths of the Balmer series and compare them with the measured values (Hα 656.3 nm, Hβ 486.1 nm, Hγ 434.0 nm, Hδ 410.2 nm). Identify the color of each one. Activity 2 · The electron configuration Using the rule of filling by increasing energy and the Pauli principle, write the electron configuration of silicon (\\( Z=14 \\)) and germanium (\\( Z=32 \\)). Identify how many valence electrons each one has. Activity 3 · The limit of Bohr Calculate the ionization energy predicted by the Bohr model for singly ionized helium (\\( \\text{He}^+ \\), a single electron, \\( Z=2 \\)) and compare it with the measured value of 54.4 eV."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Thinking that the electron “orbits” like a planet. The Schrödinger model gives a probability cloud, not a trajectory; Bohr's, although useful, has been superseded on that point since 1926. Applying the Bohr model to atoms with more than one electron. It is only exact for one-electron systems: hydrogen and ions such as \\( \\text{He}^+ \\) or \\( \\text{Li}^{2+} \\). Confusing the principal quantum number with the number of electrons. \\( n \\) organizes energy levels; how many electrons there are up to that point is decided by Pauli. Forgetting spin when counting the capacity of an orbital. Each orbital holds two electrons, not one. Believing that Pauli applies only to electrons in the same atom. The general version —for any set of fermions that share a quantum system, such as the electrons of an entire crystal— is the one that builds the energy bands. Thinking that the classical instability of the atom is only a historical curiosity. It is the question that quantum mechanics as a whole had to answer in order to exist as a theory."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why can the classical Rutherford atom not be stable? Because a charge in orbit accelerates constantly and, according to classical electromagnetism, it should radiate energy and spiral into the nucleus within picoseconds. What did Bohr postulate to avoid that collapse? That the electron can only occupy orbits with quantized angular momentum, \\( L = n\\hbar \\), and that in those orbits it does not radiate. What replaces the orbit in the Schrödinger model? A probability cloud, the orbital, described by four quantum numbers: \\( n, l, m_l, m_s \\). What does the Pauli exclusion principle say? That two electrons of the same quantum system can never have all four quantum numbers equal, so each orbital admits at most two electrons with opposite spin. What would happen to matter without the Pauli principle? All the electrons would fall to the lowest-energy state; there would be no shells, no periodic table, and no chemistry as we know it. Why do atomic levels become bands inside a crystal? Because Pauli forbids two electrons in the crystal from sharing the same state: the single level of each isolated atom splits into as many levels as there are atoms, so close together that they form a continuous band."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Atomic models and Schrödinger","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-atomicos#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Robert Eisberg and Robert Resnick. Quantum Physics of Atoms, Molecules, Solids, Nuclei, and Particles. 2nd ed., John Wiley & Sons, 1985 (Spanish edition, “Física cuántica: átomos, moléculas, sólidos, núcleos y partículas”, Limusa). The chapters on the Bohr atom, the Schrödinger solution for hydrogen and the Pauli principle, with the same rigor as the whole unit. Niels Bohr. On the Constitution of Atoms and Molecules. Philosophical Magazine, vol. 26, 1913. The original paper in which he postulates the quantization of angular momentum, with no justification other than “it works”. Wolfgang Pauli. Über den Zusammenhang des Abschlusses der Elektronengruppen im Atom mit der Komplexstruktur der Spektren. Zeitschrift für Physik, vol. 31, 1925. The exclusion principle paper, written before the modern concept of spin existed. Charles Kittel. Introduction to Solid State Physics. 8th ed., Wiley, 2004. The opening chapter explicitly connects atomic levels with the bands of a crystal: the exact bridge to the next two units."},{"t":"Every circuit is, at bottom, a wave problem","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#ondas","x":"01 Every circuit is, at bottom, a wave problem Maxwell’s equations make no distinction between a wire and an antenna: any conductor carrying a varying current radiates and propagates fields at the speed of light. Solving a circuit that way —field by field, point by point in space— is exact but, for the vast majority of the circuits that get designed, a waste of effort. The complete circuit theory that begins in this course is a deliberate approximation: one of the great shortcuts of electrical engineering, and it pays to know exactly what is being approximated. The approximation is called the lumped-element model ( lumped elements ): instead of fields that vary with position and time, the current is assumed to be the same throughout a conductor at every instant, and an entire resistor, capacitor or inductor can be treated as a single point with one relationship between voltage and current. That assumption is false, strictly speaking, for any real component. It is an excellent approximation when the circuit is much smaller than the wavelength of the signals it handles."},{"t":"The lumped-element condition: the \\( \\lambda /10 \\) rule","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#regla","x":"02 The lumped-element condition: the \\( \\lambda /10 \\) rule The practical criterion used throughout engineering: a circuit can be treated as lumped elements if its largest physical dimension is less than one tenth of the wavelength of the signal passing through it. Beyond that, the propagation delay inside the circuit itself is no longer negligible and you need transmission-line theory —part of the courses that follow this one. \\[ \\lambda = \\frac{v_p}{f} \\approx \\frac{c}{f} \\qquad\\qquad \\text{lumped if } \\; d \\ll \\frac{\\lambda}{10} \\] \\( v_p \\) is the propagation velocity in the medium —close to \\( c \\) in air, slower in a printed-circuit dielectric— and \\( d \\) is the largest dimension of the circuit. Signal Frequency \\( \\lambda \\) \\( \\lambda/10 \\) Is a desktop circuit lumped? Household mains 50 Hz 6,000 km 600 km Without any doubt Audio 20 kHz 15 km 1.5 km Without any doubt AM radio 1 MHz 300 m 30 m Yes, with no special care FM / VHF 100 MHz 3 m 30 cm Yes, but it is already worth taking care with the trace layout Wi-Fi 2.4 GHz 2.4 GHz 12.5 cm 1.25 cm Not always: a trace several centimeters long is already a transmission line 60 GHz radar (automotive, 5G mmWave) 60 GHz 5 mm 0.5 mm No: any trace longer than half a millimeter needs transmission-line design The same physical board, with the same layout, can be “a circuit” at 1 MHz and “an accidental antenna” at 2.4 GHz. The model does not change because the designer decides so: it changes because the ratio between the physical "},{"t":"What it means for a circuit to be time-invariant","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#invariancia","x":"03 What it means for a circuit to be time-invariant A system is time-invariant if its behavior does not depend on when it is excited: applying the same input today or tomorrow gives exactly the same output, only shifted in time. Formally, if an input \\( x(t) \\) produces an output \\( y(t) \\), a time-invariant system responds to \\( x(t-t_0) \\) with \\( y(t-t_0) \\), for any shift \\( t_0 \\). Circuit Time-invariant? Why An RC filter with fixed components Yes The values of \\( R \\) and \\( C \\) do not change with time A potentiometer someone is turning No The value of the resistance depends on the instant at which you look at it A mixer with a sampling clock No It multiplies the signal by something that varies periodically in time, on purpose An amplifier that warms up and drifts Approximately yes, in the short term Thermal drift is a change of parameters that is slow compared with the signal being amplified Almost all of this course —and Circuit Theory II, which follows— assumes linear time-invariant (LTI) circuits, because that is the class of systems where the frequency response, the Laplace transform and systematic solution methods work without caveats. A circuit that changes its parameters on purpose —like the mixer— is a tremendously valuable tool, but it belongs to a different kind of analysis."},{"t":"When the ideal model breaks down: self-resonance","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#resonanciapropia","x":"04 When the ideal model breaks down: self-resonance An ideal capacitor is a lumped element by definition: its impedance is \\( 1/j\\omega C \\), which falls with frequency without limit. A real capacitor does not. Its leads and its internal construction add a parasitic series inductance, \\( L_{ESL} \\), and a parasitic series resistance, \\( R_{ESR} \\). The result is a series RLC circuit, not a pure capacitor, with a self-resonant frequency above which the capacitor starts to behave, electrically, like an inductor. \\[ Z(\\omega) = R_{ESR} + j\\left(\\omega L_{ESL} - \\frac{1}{\\omega C}\\right) \\qquad f_0 = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{L_{ESL}\\,C}} \\] Below \\( f_0 \\) the capacitive term dominates and \\( |Z| \\) falls with frequency, as expected. Right at \\( f_0 \\) the two reactive terms cancel and only \\( R_{ESR} \\) remains, the minimum of the impedance. Above \\( f_0 \\), \\( \\omega L_{ESL} \\) dominates: the capacitor conducts worse and worse at high frequency, exactly the opposite of what its symbol promises. Lab · where it stops being a capacitor You choose the capacitance and the package type —each with its typical parasitic inductance— and the plot shows the real impedance against the ideal one, on a logarithmic scale. 100 pF 1 nF 10 nF 100 nF 1 µF 10 µF through-hole (THT) · ESL ≈ 5 nH SMD 0805 · ESL ≈ 1 nH SMD 0402 · ESL ≈ 0.3 nH"},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics This is the real reason, not an anecdote, that computer and microcontroller boards carry several decoupling capacitors in parallel with different values —typically 10 µF, 100 nF and sometimes 1 nF right next to the chip itself—: each value has its own \\( f_0 \\), and together the three cover a much wider frequency range than any one alone. The one with the largest capacitance filters the low-frequency noise; the smallest, with a lower relative \\( L_{ESL} \\), is still capacitive at the highest frequencies, where the other one has already stopped being useful. The same problem, at a different scale The \\( \\lambda /10 \\) rule of section 2 and the self-resonance of this section are the same idea applied at two different scales. One says when the whole circuit stops being “a point” compared with the signal; the other, when a single component stops behaving like the ideal element that its symbol says it is. Both are the same question: is the lumped-element approximation still valid here?"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Checking the \\( \\lambda /10 \\) rule Calculate the wavelength of a 433 MHz signal (the remote-control band) and determine whether a board 8 cm on a side can be treated as lumped elements at that frequency. Activity 2 · Choosing the right decoupling capacitor Using this page’s lab, for a microcontroller that switches at 48 MHz, determine which combination of capacitance and package keeps the capacitor in its capacitive region at that frequency and at its third harmonic. Activity 3 · A time-varying system Explain why a circuit with a relay that opens and closes during the analysis cannot be treated as time-invariant, and which tools of this course stop applying while the relay is switching."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Thinking a circuit “is” or “is not” lumped based on its physical size alone. It depends on the ratio between the size and the wavelength of the signal, not on the size in isolation. Assuming a capacitor conducts better and better at higher frequency, without limit. Only up to its self-resonance; above it, it behaves like an inductor. Choosing the decoupling capacitor by its capacitance alone, ignoring the package. The package determines the parasitic inductance, which determines up to what frequency it remains useful. Confusing time invariance with the circuit “never changing.” It means that its parameters do not change during the interval of interest, nor as a function of the signal being analyzed. Applying LTI circuit tools —phasors, Laplace— to a circuit with an element that varies in time on purpose. A mixer or a circuit with a sampling clock needs a different treatment."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What does the lumped-element model approximate? It ignores the propagation of electromagnetic fields inside the circuit itself, treating the current as uniform throughout a conductor at every instant. What is the practical rule for knowing whether a circuit is lumped? That its largest physical dimension be much smaller than one tenth of the wavelength of the signal passing through it. What does it mean for a system to be time-invariant? That its response to an input does not depend on when that input is applied: the same input, shifted in time, gives the same output shifted by the same amount. Why does a real capacitor stop behaving like a capacitor at high frequency? Because its parasitic series inductance dominates above its self-resonant frequency, and from there the impedance starts to grow with frequency instead of falling. What determines the self-resonant frequency of a real capacitor? Its capacitance \\( C \\) and its parasitic series inductance \\( L_{ESL} \\), according to \\( f_0 = 1/(2\\pi\\sqrt{L_{ESL}C}) \\). Why does a board usually carry several decoupling capacitors of different values? Because each value has its own self-resonant frequency, and combining them covers a wider frequency range than any one alone."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Lumped elements and time invariance","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modelos-concentrados#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 7th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2021. The reference book for this whole course: the introductory chapter develops the lumped-element model with the same approach as this page. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. The formal treatment of when circuit theory stops being enough and transmission-line theory is needed, on the other side of the \\( \\lambda /10 \\) rule. Henry W. Ott. Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering. Wiley, 2009. The chapter on power-supply decoupling explains in detail, with manufacturer data, why capacitors of different value and package are combined."},{"t":"Bits, symbols and pulses","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#simbolos","x":"01 Bits, symbols and pulses A digital system groups bits \\( k \\) at a time and transmits one of \\( M = 2^k \\) possible symbols. Each symbol is a pulse of known shape whose amplitude, phase or frequency depends on the bits. Two rates must be distinguished: \\[ R_s = \\frac{R_b}{\\log_2 M} \\] \\( R_b \\) is the bit rate, in bit/\\( s \\), and \\( R_s \\) the symbol rate, in bauds. With 16 symbols each one carries 4 bits, and 100 Mbit/\\( s \\) is transmitted at 25 Mbaud. The bandwidth depends on \\( R_s \\), not on \\( R_b \\). The introduction to the modulations with the equipment is in digital modulation, in the technician program. Here we work with the models: the pulse shape, the bandwidth and the complex envelope of each modulation."},{"t":"Intersymbol interference and the Nyquist criterion","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#nyquist","x":"02 Intersymbol interference and the Nyquist criterion A band-limited pulse lasts, in theory, an infinite time, and overlaps with its neighbors. This intersymbol interference (ISI) does no harm if, at the sampling instant of each symbol, the other pulses pass through zero. This is the Nyquist criterion: the pulse \\( p(t) \\) must equal 1 at \\( t = 0 \\) and 0 at all multiples of \\( T = 1/R_s \\). The narrowest pulse in frequency that meets it is the sinc, which occupies \\( R_s/2 \\); in practice the raised cosine is used, which falls off faster in time in exchange for an excess bandwidth \\( \\alpha \\): \\[ p(t) = \\frac{\\sin(\\pi t/T)}{\\pi t/T} \\cdot \\frac{\\cos(\\pi \\alpha t/T)}{1 - (2\\alpha t/T)^2} \\] \\[ B = \\frac{(1 + \\alpha)\\, R_s}{2} \\] \\( \\alpha \\) ranges from 0 (the sinc) to 1. With \\( \\alpha = 0.35 \\), usual in satellite links, a 10 Mbaud channel in baseband occupies 6.75 MHz. Normally the raised cosine is split between the transmitter and the receiver, with a root-raised-cosine filter at each end. The eye diagram is the tool for seeing ISI: on the oscilloscope, segments of the signal two symbol periods long are superimposed, triggering on the clock. The vertical opening of the eye is the margin against noise; the horizontal one, against errors in the sampling instant. Lab · the eye diagram 240 random binary symbols (\\( \\pm 1 \\)) with raised-cosine pulses, passed through a first-order lowpass channel. The yellow line is the sampling instant, which can be shifted from the optimum"},{"t":"Bandpass modulations","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#pasabanda","x":"03 Bandpass modulations To transmit by radio, the pulse modulates a carrier. Written as a complex envelope, each modulation is a set of points in the I/Q plane, the constellation, and the signal jumps from point to point at the symbol rate: Modulation Bits per symbol Constellation Typical use ASK / OOK 1 Two points on the I axis, one at the origin Remote controls, short-range optical fiber FSK 1 or more Different frequencies: not a flat constellation Low-speed links, Bluetooth (GFSK) BPSK 1 Two opposite points Very noisy links, GPS QPSK 2 Four points on a circle Satellite, cellular telephony M-QAM \\( \\log_2 M \\) A grid of \\( \\sqrt{M} \\times \\sqrt{M} \\) Wi-Fi, cable modem, microwave A bandpass modulation with raised-cosine pulses occupies twice as much as in baseband, \\( (1 + \\alpha) R_s \\), because the spectrum is split to both sides of the carrier. Bits are assigned to the points with a Gray code: neighboring points differ in a single bit, and the most probable error, mistaking a point for its neighbor, costs a single bit."},{"t":"Spectral efficiency","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#eficiencia","x":"04 Spectral efficiency \\[ \\eta_B = \\frac{R_b}{B} = \\frac{\\log_2 M}{1 + \\alpha} \\ \\ \\mathrm{bit/s/Hz} \\] With \\( \\alpha = 0.25 \\), QPSK gives 1.6 bit/\\( s \\)/Hz and 256-QAM, 6.4 bit/\\( s \\)/Hz. Modulations with many points make better use of the spectrum, but the points end up closer together and need more signal-to-noise ratio for the same error rate. That is why modern systems adapt the modulation to the state of the channel: a phone near the antenna uses 256-QAM and far away drops to QPSK. The relationship among error rate, power and number of points is the subject of the unit on noise in digital modulations. M-PSK and FSK constellations, with constant or nearly constant envelope, are preferred when the power amplifier has to work close to saturation."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Eye on a cable Transmit a pseudorandom sequence over a long cable and observe the eye diagram on the oscilloscope, triggering on the data clock. Increase the rate until the eye closes. Activity 2 · Constellation With a vector signal generator and an analyzer, observe the QPSK and 16-QAM constellations and the error vector magnitude (EVM). Activity 3 · Spectrum and \\( \\alpha \\) Measure the occupied bandwidth of a QPSK signal for several values of \\( \\alpha \\) and compare it with \\( (1 + \\alpha)R_s \\)."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Confusing bauds with bit/s. They coincide only in binary modulations. Believing that the sinc is the best choice. It occupies the minimum band, but its eye closes with the slightest timing error. Forgetting that the channel adds ISI. Even if the pulse meets the Nyquist criterion, a band-limited channel deforms it, and an equalizer is needed. Assigning bits to the points in natural binary order. Without a Gray code, an error between neighbors can cost several bits."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment At how many bauds are 60 Mbit/s transmitted with 64-QAM? At 10 Mbaud: each symbol carries 6 bits. What bandwidth does a 20 Mbit/\\( s \\) QPSK with \\( \\alpha = 0.2 \\) occupy? \\( R_s \\) = 10 Mbaud and \\( B = 1.2 \\cdot 10 = 12 \\) MHz. What does the vertical opening of the eye measure? The amplitude margin between the symbols at the sampling instant: how much noise can be added before a mistake is made. Why does a larger \\( \\alpha \\) tolerate timing errors better? Because the tails of the pulse decay faster and the ISI grows more slowly as one moves away from the optimum instant."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Digital modulation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-digital-ing#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading John G. Proakis and Masoud Salehi. Digital Communications. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2008. The reference on digital modulations, the Nyquist criterion and equalization. Bernard Sklar. Digital Communications: Fundamentals and Applications. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2001. ISI, the raised cosine and bandpass modulations with numerical examples. B. P. Lathi and Zhi Ding. Modern Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 5th ed., Oxford University Press, 2019. Baseband and bandpass digital transmission in a single framework."},{"t":"Modulating the angle","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#angulo","x":"01 Modulating the angle In exponential or angle modulations, the message changes the phase or the frequency of the carrier and the amplitude stays constant. In phase modulation (PM), the phase follows the message; in frequency modulation (FM), the instantaneous frequency does: \\[ x_{PM}(t) = A_c \\cos\\big(2\\pi f_c t + k_p\\, m(t)\\big) \\] \\[ x_{FM}(t) = A_c \\cos\\Big(2\\pi f_c t + 2\\pi k_f \\int_0^t m(\\tau)\\, d\\tau\\Big) \\] In FM the instantaneous frequency is \\( f_c + k_f m(t) \\), and its maximum excursion is the frequency deviation \\( \\Delta f \\). PM and FM are the same family: FM is PM of the integrated message. Since the envelope is constant (as seen in complex envelope ), FM transmitters use saturated amplifiers, and receivers clip the amplitude before demodulating, eliminating a good part of the noise and interference that affect the amplitude."},{"t":"The FM spectrum and Bessel functions","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#espectro","x":"02 The FM spectrum and Bessel functions Unlike AM, FM is not linear: its spectrum is not that of the shifted message. With a tone of frequency \\( f_m \\), the modulation index is \\( \\beta = \\Delta f / f_m \\), and the signal has infinitely many lines spaced \\( f_m \\) apart around the carrier, with amplitudes given by the Bessel functions: \\[ x(t) = A_c \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} J_n(\\beta)\\cos\\big(2\\pi (f_c + n f_m) t\\big) \\] The total power does not change with modulation: \\( \\sum J_n^2(\\beta) = 1 \\). Modulation only distributes it between the carrier and the sidebands; with \\( \\beta = 2.405 \\) the carrier disappears. The lines become negligible shortly after \\( n = \\beta + 1 \\), and from this comes Carson's rule for the bandwidth, which contains about 98 % of the power: \\[ B \\approx 2(\\Delta f + W) = 2(\\beta + 1)\\, W \\] \\( W \\) is the maximum frequency of the message. A commercial FM station, with \\( \\Delta f = 75\\text{ kHz} \\) and audio up to 15 kHz \\( (\\beta = 5) \\), occupies about 180 kHz: that is why channels are spaced 200 kHz apart. Lab · the FM spectrum A carrier frequency-modulated by a 15 kHz tone. The lines are the amplitudes \\( |J_n(\\beta)| \\) of the carrier and the sidebands; the two yellow dashed lines, the Carson bandwidth; the gray strip, the bandwidth that contains 98 % of the power. Modulation index β ="},{"t":"Narrowband and wideband","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#banda","x":"03 Narrowband and wideband With \\( \\beta \\) much less than 1 ( narrowband FM ), only the carrier and the first pair of lines are appreciable, and the bandwidth is \\( 2W \\), as in AM: it is used in two-way radios, where spectrum is scarce. With large \\( \\beta \\) ( wideband ), the bandwidth grows almost in proportion to the deviation. In return, as seen in the unit on noise in analog modulations, the output signal-to-noise ratio improves with the square of \\( \\beta \\): wideband FM trades bandwidth for quality."},{"t":"Generation and demodulation","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#circuitos","x":"04 Generation and demodulation Function Methods Direct generation A voltage-controlled oscillator (VCO), stabilized with a PLL Indirect generation (Armstrong) Narrowband PM of the integrated message, and frequency multiplication Slope demodulation A resonant circuit converts frequency into amplitude, followed by an envelope detector Quadrature discriminator Compares the signal with a copy phase-shifted by an amount that depends on frequency PLL The control voltage of the locked VCO follows the frequency of the signal: it is the message"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Nulling the carrier Frequency-modulate a generator with a 1 kHz tone and increase the deviation until the carrier disappears on the spectrum analyzer. Verify that it happens with \\( \\Delta f = 2.405\\text{ kHz} \\): it is the classic method for calibrating the deviation. Activity 2 · Carson Measure the bandwidth that contains 98 % of the power for several indices and compare it with Carson's rule. Activity 3 · Demodulating with a PLL Demodulate an FM signal with an integrated PLL and observe the VCO control voltage."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Calculating the FM bandwidth as \\( 2\\Delta f \\). The message term is missing: it is \\( 2(\\Delta f + W) \\). Believing that modulation adds power. In FM the total power is constant. Confusing frequency deviation with index. The index is the deviation divided by the message frequency. Forgetting pre-emphasis. FM stations boost the high frequencies before transmitting and the receiver attenuates them, to reduce noise."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What modulation index does an FM signal with \\( \\Delta f = 5\\text{ kHz} \\) and a 1 kHz tone have? \\( \\beta = 5 \\). What Carson bandwidth does a two-way radio with \\( \\Delta f = 2.5\\text{ kHz} \\) and audio up to 3 kHz occupy? \\( 2(2.5 + 3) = 11 \\) kHz. At what index does the carrier disappear? At \\( \\beta = 2.405 \\), the first zero of \\( J_0 \\). What fraction of the power remains in the carrier with \\( \\beta = 1 \\)? \\( J_0^2(1) = 0.765^2 \\approx 0.59 \\): 59 %."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Exponential modulation: FM and PM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-exponencial#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading B. P. Lathi and Zhi Ding. Modern Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 5th ed., Oxford University Press, 2019. Angle modulations, Bessel functions and Carson's rule. Simon Haykin and Michael Moher. Communication Systems. 5th ed., Wiley, 2009. FM with its demodulators and the PLL. Wayne Tomasi. Electronic Communications Systems: Fundamentals Through Advanced. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Sistemas de comunicaciones electrónicas”), Pearson, 2003. FM transmitters and receivers from the circuits side."},{"t":"Shifting the spectrum","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#lineal","x":"01 Shifting the spectrum In linear modulations, the complex envelope is a linear function of the message. Its effect in frequency is simple: the spectrum of the message is shifted around the carrier, without changing shape. The variants differ in whether they transmit the carrier and in how many sidebands they send. The introduction to the equipment is in analog communications, in the technician program."},{"t":"Double-sideband AM with carrier","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#am","x":"02 Double-sideband AM with carrier \\[ x(t) = A_c\\,[1 + m\\, s(t)]\\cos 2\\pi f_c t \\] \\[ \\eta = \\frac{m^2 \\langle s^2 \\rangle}{1 + m^2 \\langle s^2 \\rangle} \\] \\( s(t) \\) is the message normalized to \\( |s| \\le 1 \\) and \\( m \\) the modulation index. The efficiency \\( \\eta \\) is the fraction of the power that goes into the sidebands, which are the ones that carry the message. With a tone and \\( m = 1 \\), \\( \\langle s^2 \\rangle = 1/2 \\) and \\( \\eta = 33\\,\\% \\): two thirds of the power is spent on the carrier. The advantage of AM is the receiver: as long as \\( m \\le 1 \\), the envelope reproduces the message and a diode with an RC filter recovers it. With \\( m > 1 \\) there is overmodulation: the envelope folds over the axis and the detector distorts. It occupies a bandwidth of twice the maximum frequency of the message."},{"t":"DSB and SSB","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#dsb-ssb","x":"03 DSB and SSB Modulation Bandwidth Useful power Receiver AM \\( 2W \\) At most 33 % with a tone Envelope detector DSB-SC (suppressed carrier) \\( 2W \\) 100 % Coherent: needs the exact carrier SSB (single sideband) \\( W \\) 100 % Coherent, tolerates small frequency errors VSB (vestigial sideband) Somewhat more than \\( W \\) Almost all Coherent; used in analog television DSB-SC spends no power on the carrier, but the receiver has to regenerate it with the exact frequency and phase: a phase error \\( \\theta \\) multiplies the output by \\( \\cos\\theta \\). SSB sends only one of the two sidebands, which carry the same information, and occupies half the spectrum: it is the voice modulation of amateur radio operators and of shortwave communications. Lab · AM, DSB and SSB with a tone A carrier modulated by a tone. Above, the signal and its envelope; below, its spectrum around the carrier, with the power of each line relative to the unmodulated carrier. AM DSB-SC SSB (upper sideband) Modulation index m ="},{"t":"How they are generated and received","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#generacion","x":"04 How they are generated and received AM is generated by modulating the supply voltage of a class C amplifier, or with a multiplier. DSB-SC comes from a balanced modulator, such as the MC1496 integrated circuit analyzed in a publication on this site. SSB is obtained by filtering out one of the sidebands of a DSB signal with a very selective filter, or by the phase method, which uses the Hilbert transform of the message. In reception, the superheterodyne shifts all the stations to a fixed intermediate frequency, where filtering is easier."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · The modulation index Modulate a generator in AM, measure the maximum and minimum envelopes on the oscilloscope and calculate m. Verify the sidebands on the spectrum analyzer. Activity 2 · Overmodulation Raise the index above 1 and observe the output of a diode envelope detector. Activity 3 · Balanced modulator Build a balanced modulator with an MC1496 and adjust the carrier balance until the carrier is suppressed."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Overmodulating an AM station. It distorts the audio and widens the spectrum. Demodulating DSB with an envelope detector. What you get is the absolute value of the message. Forgetting the phase error in coherent demodulation. A 90° phase shift cancels the output. Believing that the AM carrier carries information. It only makes reception easier."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What efficiency does AM have with \\( m = 0.5 \\) and a tone? \\( 0.125/1.125 \\approx 11 \\) %. What bandwidth does an AM station occupy with audio up to 5 kHz? And in SSB? 10 kHz in AM; 5 kHz in SSB. If the AM carrier is 100 W and \\( m = 1 \\), how much power is in each sideband? 25 W in each. What happens if DSB is demodulated with a phase error of 60°? The output is multiplied by \\( \\cos 60° = 0.5 \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Linear modulation: AM, DSB and SSB","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-lineal#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading B. P. Lathi and Zhi Ding. Modern Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 5th ed., Oxford University Press, 2019. Amplitude modulations with their spectra, generators and demodulators. Wayne Tomasi. Electronic Communications Systems: Fundamentals Through Advanced. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Sistemas de comunicaciones electrónicas”), Pearson, 2003. The circuits approach: AM and SSB modulators, transmitters and receivers. Herbert Taub and Donald L. Schilling. Principles of Communication Systems. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1986. A classic with a very clear treatment of AM, DSB, SSB and VSB."},{"t":"Modulating a pulse train","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#pulsos","x":"01 Modulating a pulse train If the message is sampled at a frequency greater than twice its bandwidth, it is enough to transmit the samples. The carrier becomes a pulse train, and the message can change its amplitude, its duration or its position: Modulation What the sample carries Use PAM (amplitude) The height of the pulse Intermediate step in every converter; basis of PCM PWM (width) The width of the pulse Motor control, class D amplifiers, switching power supplies PPM (position) The delay of the pulse relative to a clock Radio control, low-power optical communications PCM (code) A binary number with the quantized value Digital telephony, audio, almost all present-day transmission PAM, PWM and PPM remain analog: the noise added to the height, width or position of the pulse reaches the message. In PCM, on the other hand, each sample is rounded to one of \\( 2^n \\) levels and transmitted as \\( n \\) bits. The receiver only has to decide whether a 1 or a 0 arrived, and a repeater can regenerate the signal without accumulating noise. The introduction to the equipment is in sampling theorem, in the technician program."},{"t":"PCM: bit rate and quantization noise","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#pcm","x":"02 PCM: bit rate and quantization noise A PCM system samples at \\( f_s \\), quantizes with \\( n \\) bits and transmits the bit sequence. The bit rate and the minimum bandwidth, with Nyquist pulses, are: \\[ R_b = n\\, f_s \\] \\[ B_{\\min} = \\frac{R_b}{2} \\] Telephony samples voice at 8 kHz with 8 bits: 64 kbit/s per channel. CD audio, at 44.1 kHz with 16 bits and two channels, needs 1.41 Mbit/s. Rounding adds an error that, with a uniform quantizer of step \\( \\Delta \\), is spread between \\( \\pm\\Delta/2 \\) and has power \\( \\Delta^2/12 \\). With a sine wave that fills the whole range, the quantization signal-to-noise ratio is: \\[ \\mathrm{SNR_q} = 6.02\\, n + 1.76 \\ \\mathrm{dB} \\] Each bit adds about 6 dB. The formula holds only at full scale: with a signal 20 dB smaller, the quantization error is the same and the ratio drops by 20 dB. The details of the quantizer and the converter are in data converters."},{"t":"Companding: \\( \\mu \\)-law and A-law","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#compansion","x":"03 Companding: \\( \\mu \\)-law and A-law Voice has a dynamic range of about 40 dB: a person speaking softly produces signals a hundred times smaller than one who shouts. With a uniform quantizer, weak signals end up with very few levels. The solution is to compress the signal before quantizing, with a logarithmic curve that expands small values, and to expand it at the receiver. The G.711 standard uses \\( \\mu \\)-law in North America and Japan: \\[ c(x) = \\operatorname{sgn}(x)\\, \\frac{\\ln(1 + \\mu |x|)}{\\ln(1 + \\mu)} \\] The input is normalized to \\( |x| \\le 1 \\) and the standard uses \\( \\mu = 255 \\). In Europe and Argentina, A-law is used, with \\( A = 87.6 \\), which is very similar. In both cases the quantization step grows with amplitude and the error is approximately proportional to the signal. The result is a signal-to-noise ratio that is nearly constant over a wide range of levels, in exchange for losing about 12 dB at full scale. With 8 bits, \\( \\mu \\)-law gives a −40 dB signal the same quality as a 12-bit uniform quantizer. Lab · uniform PCM and \\( \\mu \\)-law A sine wave quantized with \\( n \\) bits, directly or companded with \\( \\mu \\)-law. The blue curve is the signal-to-noise ratio of the uniform quantizer as a function of the signal level; the green one, that of \\( \\mu \\)-law. The dot marks the chosen level. The values come from quantizing 4096 samples. Bits per sample n = Signal level ="},{"t":"Time-division multiplexing","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#tdm","x":"04 Time-division multiplexing Between two samples of one channel there is free time, which is used to transmit the samples of other channels: this is time-division multiplexing (TDM). A frame contains one sample of each channel and repeats at the sampling frequency. The European hierarchy, which Argentina uses, begins with the E1 system: \\[ R_{E1} = 32 \\times 8\\ \\mathrm{bit} \\times 8\\ \\mathrm{kHz} = 2.048\\ \\mathrm{Mbit/s} \\] Of the 32 time slots, 30 carry voice; one carries frame synchronization and another signaling. The North American T1 system groups 24 channels with one framing bit: 1.544 Mbit/s. Other variants reduce the bit rate at the cost of complexity: differential PCM (DPCM) quantizes the difference between the sample and a prediction, and delta modulation uses a single bit per sample, at a much higher rate. Present-day speech coders transmit parameters of a vocal-tract model and get down to a few kbit/s."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Quantization noise Digitize a sine wave with a microcontroller, reducing the number of bits in software, and measure the signal-to-noise ratio with the FFT for each resolution. Verify the 6 dB per bit. Activity 2 · Companding Implement \\( \\mu \\)-law on the microcontroller and repeat the measurement for different input levels. Compare with the uniform quantizer of the same number of bits. Activity 3 · PWM Generate a PWM modulated by a tone and recover it with an RC lowpass filter. Observe the residue of the switching frequency."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Applying \\( 6.02n + 1.76\\text{ dB} \\) to any signal. It holds for a full-scale sine wave; with smaller signals the ratio drops dB for dB. Forgetting the anti-aliasing filter. Whatever is above \\( f_s/2 \\) folds over the useful band and cannot be separated. Believing that companding improves everything. It evens out the quality across levels, but at full scale it is worse than uniform. Confusing bit rate with bandwidth. The bandwidth depends on the pulse shape: at minimum, half the bit rate."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What quantization signal-to-noise ratio does a 12-bit converter give with a full-scale sine wave? \\( 6.02 \\cdot 12 + 1.76 \\approx 74 \\) dB. What bit rate does an audio channel sampled at 48 kHz with 24 bits need? 1.152 Mbit/s. How many voice channels does an E1 carry and what is its bit rate? 30 voice channels at 2.048 Mbit/s. With an 8-bit uniform quantizer, what signal-to-noise ratio does a sine wave 30 dB below full scale have? About \\( 50 - 30 = 20\\text{ dB} \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Pulse modulation and PCM","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/modulacion-pulsos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading B. P. Lathi and Zhi Ding. Modern Digital and Analog Communication Systems. 5th ed., Oxford University Press, 2019. Sampling, PCM, companding, DPCM and delta modulation. Simon Haykin. Digital Communication Systems. Wiley, 2014. Quantization and waveform coding with a model-based approach. Bernard Sklar. Digital Communications: Fundamentals and Applications. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2001. PCM and multiplexing from the system point of view."},{"t":"The I/Q modulator","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#iq","x":"01 The I/Q modulator Any modulation can be written as a complex envelope \\( I(t) + jQ(t) \\), and every modern modulator generates it in the same way: two mixers fed with the local oscillator in phase and in quadrature, and a summer: \\[ s(t) = I(t)\\cos\\omega_c t - Q(t)\\sin\\omega_c t \\] With I and Q generated by a processor and two converters, the same hardware produces AM, FM, SSB, QPSK or QAM: only the software changes. It is the basis of the software-defined radio of the last unit. The I/Q demodulator does the inverse operation: it multiplies the received signal by the cosine and the sine of the local oscillator and filters, and recovers I and Q. For that, the phase of the oscillator has to match that of the received carrier: it is synchronized with a Costas loop, a PLL that uses the product of I and Q as a phase detector, or it is corrected digitally after sampling."},{"t":"Classic modulators and demodulators","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#clasicos","x":"02 Classic modulators and demodulators Modulation Modulator Demodulator AM Variation of the final amplifier's supply Diode envelope detector DSB and SSB Balanced modulator; SSB by filter or by the phasing method Product detector with local oscillator FM Modulated VCO, or phase modulator with the integrated message Quadrature discriminator, PLL Digital I/Q modulator I/Q demodulator with carrier and clock recovery The fundamentals of each modulation were covered in linear modulation, exponential and digital. The phasing method for SSB is the I/Q modulator itself, with Q equal to the Hilbert transform of the message."},{"t":"Imperfections: imbalance and leakage","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#desbalance","x":"03 Imperfections: imbalance and leakage A real I/Q modulator has the two branches slightly different: a gain difference \\( \\varepsilon \\) and a quadrature error \\( \\varphi \\). With a tone, instead of a single sideband the opposite one also appears, the image, with a rejection of: \\[ IRR = \\frac{1 + 2\\varepsilon\\cos\\varphi + \\varepsilon^2}{1 - 2\\varepsilon\\cos\\varphi + \\varepsilon^2} \\] \\( \\varepsilon \\) is the gain ratio of the branches (1 if they are equal). With 0.1 dB of imbalance and 1° of error, the rejection is about 40 dB; uncalibrated analog modulators are around 30 to 40 dB. A DC voltage on I or Q also produces carrier leakage, a line at the center of the spectrum. Lab · imbalance of an I/Q modulator On the left, the spectrum of an I/Q modulator generating a tone at \\( f_c + f_m \\): the image appears at \\( f_c - f_m \\). On the right, the constellation of a QPSK with the same imbalance: the ideal points in gray and the transmitted ones in color. Gain imbalance = Quadrature error = Current transmitters and receivers measure these errors with a test signal and correct them digitally, predistorting I and Q, and reach rejections of more than 60 dB."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Single sideband Generate a single-sideband tone with an integrated I/Q modulator and measure with the spectrum analyzer the rejection of the opposite sideband and the carrier leakage. Activity 2 · Correcting Adjust by software the gain and phase of one of the branches until the image is minimized, and a DC offset to minimize the leakage. Activity 3 · Costas loop Simulate in Python a Costas loop that recovers the carrier of a BPSK with a frequency offset."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Assuming that the two branches are equal. An error of a few degrees limits the image rejection to 30 dB. Forgetting the DC offset of the converters. It shows up as carrier leakage. Demodulating without recovering the phase. The constellation rotates and the symbols get confused. Confusing the modulator's image with the receiver mixer's image. They have the same origin, but appear in different places."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What image rejection does a modulator with no gain imbalance and 1° of quadrature error give? About 41 dB. What signal has to be put on Q to generate SSB with the phasing method? The Hilbert transform of the message. What does a Costas loop do? It recovers the carrier phase from the modulated signal, using the product of I and Q as a phase detector. What does a DC offset on the I branch produce? Carrier leakage: a line at f c."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Modulators and demodulators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/moduladores-demoduladores#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Behzad Razavi. RF Microelectronics. 2nd ed., Prentice Hall, 2011. Quadrature modulators and demodulators and the effect of their imperfections. Qizheng Gu. RF System Design of Transceivers for Wireless Communications. Springer, 2005. System design of transmitters and receivers, with the imbalance budgets. Wayne Tomasi. Electronic Communications Systems: Fundamentals Through Advanced. 4th ed., Prentice Hall, 2001 (Spanish edition: “Sistemas de comunicaciones electrónicas”, 4th ed., Pearson, 2003). Analog modulators and demodulators from the circuits up."},{"t":"The MOS capacitor and inversion","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#capacitor","x":"01 The MOS capacitor and inversion The MOS transistor is a capacitor with a semiconductor as one of its plates: a conducting gate, a very thin oxide and a P-type silicon substrate. A positive voltage on the gate first pushes holes away from the surface ( depletion ) and, if it keeps rising, attracts so many electrons that the surface of the P-type silicon starts to behave as N-type: this is inversion. That layer of electrons, only a few nanometers thick, is the channel that joins the N⁺ source and drain regions. The gate voltage at which inversion appears is the threshold voltage: \\[ V_{th} = V_{FB} + 2\\phi_F + \\frac{\\sqrt{2\\, \\varepsilon_s\\, q\\, N_A\\, (2\\phi_F)}}{C_{ox}} \\] \\[ C_{ox} = \\frac{\\varepsilon_{ox}}{t_{ox}} \\qquad \\phi_F = V_T \\ln \\frac{N_A}{n_i} \\] \\( V_{FB} \\) is the flat-band voltage, which depends on the gate material and on the oxide charges. It is written \\( V_{th} \\) so as not to confuse it with the thermal voltage \\( V_T \\). With a 2 nm oxide, \\( C_{ox} = 17.3 \\) fF/µm²: almost the whole history of the MOS is that of making this capacitor larger and larger per unit area, because the channel charge, and with it the current, is proportional to \\( C_{ox} \\)."},{"t":"The long-channel model","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#canal-largo","x":"02 The long-channel model With an overdrive voltage \\( V_{ov} = V_{GS} - V_{th} \\), the channel charge per unit area is \\( C_{ox} V_{ov} \\), and the electrons drift from source to drain with the channel mobility. Integrating along the channel gives the quadratic model: \\[ I_D = \\mu_n C_{ox} \\frac{W}{L} \\left( V_{ov} V_{DS} - \\frac{V_{DS}^2}{2} \\right) \\qquad V_{DS} In saturation the channel pinches off next to the drain; \\( \\lambda \\) models how the pinch-off point moves toward the source as \\( V_{DS} \\) rises. For small signals, \\( g_m = \\sqrt{2\\, \\mu_n C_{ox} (W/L)\\, I_D} = 2 I_D / V_{ov} \\). Compared with the bipolar, \\( g_m / I_D = 2/V_{ov} \\) versus \\( 1/V_T \\): with \\( V_{ov} = 0.2 \\) V the MOS gives 10 S/A and the bipolar 38.7. For the same current, the MOS amplifies less. Below threshold it is not zero With \\( V_{GS} subthreshold slope \\( S = n V_T \\ln 10 \\) cannot go below 60 mV per decade at room temperature. For a transistor that is off to conduct a million times less than when it is on, a threshold of at least 360 mV is needed, and that number is the reason that the supply voltage of processors stopped falling near 0.7 V."},{"t":"Short channel","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#canal-corto","x":"03 Short channel When the channel length drops to hundreds of nanometers, the quadratic model goes wrong for several reasons at once: Effect What happens Velocity saturation The field along the channel exceeds \\( 10^4 \\) V/cm and the electrons stop accelerating: the current starts to grow with \\( V_{ov} \\) instead of \\( V_{ov}^2 \\), and no longer improves as \\( 1/L \\) Drain-induced barrier lowering (DIBL) The drain, very close to the source, lowers the channel barrier: the threshold falls as \\( V_{DS} \\) rises and the off-state current goes up Threshold roll-off with \\( L \\) Part of the depletion charge under the gate is supported by the source and drain, and the gate has less control Hot carriers High-energy electrons become embedded in the oxide and the transistor degrades with use Gate tunneling leakage With oxides thinner than 2 nm electrons tunnel through it; SiO₂ was replaced by hafnium oxide, with a higher dielectric constant, which gives the same \\( C_{ox} \\) with more thickness Velocity saturation is incorporated into the model with a critical field \\( E_c = 2 v_{sat} / \\mu_n \\): the saturation current becomes \\[ I_{D,sat} = \\frac{W\\, C_{ox}\\, v_{sat}\\, V_{ov}^2}{V_{ov} + E_c L} \\] \\[ V_{DS,sat} = \\frac{V_{ov}\\, E_c L}{V_{ov} + E_c L} \\] If \\( E_c L \\gg V_{ov} \\) the quadratic model is recovered; if \\( E_c L \\ll V_{ov} \\), the current is \\( W C_{ox} v_{sat} V_{ov} \\) and no longer depends on \\( L \\). Lab · the same transistor, shorter and shorter An NMOS with a 2 nm "},{"t":"Dennard scaling and its end","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#escalado","x":"04 Dennard scaling and its end In 1974 Robert Dennard and his colleagues at IBM showed that if all the dimensions and all the voltages of a MOS transistor are divided by the same factor \\( \\kappa \\), and the doping levels are multiplied by it, the transistor works the same and improves in every respect: Quantity Scales as Dimensions \\( L, W, t_{ox} \\) and voltages \\( 1/\\kappa \\) Doping \\( \\kappa \\) Current per transistor \\( 1/\\kappa \\) Gate delay \\( 1/\\kappa \\) Power per transistor \\( 1/\\kappa^2 \\) Transistors per area \\( \\kappa^2 \\) Power per area Constant That last row is what sustained Moore's law for thirty years: chips with twice as many transistors, faster, that gave off the same heat. It broke down around 2005, when the threshold voltage could no longer keep falling because of the 60 mV per decade limit and with it the supply voltage stalled. Since then the power per area grows with each generation, clock frequencies have stayed near 3 to 5 GHz, and the performance gains have come from adding more cores, not from making them faster."},{"t":"The technologies that followed","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#tecnologias","x":"05 The technologies that followed Technology Introduction What it solves Strained silicon Intel, 90 nm, 2003 Deforms the channel lattice to raise the mobility High-k dielectric and metal gate Intel, 45 nm, 2007 More \\( C_{ox} \\) without tunneling leakage FinFET (tri-gate) Intel, 22 nm, 2011 The channel is a fin surrounded by the gate on three sides: better control, less DIBL Gate-all-around (nanosheets) Samsung, 3 nm, 2022 The gate completely surrounds the channel Node names (“7 nm”, “3 nm”) stopped being a physical measurement more than a decade ago: they are commercial labels for the generation, and the actual gate length is considerably larger."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#electronica","x":"06 Where it appears in electronics Case What matters about the MOS Processors and memories Density, current per micron and off-state current Integrated analog circuits \\( g_m/I_D \\), intrinsic gain and matching; channels longer than the minimum are used Power MOSFETs Vertical structures with on-resistance of milliohms CMOS image sensors One photodiode and four transistors per pixel Electrostatic discharge protection The nanometer-thick oxide is punctured by a few volts: that is why pins have protection diodes"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Threshold and transconductance parameter With a small-signal MOSFET (2N7000 or BS170), measure \\( I_D \\) in saturation for five values of \\( V_{GS} \\). Plot \\( \\sqrt{I_D} \\) against \\( V_{GS} \\): the line crosses the axis at \\( V_{th} \\) and its slope gives the transconductance parameter. Activity 2 · The subthreshold region With the same transistor, measure currents between 10 nA and 10 µA with \\( V_{GS} \\) below the threshold and obtain the slope in mV per decade. Compare it with the 60 mV limit. Activity 3 · Simulating the short channel In a simulator with BSIM models of a 180 nm technology, plot \\( I_D \\) against \\( V_{GS} \\) in saturation for \\( L \\) of 180 nm and 2 µm. Verify which one follows a parabola and which one a straight line."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Using the quadratic model in a short-channel transistor. It overestimates the current by a factor of several. Confusing \\( V_{th} \\) with \\( V_T \\). One is the threshold, hundreds of millivolts; the other the thermal voltage, 25.85 mV. Believing that a transistor that is off does not conduct. Subthreshold current is most of the standby consumption of a modern chip. Reading the node name as a length. “3 nm” is a commercial label. Handling gates without protection. An electrostatic discharge punctures the oxide."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment What is inversion in a MOS capacitor? The accumulation, under the gate, of so many minority carriers that the surface of the substrate changes type and forms a conducting channel. In a long channel, how does the saturation current change when \\( V_{ov} \\) is doubled? And with extreme velocity saturation? In a long channel it is quadrupled; with extreme velocity saturation, it is doubled. Why can the subthreshold slope not be less than 60 mV per decade at 300 K? Because the current is carried by carriers that surmount a barrier through thermal agitation, with a Boltzmann distribution: \\( V_T \\ln 10 = 59.5 \\) mV is the minimum per decade. According to Dennard, what happens to the power per area when scaling? It stays constant, because the power of each transistor falls as \\( 1/\\kappa^2 \\) and the number per area rises as \\( \\kappa^2 \\). What problem did the high-k dielectric solve? The tunneling leakage of very thin oxides: it gives the same capacitance per area with a greater physical thickness."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"MOS transistor: long channel, short channel and scaling","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/mosfet-escalado#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Yuan Taur and Tak H. Ning. Fundamentals of Modern VLSI Devices. 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2009. The reference book on the modern MOSFET: short channel, scaling and their limits. Chenming Calvin Hu. Modern Semiconductor Devices for Integrated Circuits. Pearson, 2010. Written by one of the authors of the FinFET and of the BSIM model, with velocity saturation and DIBL clearly explained. Robert H. Dennard et al. Design of Ion-Implanted MOSFET's with Very Small Physical Dimensions. IEEE Journal of Solid-State Circuits, 1974. The original paper on the scaling rules."},{"t":"Positioning without measuring","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#que-es","x":"01 Positioning without measuring A stepper motor turns in fixed increments, typically 1.8° (200 steps per revolution), each time the controller changes the current in its windings. If the load does not exceed its torque, the position is the number of steps sent: it is positioned open-loop, without an encoder. That makes it cheap and simple for 3D printers, small CNC machines, cameras and valves. The basic operation is presented in the technician program, in stepper motors. Type Rotor Characteristic Variable reluctance Toothed iron without a magnet No detent torque without current Permanent magnet Magnet with radial poles Large steps, from 7.5° to 15°, inexpensive Hybrid Axial magnet between two toothed caps Steps of 1.8° or 0.9° with good torque: the most used"},{"t":"Excitation sequences","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#secuencias","x":"02 Excitation sequences Mode How it is energized Steps per revolution (1.8° motor) Torque Wave One phase at a time 200 Lower Full step Two phases at once 200 About 1.4 times that of wave Half step Alternates one and two phases 400 Uneven between steps Microstepping Stepped sine and cosine currents Up to 51,200 Smoother, less resonance Microstepping improves smoothness, but not accuracy in the same proportion: the torque that holds a microstep against the load is small, and the actual position deviates from the ideal value."},{"t":"Drive: why the torque falls with speed","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#accionamiento","x":"03 Drive: why the torque falls with speed Each winding is an inductor with resistance, and the current takes time to establish itself with the time constant \\( \\tau = L/R \\). At a few steps per second it reaches its rated value; at many, it reverses before getting there, and since the torque is proportional to the current, the motor loses strength. There are three ways to drive it: Drive How it works Effect Simple L/R The rated winding voltage is applied The current rises slowly: little torque at high speed L/R with series resistor Higher voltage and a resistor that limits the final current Smaller time constant, but the resistor dissipates power Constant-current chopper High voltage and an H-bridge that cuts off when the current reaches the set value Fast rise without dissipating: the current standard \\[ i(t) = \\frac{V}{R} + \\left(i_0 - \\frac{V}{R}\\right) e^{-t/\\tau} \\] At each step change the current starts from its previous value and tends toward \\( V/R \\). With the chopper, \\( V \\) is several times larger than the winding’s rated voltage and the current is cut off at the set value: it rises with slope \\( V/L \\), much faster. Lab · the current of a winding at different speeds A 200-steps-per-revolution motor with 5 mH, 2 Ω windings, rated 1.5 A. Driven in full step, each winding reverses its current every two steps. The graph shows one period of the current in steady state; the main figure is the average value of its magnitude, to which the torque is proportional. Simple L"},{"t":"The torque-speed curve and step loss","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#curva","x":"04 The torque-speed curve and step loss The manufacturer gives two curves. The pull-in curve indicates up to what speed the motor starts and stops without losing steps with a given load; the pull-out curve, how far one can go by accelerating gradually. Between the two, acceleration ramps are used. If the torque demanded exceeds the available one, the rotor lags more than half a step behind the field and loses steps: the controller thinks it is at one place and the motor is at another, without anyone noticing. At intermediate speeds a mechanical resonance also appears that can make it lose steps even with little load; microstepping and dampers attenuate it."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · The actual current With a current probe, measure the waveform of a winding at 100, 500 and 1000 steps per second, with a constant-current driver. Activity 2 · Losing steps Mark the shaft, give 2000 steps at increasing speed with a load on a pulley and measure how many were lost. Activity 3 · Microstepping Compare the noise and vibration of the same movement in full step and with 16 microsteps."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Starting at the final speed without a ramp. The motor loses steps or does not start. Setting the driver current above the rated value. The motor heats up, even if it gives more torque. Trusting the position without a reference. A limit switch has to be found at power-up. Powering the chopper with too little voltage. Without margin over the winding’s, it behaves like an L/R."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How many steps per second does a 1.8° motor make at 300 rpm in full step? \\( 300/60 \\cdot 200 = 1000 \\) steps per second. What time constant does a 5 mH, 2 Ω winding have? And with 6 Ω in series? 2.5 ms; with the resistor, \\( 5/8 = 0.625 \\) ms. How many microsteps per revolution does a 200-step motor give with 16 microsteps? 3200. Why does a chopper need a high voltage? Because the rise slope of the current is V/L: the more voltage, the faster it reaches the set value."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Stepper motors","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/motores-paso#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Takashi Kenjo and Akira Sugawara. Stepping Motors and Their Microprocessor Controls. 2nd ed., Clarendon Press, 1994. The reference book on stepper motors and their control. Austin Hughes and Bill Drury. Electric Motors and Drives. 5th ed., Newnes, 2019. The stepper motor chapter with the torque-speed curve and the drives. Douglas W. Jones. Control of Stepping Motors: A Tutorial. University of Iowa, online. A classic, practical tutorial on sequences, drives and microstepping."},{"t":"What travels in a wave","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#que","x":"01 What travels in a wave A wave carries energy and momentum, not matter. The particles of the medium oscillate around their position and stay where they were; what moves forward is the disturbance. This holds for sound, for a string and also for the signals in a cable. \\[ \\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial x^2} = \\frac{1}{v^2}\\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial t^2} \\qquad\\qquad y(x,t) = A\\sin(kx - \\omega t + \\varphi) \\] The wave equation and its harmonic solution. \\( k = 2\\pi/\\lambda \\) is the wavenumber and \\( \\omega = 2\\pi f \\) the angular frequency; the minus sign between the two terms indicates travel toward \\( +x \\), and the plus sign, toward \\( -x \\). \\[ v = \\lambda f \\] The most used relation in all of wave physics. The speed is set by the medium; the frequency, by the source. That is why, when passing from one medium to another, the wavelength changes and the frequency never does. Wave What oscillates Speed On a string Transverse position \\( \\sqrt{T/\\mu} \\): tension and linear density Sound in air Pressure 343 m/s at 20 °C In a coaxial cable Voltage and current \\( 1/\\sqrt{LC} \\) per unit length Electromagnetic Fields \\( E \\) and \\( B \\) \\( c/n \\) The third row is the one that connects this unit with electronics: a signal traveling along a cable is a wave, with its speed, its wavelength and its reflections, and it obeys the same equation as a plucked string."},{"t":"Superposition","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#superposicion","x":"02 Superposition When two waves coincide at a point, the displacements add. It is a principle of deceptive simplicity: from it come interference, beats, standing waves and, at bottom, the whole of Fourier analysis. Lab · superposition of two waves Two waves and their sum. Change the frequency of the second, its amplitude and its phase, and try reversing its direction of travel: that is where the standing wave appears. both travel to the right the second travels in the opposite direction Frequency of the second = 1.00 times the first Amplitude of the second = 1.00 Phase shift = 0° Time = 0 The three cases to recognize With equal frequency and the same direction, the sum is another wave of the same frequency whose amplitude depends on the phase shift: maximum in phase, zero in antiphase. With slightly different frequencies a beat appears, a slow envelope of frequency \\( |f_1 - f_2| \\), which is what you hear when tuning a guitar and what makes a superheterodyne receiver work. And with opposite directions, the wave stops advancing: it becomes standing, with fixed nodes where the amplitude is always zero."},{"t":"Standing waves and reflection","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#estacionarias","x":"03 Standing waves and reflection A wave that reaches the end of its medium is reflected. If the end is fixed, it comes back inverted; if it is free, it comes back upright. The superposition of the incident wave with the reflected one gives the standing wave. \\[ \\lambda_n = \\frac{2L}{n} \\qquad\\qquad f_n = n\\,\\frac{v}{2L} \\] Modes of a string fixed at both ends. Only certain frequencies “fit” in the available length, and that is the reason a stringed instrument sounds in tune and not like noise. The same phenomenon, in a cable carrying a signal, is a problem. If the end is not terminated with the correct impedance, part of the signal comes back and superimposes on the one arriving: steps appear, overshoots and —in radio frequency— a standing wave along the line. The measure of how much comes back is the standing wave ratio: \\[ \\Gamma = \\frac{Z_L - Z_0}{Z_L + Z_0} \\qquad\\qquad \\mathrm{SWR} = \\frac{1 + |\\Gamma|}{1 - |\\Gamma|} \\] With the load equal to the characteristic impedance, \\( \\Gamma = 0 \\) and the SWR is 1: nothing comes back. With the end open or short-circuited everything comes back, and the SWR goes to infinity. The Doppler effect When the source and the observer approach each other, the perceived frequency rises; as they move apart, it drops. It is the phenomenon of the sound of a passing siren, and also the principle of the speed radar, which measures the shift of the wave bouncing off a car. In electronics it also appears as a problem in mobile communications: the D"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Modes of a string With a generator and an electromagnetic driver on a taut string, find the frequencies that produce one, two and three antinodes. Verify the proportionality with \\( n \\) and obtain the wave speed; compare it with \\( \\sqrt{T/\\mu} \\). Activity 2 · Audible beats With two audio generators near 440 Hz and a speaker, separate them little by little and count the beats per second. Verify that they match the difference in frequencies, and observe the envelope on the oscilloscope. Activity 3 · Reflection in a cable Send a narrow pulse down a long coaxial cable and observe the end with the oscilloscope, first open, then short-circuited and finally terminated with 50 Ω. Identify the sign of each reflection and verify that with the correct termination it disappears."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that the wave carries matter. It carries energy; the medium oscillates and stays. Thinking that the frequency changes when the medium changes. It is set by the source; what changes is \\( \\lambda \\). Confusing the speed of the wave with the speed of the particles of the medium. They are different things and very different values. Believing that there is no energy in a standing wave. There is: what there is not is net transport. Leaving a signal cable unterminated. What is not absorbed comes back, and superimposes on what arrives. Adding intensities instead of amplitudes. What superimposes is the amplitudes; the intensity goes with the square."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What does a wave carry? Energy and momentum. The medium oscillates around its position and does not move along. When passing to another medium, what changes? The speed and the wavelength. The frequency is set by the source and does not change. What is the frequency of a beat? The difference between the two frequencies that are superimposed. When does a standing wave appear? When two equal waves traveling in opposite directions are superimposed, almost always by reflection at an end. What is the SWR of a properly terminated line? One: with \\( Z_L = Z_0 \\) the reflection coefficient is zero and nothing comes back. What happens on reflection at a fixed end? The wave comes back inverted. At a free end it comes back upright."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Wave motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-ondulatorio#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. Wave motion, superposition, standing waves and the Doppler effect. Frank S. Crawford. Waves: Berkeley Physics Course, Vol. 3. McGraw-Hill, 1968 (Spanish edition, “Ondas. Berkeley Physics Course, vol. 3”, Reverté). An entire volume devoted to waves, with the modes approach that later reappears throughout engineering. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. The natural continuation: transmission lines, reflection coefficient and SWR treated seriously."},{"t":"Simple harmonic motion","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#mas","x":"01 Simple harmonic motion Whenever a restoring force is proportional to the displacement and points in the opposite direction, the resulting motion is simple harmonic. Hooke’s law, \\(F = -k \\cdot x\\), combined with the second law gives: \\[ m \\frac{d^2 x}{d t^2} + k x = 0 \\quad \\implies \\quad x (t) = A \\cos (\\omega_0 t + \\varphi), \\; \\omega_0 = \\sqrt{\\frac{k}{m}} \\] The natural frequency does not depend on the amplitude: that is why a pendulum works as a clock. System Natural angular frequency Condition Mass and spring \\( \\omega _{0} = \\sqrt{k/m} \\) Ideal spring Simple pendulum \\( \\omega _{0} = \\sqrt{g/L} \\) Small angles Physical pendulum \\( \\omega _{0} = \\sqrt{m\\cdot g\\cdot d/I} \\) Small angles LC circuit \\( \\omega _{0} = 1/\\sqrt{L\\cdot C} \\) No resistance The energy is exchanged between kinetic and elastic potential, and their sum stays constant: \\(E = \\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot k \\cdot A^2\\). In the LC circuit exactly the same thing happens between the capacitor and the inductor."},{"t":"Damped oscillations","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#amortiguado","x":"02 Damped oscillations With a viscous friction force proportional to the velocity, \\(F = -b \\cdot v\\), the equation is completed: \\[ m \\ddot{x} + b \\dot{x} + k x = 0 \\] It is the same equation as the series RLC circuit, with L in place of m, R in place of b and 1/C in place of k. Regime Condition Behavior Underdamped \\( b < 2\\sqrt{m\\cdot k} \\) Oscillates with an amplitude that decays exponentially Critically damped \\( b = 2\\sqrt{m\\cdot k} \\) Returns to rest in the shortest possible time, without oscillating Overdamped \\( b > 2\\sqrt{m\\cdot k} \\) Returns slowly, without oscillating The quality factor Q \\(Q = \\sqrt{m \\cdot k}/b\\) measures how many oscillations survive before the amplitude drops appreciably. It is the same Q as in a resonant circuit: a high Q means little loss, a sharp resonance and selectivity. A quartz crystal reaches a Q of 10 5, and that is why it sets the frequency of a clock or of a microcontroller. Critical damping is what is sought in a car’s shock absorber, in the motion of the needle of an analog instrument and in the response of a switching power supply: reaching the final value quickly without overshooting."},{"t":"Forced oscillations and resonance","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#forzado","x":"03 Forced oscillations and resonance If a periodic force \\(F_0 \\cdot \\cos(\\omega t)\\) is also applied, once the transient has died out the system oscillates at the frequency of the excitation, not its own, and with an amplitude that depends on how close one is to the other: \\[ A (\\omega) = \\frac{F_0 / m}{\\sqrt{{\\left(\\omega_0^2 -\\omega^2 \\right)}^2 + {\\left(b \\omega / m \\right)}^2}} \\] When \\(\\omega\\) approaches \\(\\omega_0\\) the denominator becomes small and the amplitude grows: that is resonance. With little damping the amplitude can become destructive —it is what shatters glasses with sound and what forces engineers to study the wind on a bridge—, but put to good use it is what lets you tune in a station among all those that reach the antenna. Lab · damped and driven oscillator Top, the response in time; bottom, the resonance curve. It is the same lab as an RLC circuit, with different names. Apply a periodic force Mass 1.0 kg Spring constant 40 N/m Damping 0.40 N·s/m Driving frequency 6.30 rad/s"},{"t":"The analogy worth memorizing","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#analogia","x":"04 The analogy worth memorizing Mechanical Electrical (series RLC) Role Mass m Inductance L Inertia: opposes change Damping b Resistance R Dissipates energy Constant k \\( \\dfrac{1}{C} \\) Restoring: stores energy Position x Charge q State variable Velocity v Current i Its derivative Force F Voltage V Excitation \\( \\omega _{0} = \\sqrt{k/m} \\) \\( \\omega _{0} = 1/\\sqrt{L\\cdot C} \\) Natural frequency \\( Q = \\sqrt{m\\cdot k}/b \\) \\( Q = \\dfrac{1}{R}\\cdot \\sqrt{L/C} \\) Selectivity It is not a metaphor: the two differential equations are identical, so everything that is proved in one domain holds in the other. Whoever understood the response of a spring has already understood that of a filter, and vice versa."},{"t":"Where it shows up in electronics","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#electronica","x":"05 Where it shows up in electronics Quartz crystals and MEMS resonators. A mechanical oscillator of very high Q that sets the timebase of almost everything digital. Filters and tuning. The resonance curve in this lab is the frequency response of a bandpass filter. Piezoelectric transducers. Ultrasonic emitters work at their mechanical resonance frequency, where the efficiency is highest. Vibration-isolation mounting. Onboard equipment is mounted on elastic elements chosen so that its natural frequency stays far from that of the excitation. Response of a switching power supply. The feedback loop is compensated aiming for a behavior close to critical: fast but without overshoot."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring g with a pendulum Time twenty oscillations of a pendulum of known length and solve for \\(g\\) from \\(T = 2\\pi\\sqrt{L/g}\\). Repeat with amplitudes of 5° and 30° to see where the approximation stops holding. Activity 2 · Spring constant and Q Measure k statically with weights, then let the mass oscillate and film it. From the decay of the amplitude you get \\(b\\), and with it \\(Q\\). Verify that the measured \\(\\omega_0\\) matches \\(\\sqrt{k/m}\\). Activity 3 · Resonance with a speaker and an accelerometer Drive a cantilevered strip with a speaker sweeping through frequencies and record the vibration with an accelerometer. Plot the amplitude against frequency and measure the −3 dB bandwidth: \\(Q = f_0/\\Delta f\\), the same definition as in a resonant circuit."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Believing the period of a pendulum depends on the mass. It does not; it depends only on the length. Using the pendulum formula with large amplitudes. It holds as long as \\(\\sin\\theta \\approx \\theta\\). Confusing frequency \\(f\\) with angular frequency \\(\\omega\\). \\(\\omega = 2\\pi \\cdot f\\). Assuming that in the forced regime the system oscillates at its natural frequency. It oscillates at that of the excitation; the natural one appears only in the transient. Believing that more damping is always better. Beyond critical, the response becomes slower. Thinking resonance is always a problem. Without resonance there would be no radio."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment A 0.5 kg mass on a 200 N/m spring. What is its natural frequency? \\(\\omega_0 = \\sqrt{200/0.5} = 20\\text{ rad/s}\\), that is \\(f_0 = 20/2\\pi \\approx\\) \\(3.18\\text{ Hz}\\). What must b be for critical damping in that system? \\(b = 2\\sqrt{m \\cdot k} = 2\\sqrt{100} =\\) \\(20\\,\\mathrm{N \\cdot s/m}\\). How long is a pendulum that takes 2 s for each complete oscillation? \\(L = g \\cdot T^2/(4\\pi^2) = 9.8 \\cdot 4/39.48 \\approx\\) \\(0.99\\text{ m}\\). It is the classic seconds pendulum. What is the electrical equivalent of mass? Inductance: both oppose abrupt changes in their variable —velocity and current— and store energy in the same form, \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot m \\cdot v^2\\) and \\(\\tfrac{1}{2} \\cdot L \\cdot i^2\\). A system has \\(Q = 50\\) and \\(f_0 = 1\\text{ kHz}\\). What is its bandwidth? \\(\\Delta f = f_0/Q =\\) \\(20\\text{ Hz}\\). The same calculation as for a resonant circuit. Why does a quartz crystal define a frequency so well? Because its extremely high Q makes the resonance curve very sharp: only an extremely narrow band of frequencies produces appreciable oscillation."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Oscillatory motion","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/movimiento-oscilatorio#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapter 15 covers simple, damped and forced harmonic motion. A. P. French. Vibrations and Waves. W. W. Norton, 1971 (Spanish edition, “Vibraciones y ondas”, Reverté, 1974). An entire book devoted to the subject, with the mechanical–electrical parallel developed in earnest. Richard P. Feynman. The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I. Addison-Wesley, 1963 (Spanish edition, “Física, volumen I”, Addison-Wesley / Pearson). Chapters 21 to 24 treat the oscillator and resonance as a single phenomenon with many faces. Paul A. Tipler and Gene Mosca. Physics for Scientists and Engineers, Volume 1. 6th ed., W. H. Freeman, 2008 (Spanish edition, “Física para la ciencia y la tecnología”, Reverté, 2010). With good numerical examples of quality factor and bandwidth."},{"t":"The statement, and why it is not obvious","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#enunciado","x":"01 The statement, and why it is not obvious Sampling means keeping the value of a signal every \\( T \\) seconds and throwing away everything that happened in between. It seems that information is lost forever, and yet: \\[ f_s \\gt 2\\,f_{\\text{max}} \\;\\Longrightarrow\\; x(t) \\text{ can be recovered exactly} \\] If the signal is bandlimited and is sampled at more than twice its highest frequency, the samples contain all the information. Nothing was lost. That is the sampling theorem, and it is the reason why digital audio, cellular telephony and any instrument with an A/D converter exist. The frequency \\( 2f_{\\text{max}} \\) is called the Nyquist rate, and the statement is attributed to Nyquist, Shannon, Kotelnikov and Whittaker depending on the country where it is studied."},{"t":"What sampling does to the spectrum","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#replicas","x":"02 What sampling does to the spectrum Ideal sampling is multiplying the signal by a train of impulses spaced \\( T \\) apart. And by the modulation theorem, multiplying in time is convolving in frequency. Since the transform of an impulse train is another impulse train, the result is: \\[ X_s(j\\omega) = \\frac{1}{T}\\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty} X\\big(j(\\omega - k\\omega_s)\\big) \\] The original spectrum, repeated every \\( \\omega_s \\). Sampling does not destroy the spectrum: it clones it infinitely many times. That is the whole theorem, and it is easier to see than to prove. If the replicas do not touch, the original remains intact in the middle and a low-pass filter is enough to recover it. If they overlap, they add up and there is no filter that can separate them: that is aliasing. Lab · the replicas of the spectrum On top, the spectrum of the signal and its replicas every \\( f_s \\). Below, the original signal and the one reconstructed from the samples. Lower the sampling frequency until the replicas touch. signal with three tones wideband signal a single tone Show the reconstruction Sampling frequency = 10.0 Hz"},{"t":"How the signal is recovered","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#reconstruccion","x":"03 How the signal is recovered If the replicas do not overlap, you filter with an ideal low-pass with cutoff \\( f_s/2 \\) and the original is left. In time, that filter is a sinc, and the reconstruction takes the form of an interpolation: \\[ x(t) = \\sum_{n=-\\infty}^{\\infty} x[n]\\, \\frac{\\operatorname{sin}\\big(\\pi(t - nT)/T\\big)}{\\pi(t - nT)/T} \\] Each sample is replaced by a sinc centered on it, and they are all added. The sinc equals 1 at its own sample and 0 at all the others, so the sum passes exactly through the measured points. That formula is exact but unrealizable: the sinc extends to infinity in both directions, and we already saw that this filter is neither causal nor BIBO stable. In practice a D/A converter does something else: it holds each sample until the next one. The D/A staircase, and why it has to be corrected Holding the value between samples is equivalent to convolving with a rectangular pulse of width \\( T \\), whose spectrum is a sinc. That attenuates the high frequencies of the useful band: at the edge, \\( f_s/2 \\), the drop is \\( 2/\\pi \\), that is, −3.92 dB. That is why audio converters include a compensation filter —which appears in datasheets as sinc correction — and why it pays to oversample: if the useful band occupies only a fraction of \\( f_s/2 \\), the sinc roll-off in that region is negligible."},{"t":"The numbers in practice","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#practica","x":"04 The numbers in practice Application Useful band \\( f_s \\) Margin Telephony 3.4 kHz 8 kHz 1.18 times the Nyquist rate Compact disc 20 kHz 44.1 kHz 1.10 times Professional audio 20 kHz 48 or 96 kHz 1.2 or 2.4 times Digital oscilloscope 100 MHz 1 GS/s or more 5 times or more None of them works right at the limit, for two reasons. The first is that a real anti-aliasing filter does not cut off vertically: it needs a transition band between the useful frequency and \\( f_s/2 \\), and that band is the margin. The second is the oscilloscope row: to see a waveform it is not enough to be able to reconstruct it in theory; many samples per period are needed for the drawing to be recognizable. Telephony shows the tightest case: with an 8 kHz sampling rate and a 3.4 kHz band, barely 600 Hz are left for the filter to go from passing to fully attenuating. That is why the filters in telephone channels are of high order."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Finding the limit Using an A/D converter, sample a 1 kHz sinusoid at 10, 4, 2.5 and 1.8 kS/s, reconstruct with the D/A and observe the output. Identify from what frequency it stops resembling the original. Activity 2 · Measuring the sinc roll-off Sweep the frequency of a sinusoid between DC and \\( f_s/2 \\) while keeping the input amplitude constant, and measure the amplitude at the D/A output. Compare the measured curve with \\( \\operatorname{sinc}(f/f_s) \\) and verify the −3.92 dB at the edge. Activity 3 · The anti-aliasing filter Design the anti-aliasing filter for a system with a 4 kHz useful band and \\( f_s = 10 \\) kHz. Determine what order is needed to attenuate 60 dB at 5 kHz starting from 0 dB at 4 kHz."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using exactly \\( f_s = 2f_{\\text{max}} \\). The theorem requires more than twice; at the exact limit a sinusoid can always be sampled at its zero crossings. Forgetting the anti-aliasing filter. The theorem requires the signal to be bandlimited, and that does not happen by itself: it has to be enforced. Confusing the Nyquist rate with \\( f_s/2 \\). The Nyquist rate is \\( 2f_{\\text{max}} \\); \\( f_s/2 \\) is the limit of the representable band. Believing that the D/A reconstructs exactly. The staircase attenuates; it has to be compensated. Sampling at exactly twice because “the theorem allows it”. Without margin, the anti-aliasing filter is unrealizable."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does sampling do to the spectrum? It repeats it every \\( f_s \\). If the replicas do not overlap, the original remains whole. What condition does the theorem require? That the signal be bandlimited and that \\( f_s \\) be greater than twice its maximum frequency. What is the exact interpolator? The sinc: each sample is replaced by a sinc centered on it and they are all added. Why is the sinc not used in practice? Because it extends infinitely to both sides: it is neither causal nor BIBO stable. How much does the D/A staircase attenuate at \\( f_s/2 \\)? \\( 2/\\pi \\), that is, −3.92 dB. That is why converters include sinc compensation. Why does the CD use 44.1 kHz and not 40? To leave margin for the anti-aliasing filter between the 20 kHz of useful band and the 22.05 kHz of \\( f_s/2 \\)."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The sampling theorem","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/muestreo-shannon#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1997. The complete Chapter 7: sampling, replicas, reconstruction and the zero-order hold. Walt Kester (ed.). Data Conversion Handbook. Analog Devices / Newnes, 2005. Sampling from the side of the real converter: jitter, aperture and sinc compensation. John G. Proakis and Dimitris G. Manolakis. Digital Signal Processing: Principles, Algorithms, and Applications. 4th ed. (Spanish edition, “Tratamiento digital de señales”), Prentice Hall, 2006. Sampling and reconstruction with the mathematical detail and the filter design criteria."},{"t":"Four layers, three junctions","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#cuatro-capas","x":"01 Four layers, three junctions A thyristor (or SCR, silicon-controlled rectifier) is a PNPN structure: a P anode, a wide, lightly doped N layer that withstands the voltage, a P layer that carries the gate terminal, and an N cathode. It has three junctions in series, and with the anode positive the middle one is reverse biased and blocks. So far it looks like a diode that does not conduct in either direction. The trick is that, once it starts to conduct, it keeps conducting by itself, even if the gate signal is removed. It is a power device: there are ones of a few amperes for controlling a lamp and ones of several kilovolts and thousands of amperes, made on an entire silicon wafer, for high-voltage direct-current transmission stations."},{"t":"The two-transistor model","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#dos-transistores","x":"02 The two-transistor model The PNPN structure can be cut into two interlocked transistors: a PNP formed by the first three layers and an NPN by the last three. The collector of each one feeds the base of the other. With common-base gains \\( \\alpha_1 \\) and \\( \\alpha_2 \\), the anode current becomes \\[ I_A = \\frac{\\alpha_2\\, I_G + I_{CBO1} + I_{CBO2}}{1 - (\\alpha_1 + \\alpha_2)} \\] The \\( \\alpha \\) of the bipolar transistors grow with the current. When their sum reaches 1, the denominator vanishes and the current is limited only by the external circuit. That is positive feedback: a gate pulse makes the NPN conduct, its collector feeds the base of the PNP, the PNP returns more base current to the NPN, and within microseconds both are saturated. From that moment the gate no longer has control. The thyristor turns off by itself only when the anode current drops below the holding current \\( I_H \\), which in alternating current happens by itself at every zero crossing. Triggering method What happens Gate pulse The normal, controlled way Forward breakover voltage The leakage current grows until \\( \\alpha_1 + \\alpha_2 = 1 \\) Excessive \\( dv/dt \\) The current that charges the capacitance of the central junction acts as gate current: an unwanted trigger Temperature Leakages and gains rise Light Pairs generated in the central junction; the thyristors of high-voltage valves are triggered by optical fiber"},{"t":"Phase control","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#control-fase","x":"03 Phase control On an AC mains, firing the thyristor with a delay \\( \\alpha \\) relative to the zero crossing lets through only the final part of each half-cycle. By varying the angle, the power delivered to the load is regulated while dissipating almost nothing in the control. With a resistive load and full-wave control: \\[ V_{rms} = V_m \\sqrt{\\frac{1}{2\\pi} \\left( \\pi - \\alpha + \\frac{\\sin 2\\alpha}{2} \\right)} \\] \\[ \\frac{P}{P_{\\max}} = \\frac{1}{\\pi} \\left( \\pi - \\alpha + \\frac{\\sin 2\\alpha}{2} \\right) \\] With a single thyristor, which conducts in one half-cycle, the power is halved and a DC component appears, \\( \\dfrac{V_m}{2\\pi} (1 + \\cos\\alpha) \\). Lab · phase control at 220 V A 100 Ω resistive load on the 220 V, 50 Hz mains, controlled by a thyristor (half-wave) or by a TRIAC (full-wave). You choose the firing angle: the plot shows the mains, the voltage across the load and the gate pulses over one cycle. TRIAC: conducts in both half-cycles thyristor (SCR): only in the positive half-cycle Firing angle \\( \\alpha \\) ="},{"t":"The four-layer family","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#familia","x":"04 The four-layer family Device Structure Use TRIAC Two thyristors in antiparallel with a common gate Dimmers, speed control of universal motors, solid-state relays DIAC Triggers in both directions when about 30 V is exceeded, with no gate Generates the TRIAC trigger pulse in a dimmer GTO Thyristor that can be turned off with a large negative gate current Electric traction and large converters, now almost replaced TRIAC optocoupler An LED that triggers a TRIAC by light, isolated Drives a power TRIAC from a microcontroller The same four layers appear unintentionally in CMOS circuits: the latch-up of the CMOS inverter is a parasitic thyristor that triggers and short-circuits the supply, and the two-transistor model explains why it is avoided by placing substrate contacts close to every transistor: they lower the parasitic gains."},{"t":"The IGBT","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#igbt","x":"05 The IGBT The insulated-gate bipolar transistor (IGBT) combines a MOSFET at the input with a PNP bipolar at the output: the gate is driven by voltage, with almost no current, like a MOS, and the main current is conducted by a bipolar structure. It also has four layers, but it is designed so that the parasitic thyristor never triggers. Its advantage is conductivity modulation: when conducting, the P collector layer injects holes into the lightly doped N region that withstands the voltage and fills it with carriers, greatly lowering its resistance. A 1200 V MOSFET has to have that lightly doped region thick and it conducts poorly; the IGBT, with the same region flooded with carriers, drops only about 1.5 to 2 V. The cost is the tail current at turn-off, while those carriers recombine, which limits the frequency to tens of kilohertz. Power MOSFET IGBT Thyristor Control Voltage Voltage Current pulse; it cannot be turned off through the gate Typical range Up to a few hundred volts 600 V to 6.5 kV Up to more than 8 kV Frequency Hundreds of kilohertz Up to tens of kilohertz That of the mains Use Switching power supplies Motor inverters, welders, induction cooktops Phase control, high-voltage transmission"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Mains voltage Activities with 220 V are carried out with an isolation transformer, with the circuit mounted in an enclosure and under the supervision of the instructor. The oscilloscope is not connected directly to the mains: differential probes are used. Activity 1 · Latching With a thyristor, a 12 V DC source and a 12 V lamp, trigger it with a pushbutton on the gate and verify that the lamp stays on when the button is released. Lower the voltage until it goes off and estimate the holding current. Activity 2 · The two-transistor model Build a thyristor with a BC548 and a BC558 connected according to the model, and verify that it latches just like the integrated device. Activity 3 · A dimmer With a commercial TRIAC and DIAC dimmer powered from a low-voltage isolation transformer, use the oscilloscope to observe the voltage across the load and measure the firing angle for three potentiometer positions."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Expecting the thyristor to turn off when the gate is removed. It stays latched until the current drops below \\( I_H \\). Using an ordinary thyristor in direct current without planning how to turn it off. In DC the current never passes through zero. Forgetting the snubber network. Without an RC in parallel, the \\( dv/dt \\) of the mains or of the inductive load triggers the device unintentionally. Measuring with the oscilloscope referenced to earth on the mains. The probe ground short-circuits the circuit. Confusing the IGBT with a large MOSFET. It conducts with a nearly fixed drop, not with a resistance, and it has tail current at turn-off."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Why does the thyristor keep conducting without gate current? Because each of the two transistors of the model supplies base current to the other; with \\( \\alpha_1 + \\alpha_2 = 1 \\) the feedback sustains itself. How is a thyristor turned off in an AC circuit? By itself: at every zero crossing the current drops below the holding current. With a TRIAC and \\( \\alpha = 90° \\), what fraction of the maximum power does a resistive load receive? Half: \\( (\\pi - \\pi/2 + 0)/\\pi = 0.5 \\). What is conductivity modulation in the IGBT? The flooding of the drift region with carriers injected by the P collector, which lowers its resistance when conducting. What is the relationship between the thyristor and latch-up? Latch-up is the triggering of a parasitic thyristor formed by the P and N regions of a CMOS circuit."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Multijunction devices: thyristors and IGBTs","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/multijunturas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading B. Jayant Baliga. Fundamentals of Power Semiconductor Devices. 2nd ed., Springer, 2019. The reference on thyristors, IGBTs and power MOSFETs, written by the inventor of the IGBT. Muhammad H. Rashid. Power Electronics: Devices, Circuits, and Applications. 4th ed., Pearson, 2014. Phase control and trigger circuits, with many worked examples. Ned Mohan, Tore M. Undeland and William P. Robbins. Power Electronics: Converters, Applications, and Design. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2003. The devices seen from the converter: what each application demands."},{"t":"A schematic is not a drawing of the circuit","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#esquema","x":"01 A schematic is not a drawing of the circuit A photo of a board shows where each component is; the schematic shows how they are connected. They are two different documents and both are needed, but to understand what a circuit does the second one is what helps. Document What it shows What it is used for Functional diagram Logical connections, without real positions Understanding, analyzing, repairing Multi-line diagram Each conductor, one by one Detailed wiring Single-line diagram A single line represents several conductors Electrical distribution, panels Layout drawing Physical location of the components Assembly and maintenance Wiring diagram Which terminal goes to which terminal Building the panel That is why the symbols are standardized: what you want to read at a glance is the topology, and for that it helps if a resistor always looks the same."},{"t":"Two families of symbols","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#simbolos","x":"02 Two families of symbols Two main conventions coexist, and you have to be able to read both because datasheets come mixed: Family Standard Distinguishing feature European / international IEC 60617, IRAM Rectangular shapes: the resistor is a rectangle American ANSI Y32.2, IEEE 315 Distinctive shapes: the resistor is a zigzag In practice Most schematic capture programs come with both libraries and many designers mix them: a zigzag resistor—out of habit—with rectangular gates. It is not ideal, but you see it every day. What you cannot do is change convention within the same drawing. Lab · standard symbols Choose a component and compare how each standard draws it. The reference designator is the letter used to name it on the drawing. Resistor Capacitor Electrolytic capacitor Inductor Diode LED NPN bipolar transistor DC voltage source Earth or ground Fuse Normally open pushbutton Relay contact Transformer AND gate OR gate Inverter"},{"t":"Reference designators","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#referencias","x":"03 Reference designators Every component carries a letter and a number that identify it throughout the whole set of documents: in the schematic, in the board silkscreen and in the bill of materials. That way you know that the R7 on the drawing is the same R7 in the parts list. Letter Component Letter Component R Resistor Q Transistor C Capacitor U or IC Integrated circuit L Inductor J or P Connector D Diode or LED SW or S Switch or pushbutton T Transformer K Relay or contactor F Fuse M Motor X Terminal block Y Valve or solenoid In electrical installations IEC 81346 also applies, which adds prefixes for location and function. In board-level electronics the letter and number are enough, numbering from left to right and from top to bottom."},{"t":"Single-line and multi-line diagrams","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#unifilar","x":"04 Single-line and multi-line diagrams In an electrical installation drawing it makes no sense to draw the three conductors of a three-phase circuit plus the neutral and the earth: a single line is drawn with a mark that indicates how many conductors it carries and of what cross-section. That is the single-line diagram, and it is the one submitted to get an installation approved. Single-line: one line per circuit, with the number of conductors indicated. You see the distribution of a panel at a glance. Multi-line: one line per conductor. It is the one used by whoever builds the panel to do the wiring. Functional or control diagram: shows the control logic—contacts and coils—without the power circuit. The documentation of an installation usually includes all three, plus the layout drawing on top of the architectural plan."},{"t":"Drawing rules","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#trazado","x":"05 Drawing rules Signal flows from left to right and power from top to bottom: input on the left, output on the right, positive at the top, ground at the bottom. A connection has a dot, a crossing has none. A four-conductor crossing is never made with a dot: it is drawn as two offset T junctions, so that there is no ambiguity if the dot is lost when photocopying. Horizontal and vertical lines, with right angles, and as few crossings as possible. Ground and power symbols instead of long wires that cross the whole drawing. Net labels to connect distant blocks without drawing the wire. Values next to the symbol, aligned, and in a standard format: 4k7 instead of 4.7 kΩ prevents the decimal point from being lost in a copy."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · From the built circuit to the schematic Take a simple board—a regulated power supply with an LM317, for example—and reverse-engineer its schematic by following the traces with the multimeter in continuity mode. Compare it with the original schematic. Activity 2 · The same circuit, two standards Draw the same circuit with IEC and with ANSI symbols. Discuss which one reads faster and why. Activity 3 · Single-line diagram of a panel Survey the workshop panel and draw its single-line diagram with the AEA symbols: service entrance, main switch, residual-current device, circuit breakers and branch circuits, with cross-sections and protective devices."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Mixing standards within the same drawing. Four-conductor crossings with a dot, which become ambiguous when copied. Repeating a reference designator: there cannot be two R5. Drawing the schematic following the physical layout of the board: the result is unreadable. Omitting the values or placing them far from the component. Forgetting the power pins of the integrated circuits, or not declaring them anywhere in the drawing."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What is the difference between the IEC and the ANSI symbol for a resistor? IEC draws it as a rectangle; ANSI, as a zigzag. Which letter designates an integrated circuit? U, and IC in older drawings. When is a single-line diagram used? In electrical distribution and panels, where the structure of the circuit matters and not each conductor. Why is a four-conductor crossing not drawn with a dot? Because if the dot is lost in a copy, the drawing changes meaning. Two offset T junctions are drawn instead. What does writing 4k7 mean? 4.7 kΩ. The letter takes the place of the decimal point precisely so that it is not lost when printing. In which direction should a schematic be laid out? Signal from left to right, power at the top and ground at the bottom."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Standards for representing circuits","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-circuitos#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading International Electrotechnical Commission. IEC 60617 · Graphical symbols for diagrams. IEC. The official symbol database; IRAM adopts this criterion. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. The appendix on good schematic practice is brief and excellent. Asociación Electrotécnica Argentina (Argentine Electrotechnical Association). AEA 90364 regulations. AEA. Symbols and documentation required for electrical installations in Argentina."},{"t":"What standardization is for","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#paraque","x":"01 What standardization is for A standard is a written agreement on how things are done. In technical drawing that means that the drawing that leaves an office in Rosario is manufactured in a workshop in Shenzhen without phone calls, because both read the same thing. Body Scope Examples IRAM Argentina IRAM 4504 sheet formats, 4502 lines, 4513 dimensioning ISO International ISO 216 paper formats, ISO 128 representation, ISO 129 dimensioning IEC International, electrotechnology IEC 60617 symbols, IEC 61082 documents ANSI / ASME United States ASME Y14.5 geometric tolerancing AEA Argentina, electrical installations AEA 90364 regulations for electrical installations IRAM is a member of ISO and adopts a good part of its standards, so in practice what is studied here matches the international criteria. The important differences appear with the US system: third-angle projection, inches and another family of sheet formats."},{"t":"Sheet formats","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#formatos","x":"02 Sheet formats The A series starts from A0, which has an area of exactly 1 m² and a ratio between its sides of √2. That is the only ratio that is preserved when the sheet is cut in half: that is why two A4 make an A3, two A3 an A2, and so on. Format Size (mm) Area A0 841 × 1189 1 m² A1 594 × 841 0.5 m² A2 420 × 594 0.25 m² A3 297 × 420 0.125 m² A4 210 × 297 0.0625 m² Every sheet carries a border margin —normally 10 mm, and 20 mm on the filing side for punching—and a title block in the lower right corner, with the part name, the scale, the projection symbol, the date, who drew it and who checked it, and the drawing number. Lab · format, scale and usable area Choose the format and the scale, and check whether the part fits on the sheet with its margins and its title block. A4 · 210 × 297 A3 · 297 × 420 A2 · 420 × 594 A1 · 594 × 841 A0 · 841 × 1189 Landscape Portrait Scale 2:1 · enlargement Scale 1:1 · full size Scale 1:2 Scale 1:5 Scale 1:10 Scale 1:20 Scale 1:50 Scale 1:100 Part length 400 mm Part width 250 mm"},{"t":"Scales","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#escalas","x":"03 Scales The scale is the ratio between the size on the drawing and the actual size. It is written drawing: actual, and the standard ones are multiples of 1, 2 and 5: Type Standard values When it is used Enlargement 2:1, 5:1, 10:1, 20:1 Small parts: a connector, a package Full size 1:1 Circuit boards, covers, medium-sized parts Reduction 1:2, 1:5, 1:10, 1:20, 1:50, 1:100 Panels, rooms, installations Dimensions always show the real size Even if the drawing is at 1:10, the dimensions give the true size of the part, not that of the paper. The scale is there so that the drawing fits and looks proportioned, not so that someone measures with a ruler on the drawing."},{"t":"Lines and dimensioning","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#lineas","x":"04 Lines and dimensioning Line Stroke Meaning Thick continuous ——— Visible edges and outlines Thin continuous ——— Dimensions, extension lines, section hatching Dashed - - - - Hidden edges Thin chain (dash and dot) — · — · Centerlines and symmetry Thick chain (dash and dot) — · — · Cutting plane Thin freehand ∿∿∿ Break or limit of a partial view The ratio between thick and thin lines is 2 to 1. And for dimensioning there are four rules that solve almost everything: Each measurement is dimensioned only once, in the view where it is seen best. Dimensions go outside the part whenever possible, and the small ones closer than the large ones so that the lines do not cross. Figures are read from the bottom or from the right, and are never crossed by a line. Diameters carry ⌀, radii R, and measurements are in millimeters without writing the unit."},{"t":"What an electronics engineer documents","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#electronica","x":"05 What an electronics engineer documents Enclosure drawing. With the cutouts for connectors, display and ventilation, dimensioned from a common origin so that the workshop can machine them. Panel layout drawing. Physical location of rails, terminal blocks and protective devices. Electrical schematic. With IEC or IRAM symbols and reference designators. Board documentation. Silkscreen, drill drawing and Gerber files: the electronic equivalent of the manufacturing drawing. Installation drawing. On top of an architectural plan, at 1:50 or 1:100, with the AEA symbols."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Choosing format and scale For five objects from the workshop, decide in which format and at which scale each should be drawn, and justify the choice. Check that the choice leaves room for dimensions and the title block. Activity 2 · Standard title block Design your own title block that complies with IRAM 4508 and use it in all the work for the course. It is the first step toward consistent documentation. Activity 3 · Dimensioning an enclosure cover Measure a real cover with a caliper, draw it to scale and dimension all its holes from two reference edges. Then check whether someone else could manufacture it from that drawing without seeing the part."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Dimensioning the size on paper instead of the real one. Repeating dimensions in more than one view: if one measurement changes, a contradiction remains. Using non-standard scales, such as 1:3 or 1:7. Forgetting the title block or leaving it without the scale or the projection symbol. Writing “mm” on every dimension. It is declared once in the title block. Dimensioning to centerlines without indicating it, or dimensioning to hidden edges."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment How large is an A0 and why? 841 × 1189 mm: it is the rectangle of 1 m² whose side ratio is √2. How many A4 sheets fit in an A1? Eight: each cut in half goes down one format step, and from A1 to A4 there are three cuts, 2³ = 8. A part 1200 mm long drawn at 1:5: how long is it on paper? 240 mm. It fits on a landscape A3, which is 420 mm long, with room for dimensions. Which line is used for a hidden edge? The dashed one. What does ⌀25 mean in a dimension? A diameter of 25 mm. If it were a radius it would be written R25. Why is the margin on the left side wider? Because it is the filing side: that is where the punch holes or the folder binding go, and there can be no drawing there."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"National and international standards","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/normas-dibujo#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Instituto Argentino de Normalización (Argentine Standardization Institute). Manual de normas IRAM de dibujo técnico (in Spanish; IRAM technical drawing standards manual). IRAM. Formats, lines, title block, scales and dimensioning; the local reference. Frederick E. Giesecke et al. Technical Drawing with Engineering Graphics. 14th ed. (Spanish edition, “Dibujo técnico con gráficas de ingeniería”), Pearson, 2013. A good treatment of dimensioning and tolerancing, with a manufacturing mindset. Asociación Electrotécnica Argentina (Argentine Electrotechnical Association). Reglamentación AEA 90364 para la ejecución de instalaciones eléctricas en inmuebles (in Spanish; AEA 90364 regulations for the execution of electrical installations in buildings). AEA. What governs an electrical installation drawing in Argentina."},{"t":"Scarcity and choice","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#escasez","x":"01 Scarcity and choice Economics studies how societies use scarce resources to satisfy needs that have no limit. Since there is not enough for everything, we have to choose, and every choice has an opportunity cost: the value of the best alternative that is given up. An hour spent studying is an hour not worked; a plant that makes televisions does not make, with those machines, anything else. Every society answers three questions: what to produce and how much, how to produce it and for whom. Factor of production What it is Remuneration Land (natural resources) Soil, minerals, energy, water Rent Labor The physical and intellectual effort of people Wages Capital Produced goods that are used to produce: machines, buildings, instruments Interest Entrepreneurship Organizing the other factors and taking on the risk Profit"},{"t":"The production possibilities frontier","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#frontera","x":"02 The production possibilities frontier If an economy with given resources and technology produces only two goods, the production possibilities frontier gathers the maximum combinations it can reach. Interior points are inefficient —there are idle resources—; exterior ones are unattainable today. The slope is the opportunity cost, which grows as more of one good is produced because resources are not equally suited to everything: the frontier is concave. Economic growth, through more resources or better technology, shifts it outward. \\[ Y = B\\sqrt{1 - (X/A)^2} \\] \\[ CO = -\\frac{dY}{dX} = \\frac{B\\,X / A^2}{\\sqrt{1 - (X/A)^2}} \\] A simple model of a concave frontier: \\( A \\) is the maximum output of good X and \\( B \\), that of good Y. The opportunity cost \\( CO \\) is how many units of Y are given up for each additional unit of X. Lab · production possibilities frontier An economy that produces electronic units (X) and food (Y), in thousands of units and of tonnes, with a maximum of 100 thousand units. The yellow dot is on the frontier; the red line is its slope, the opportunity cost. An improvement in food production shifts the frontier (blue) relative to the original one (gray). Electronic units X = Maximum food output B ="},{"t":"Agents, circular flow and systems","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#agentes","x":"03 Agents, circular flow and systems Economic agents are households, which offer factors and consume; firms, which demand factors and produce; the government, which collects taxes, provides public goods and regulates; and the external sector. In the circular flow, households sell factors to firms and buy goods from them, and money circulates in the opposite direction. Depending on who answers the three questions, a system is a market system, when prices and private agents decide them; planned, when the State decides them; or mixed, like all current economies. Positive economics describes what is and can be tested against data; normative economics says what ought to be and includes value judgments. Microeconomics studies the decisions of agents and markets; macroeconomics, the aggregates: total output, employment, prices and foreign trade."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Opportunity cost Calculate the opportunity cost of pursuing the degree: tuition, materials and the income given up. Activity 2 · Circular flow Draw the circular flow including the government and the external sector, and place an Argentine electronics company in it. Activity 3 · Positive or normative Classify ten statements from economic news as positive or normative."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Counting only monetary costs. Opportunity cost includes what you stop earning. Believing that opportunity cost is constant. It grows as production becomes specialized. Confusing an interior point with an unattainable one. An interior point is possible but wastes resources. Presenting an opinion as a fact. Positive and normative statements must be distinguished."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment With the lab frontier, how much food can be produced along with 60 thousand units? 160 thousand tonnes. What is the opportunity cost of one more unit there? 1.5 tonnes of food. What does a point inside the frontier represent? A possible but inefficient combination: there are idle or poorly used resources. What is the remuneration of capital? Interest."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The subject matter of economics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/objeto-economia#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Paul A. Samuelson and William D. Nordhaus. Economics. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Economía”.) The classic textbook, with the production possibilities frontier and opportunity cost. N. Gregory Mankiw. Principles of Economics. Cengage. (Spanish edition: “Principios de economía”.) The ten principles of economics, with clear examples. Francisco Mochón and Víctor Beker. Economía: principios y aplicaciones. McGraw-Hill. (In Spanish.) An introductory text with examples from the Argentine economy."},{"t":"No coherence, no interference","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#coherencia","x":"01 No coherence, no interference Two waves always superpose, but for the interference pattern to be visible the phase relationship between them has to hold steady during the observation time. That property is coherence, and it is what distinguishes a laser from a lamp. Source Coherence length What it allows Incandescent lamp A few microns Almost nothing: the beam has to be split LED Tens of microns Very short-range interferometry Ordinary diode laser Centimeters to meters Laboratory interferometry Stabilized laser Hundreds of meters Dimensional metrology The coherence length is related to the spectral width: the “purer” the color, the longer it is. That is why white-light interferometry only works with paths that are almost exactly equal, and that limitation, which looks like a defect, is precisely what makes it useful for measuring absolute distances. \\[ L_c \\approx \\frac{\\lambda^2}{\\Delta\\lambda} \\] A 633 nm red laser with a 1 pm spectral width has a 40 cm coherence length; a lamp with a 300 nm width, barely one micron."},{"t":"The Michelson interferometer","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#michelson","x":"02 The Michelson interferometer A beam splitter divides the light in two; each half travels along an arm, bounces off a mirror and comes back. The two recombine and interfere. Since the path difference is twice the difference in arm length, moving a mirror by half a wavelength is enough to go from bright to dark. \\[ \\Delta = 2(d_1 - d_2) \\qquad\\qquad N = \\frac{2\\Delta d}{\\lambda} \\] \\( N \\) is the number of fringes that pass when a mirror is moved by \\( \\Delta d \\). With 633 nm light, each micron of displacement produces more than three fringes: counting fringes is measuring distances with nanometer resolution. Lab · Michelson interferometer You move the movable mirror and the fringes shift. The fringe at the center goes from bright to dark every quarter wavelength, because the path is traveled twice. helium-neon laser · 633 nm green laser · 532 nm blue laser · 450 nm red LED · 630 nm with 20 nm width white light Mirror displacement = 0 nm Mirror tilt = 4 The instrument that redefined the meter Between 1960 and 1983 the meter was defined as 1,650,763.73 wavelengths of a krypton-86 spectral line, and it was realized with an interferometer. Today the meter is defined from the speed of light, but the interferometer is still the practical standard: a coordinate measuring machine or a lithography system locates its axes by counting fringes, with a resolution of fractions of a nanometer."},{"t":"The experiment that found nothing","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#morley","x":"03 The experiment that found nothing Every known wave needed a medium: sound needed air, water waves needed water. Light was therefore assumed to propagate in a medium that filled the universe, the ether. If it existed, the Earth had to be moving through it at no less than 30 km/s, its orbital speed. In 1887 Michelson and Morley used the interferometer to detect that motion. The idea was simple: if there is an ether wind, light should take a different time depending on whether it travels along the wind or perpendicular to it, and when the apparatus was rotated the fringes should shift. \\[ \\frac{\\Delta t}{t} \\approx \\frac{v^2}{c^2} = \\left(\\frac{3\\times10^4}{3\\times10^8}\\right)^2 = 10^{-8} \\] An effect of one part in a hundred million, which their instrument could easily see: they expected a shift of 0.4 fringe and could detect 0.01. Nothing moved. They repeated the experiment at different times of the year, in case the Earth was momentarily at rest with respect to the ether, and still nothing moved. The most important null result in the history of physics: for almost twenty years people tried to explain it with auxiliary hypotheses —ether drag, the FitzGerald contraction— until in 1905 Einstein took the short road and postulated that the speed of light is the same for everyone, and that what needed revising were space and time. The null result as a result It is worth pausing on this, because it is a lesson in method. Michelson considered his experiment a failure: he had built"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring λ by counting fringes With a Michelson interferometer and a micrometer screw, count the fringes that pass when the mirror is displaced by a known distance. Solve for the laser wavelength and compare it with the stated one. Activity 2 · Coherence runs out Repeat the fringe alignment with a laser, an LED and white light, starting from equal arms. Measure how far each source can be unbalanced before the fringes disappear, and estimate the coherence length of each one. Activity 3 · The index of air Insert a vacuum cell in one arm and count the fringes that pass as it is slowly filled with air. Calculate the refractive index of air from that count."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the factor of two from the round-trip path. Moving the mirror by \\( \\lambda/4 \\) changes the path by \\( \\lambda/2 \\). Expecting fringes from two different sources. Without mutual coherence there is no stable pattern. Believing that white light does not interfere. It does, but only with almost equal paths. Taking the null result as a failure. A well-measured null result is a conclusive result. Confusing temporal coherence with spatial coherence. One depends on the spectral width; the other, on the size of the source. Assuming the ether was foolishness. It was the reasonable hypothesis given what was known; what is remarkable is that it was abandoned."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How far does the mirror have to move to change by one fringe? Half a wavelength to go from one maximum to the next, because the path is traveled twice. A quarter of \\( \\lambda \\) takes you from bright to dark. What is the coherence length? The maximum path difference that still produces fringes. It is approximately \\( \\lambda^2/\\Delta\\lambda \\). What was the Michelson-Morley experiment looking for? The Earth's motion relative to the ether, which should have produced a fringe shift when the apparatus was rotated. What did it find? Nothing. The null result forced, twenty years later, a revision of the notions of space and time. Why does white light give so few fringes? Because its enormous spectral width gives it a coherence length of only a few microns. Where is a Michelson interferometer used today? In dimensional metrology, in lithography equipment and, with arms kilometers long, in gravitational-wave detectors."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Wave, propagation and interference","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/onda-interferencia#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Eugene Hecht. Optics. 5th ed., Pearson, 2017 (Spanish edition, “Óptica”). Coherence, amplitude-division interferometers and the full treatment of the Michelson. Robert Eisberg and Robert Resnick. Quantum Physics of Atoms, Molecules, Solids, Nuclei, and Particles. 2nd ed., John Wiley & Sons, 1985 (Spanish edition, “Física cuántica: átomos, moléculas, sólidos, núcleos y partículas”, Limusa). The book that organizes this whole subject. Its first chapter tells the story of the crisis of classical physics. Albert A. Michelson and Edward W. Morley. On the Relative Motion of the Earth and the Luminiferous Ether. American Journal of Science, vol. 34, 1887. The original paper, surprisingly accessible. It is worth seeing how they report a null result."},{"t":"What an electromagnetic wave looks like","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#estructura","x":"01 What an electromagnetic wave looks like From Maxwell’s equations in vacuum comes a wave with a very rigid structure: the two fields are perpendicular to each other and both perpendicular to the direction of propagation. It is transverse, needs no medium and its two fields oscillate in phase. \\[ E = c\\,B \\qquad\\qquad \\vec{S} = \\frac{1}{\\mu_0}\\vec{E}\\times\\vec{B} \\qquad\\qquad I = \\langle S\\rangle = \\frac{E_0^2}{2Z_0} \\] The Poynting vector \\( \\vec{S} \\) gives the power per unit area and points in the direction of propagation. Its time average is the intensity, and \\( Z_0 = 377\\ \\Omega \\) is the impedance of vacuum. That \\( E = cB \\) explains why people almost always talk about the electric field and almost never about the magnetic one: in SI units the number for \\( B \\) is three hundred million times smaller, and on a charge the electric force dominates completely except at relativistic speeds. The wave pushes An electromagnetic wave also carries momentum, so when it strikes a surface it exerts radiation pressure: \\( P = I/c \\) if it is absorbed and double that if it is reflected. With sunlight in Earth orbit —1361 W/m²— that is about 4.5 micropascals, nothing for a person and enough to move a solar sail of square kilometers, or to slowly shift the orbit of a satellite if it is not compensated."},{"t":"What radiates and what does not","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#radiacion","x":"02 What radiates and what does not A stationary charge has a field but does not radiate. One moving at constant velocity does not either. An accelerated charge radiates, and from that comes all of antenna engineering: an alternating current in a conductor is charge accelerating back and forth. Zone Distance How the field falls off What happens Reactive near field Less than \\( \\lambda/2\\pi \\) As \\( 1/r^3 \\) The energy goes back and forth: nothing is radiated Far field More than \\( 2D^2/\\lambda \\) As \\( 1/r \\) The energy leaves and never comes back The first row is the one that explains why a 50 Hz installation does not radiate: with a wavelength of 6000 km, everything one could possibly wire is in the near field, and there the energy oscillates between the conductor and space without escaping. It is also the principle of wireless charging and of a transformer: near-field coupling, not radiation. \\[ \\mathrm{EIRP} = P_t\\,G_t \\qquad\\qquad P_r = \\frac{P_t G_t G_r \\lambda^2}{(4\\pi d)^2} \\] The Friis equation. The received power falls with the square of the distance —that is geometry, not absorption— and also with the square of the frequency, because at higher frequency the receiving antenna has less effective area for the same gain. Lab · link budget A transmitter, two antennas and a distance. The free-space loss, the field at the receiver and how much margin is left over its sensitivity are calculated. Try changing the frequency without touching anything else. LoRa 433 MHz · telem"},{"t":"Energy and photons","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#espectro","x":"03 Energy and photons The wave description is enough for almost everything in this unit, but when the frequency rises a lot the other side appears: energy travels in packets. \\[ E = h f = \\frac{hc}{\\lambda} \\qquad h = 6.626\\times10^{-34}\\ \\mathrm{J\\,s} \\qquad E[\\mathrm{eV}] = \\frac{1240}{\\lambda[\\mathrm{nm}]} \\] The last form is the one worth keeping in your head: a 1240 nm photon has 1 eV. From there follows directly the color of an LED according to its band gap and the wavelength limit that a photodiode can detect. Material \\( E_g \\) LED \\( \\lambda \\) Color GaAs 1.42 eV 873 nm Infrared: remote controls GaAsP 1.9 eV 653 nm Red GaP 2.2 eV 564 nm Green InGaN 2.7 eV 459 nm Blue The table is the unit on chemical bonds seen from the viewpoint of optics: the color of an LED is its band gap. And silicon, at 1.12 eV, emits at 1107 nm, in the infrared, but it also has an indirect band gap and so is a terrible emitter: other compounds are used to make light. As a detector, on the other hand, it works very well up to those 1100 nm, and that is the reason a silicon photodiode sees nothing beyond."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying the inverse-square law With a low-power transmitter and a field-strength meter, record the received signal at increasing distances in an open space. Plot in dB against the logarithm of the distance and verify the slope of 20 dB per decade. Activity 2 · Gain of an antenna Compare the signal received with a whip antenna and with a pointed directional one, at the same distance. Calculate the difference in dB and compare it with the stated gain. Activity 3 · The spectrum of an LED With a simple spectrometer, measure the emission wavelength of LEDs of different colors. Calculate the photon energy and compare it with the tabulated band gap of the semiconductor of each one."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that the magnetic field of a wave is negligible. It is not: it is \\( E/c \\), small in number and essential to the phenomenon. Thinking that free-space loss is absorption. It is geometry: the same power spread over a larger sphere. Assuming that an antenna amplifies. Gain is directivity: it concentrates what there is, it does not add power. Measuring a radiated field in the near field. There \\( 1/r \\) does not hold and the measurement does not mean what it seems to. Forgetting that the Friis equation gets worse with frequency. At equal gain, 5 GHz reaches less far than 2.4. Confusing transmitter power with EIRP. The latter already includes the gain of the antenna."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the relation between \\( E \\) and \\( B \\) in a wave? \\( E = cB \\), and the two oscillate in phase, perpendicular to each other and to the propagation. What radiates and what does not? An accelerated charge radiates. A stationary one, or one at constant velocity, does not. What is the impedance of vacuum? 377 ohms. It relates the electric field to the magnetic one in a plane wave. Why does a 50 Hz installation not radiate? Because with a wavelength of 6000 km everything is in the near field, where the energy goes back and forth without escaping. What energy does a 620 nm photon have? Two electronvolts: \\( 1240/620 \\). Why does a silicon photodiode not see beyond 1100 nm? Because there the photon no longer has the energy of its 1.12 eV band gap and does not generate an electron-hole pair."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Electromagnetic waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-electromagneticas#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. Electromagnetic waves, the Poynting vector, radiation pressure and the spectrum. John D. Kraus and Ronald J. Marhefka. Antennas. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 2001. From the accelerated charge to the real antenna, with field zones, gain and radiation patterns. Eugene Hecht. Optics. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Óptica”), Pearson, 2017. The treatment of the electromagnetic wave from the viewpoint of optics, which is the continuation of the last three units."},{"t":"What a wave is","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#que","x":"01 What a wave is A wave carries energy and momentum without carrying matter. Each particle of the medium oscillates about its equilibrium position, and what travels is the disturbance. Transverse: the particles oscillate perpendicular to the direction of propagation. A string, an electromagnetic wave. Longitudinal: they oscillate in the same direction in which the wave advances. Sound. The fundamental relation \\[ v = \\lambda \\cdot f \\] The speed is set by the medium; the frequency, by the source. The wavelength is what results from the two. That is why, on passing from one medium to another, λ changes but f never does. Medium Speed What it depends on Stretched string \\( v = \\sqrt{T/\\mu } \\) Tension and mass per unit length Air (sound, 20 °C) 343 m/s Temperature, not pressure Water \\( \\approx 1500 \\dfrac{m}{s} \\) Compressibility and density Coaxial cable \\( \\approx 0.66\\cdot c \\) Permittivity of the dielectric"},{"t":"The wave equation","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#ecuacion","x":"02 The wave equation A harmonic wave traveling to the right is written: \\[ y (x, t) = A \\, \\operatorname{sin} (k x -\\omega t), \\quad k = \\frac{2 \\pi}{\\lambda}, \\quad \\omega = 2 \\pi f \\] \\(k\\) is the wavenumber and \\(\\omega\\) the angular frequency. Their quotient, \\(\\omega/k\\), is the propagation speed. And all of them are solutions of one and the same partial differential equation: \\[ \\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial x^2} = \\frac{1}{v^2} \\frac{\\partial^2 y}{\\partial t^2} \\] The same equation that comes out of combining Maxwell’s equations, and the same one as for transmission lines. That coincidence is no accident, and it has an enormous practical consequence: everything learned about a string holds for a cable. Reflection, impedance, standing waves and matching are the same phenomenon with different units."},{"t":"Superposition and standing waves","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#estacionarias","x":"03 Superposition and standing waves When two waves coincide at a point, the displacements add. If two equal waves travel in opposite directions —which happens naturally when one is reflected at an end— the result no longer advances: it is a standing wave, with points that never move, the nodes, and points of maximum oscillation, the antinodes. \\[ f_n = \\frac{n v}{2 L}, \\quad \\lambda_n = \\frac{2 L}{n} \\] For a string with both ends fixed. Only those frequencies survive: they are the normal modes. The lowest frequency, with \\(n = 1\\), is the fundamental; the others are its harmonics, and the mix of their intensities is what gives each instrument its timbre. Lab · modes of a string with fixed ends Choose the harmonic and move through time. With the checkbox you can see the two traveling waves that, when added, produce the standing wave. Harmonic n = 3 Length of the string 1.20 m Propagation speed 120 m/s Time, as a fraction of the period 0.00 Show the two traveling waves that add up"},{"t":"Reflection and interference","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#reflexion","x":"04 Reflection and interference End What happens Electrical equivalent Fixed The wave returns inverted Short-circuited line Free It returns without inversion Open-circuited line Matched Nothing is reflected: all the energy is absorbed Load equal to the characteristic impedance That third case is the one always sought in radio frequency, and the whole Smith chart exists to achieve it. The standing wave ratio of a poorly matched line is, exactly, the same pattern of nodes and antinodes as on the string. Interference and beats Two waves of the same frequency reinforce each other if they arrive in phase and cancel if they arrive in antiphase: hence the sound cancellations in a room and the nulls of a radiation pattern. If the frequencies differ slightly, the amplitude pulses at the difference frequency: that is the beat, and it is the principle used to tune an instrument and the one used by a superheterodyne receiver to shift a signal in frequency."},{"t":"Sound and the Doppler effect","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#sonido","x":"05 Sound and the Doppler effect Sound is a longitudinal pressure wave. Its intensity is measured on a logarithmic scale because the ear responds that way: \\[ \\beta = 10 \\, \\log \\frac{I}{I_0} \\; [ \\mathrm{dB} ], \\quad I_0 = {10}^{-12} \\, W / m^2 \\] The decibel of acoustics and that of electronics are the same definition: a ratio of powers on a logarithmic scale. When the source or the receiver moves, the perceived frequency changes: this is the Doppler effect, \\(f' = f \\cdot (v \\pm v_{\\text{obs}})/(v \\mp v_{\\text{source}})\\). Speed radar, the ultrasonic flowmeter and Doppler ultrasound imaging all work on it."},{"t":"The same thing, in electronics","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#electronica","x":"06 The same thing, in electronics Transmission lines. A cable that is long compared with λ does not behave like a conductor but like a medium that carries waves: there is reflection, standing waves and matching. Signal integrity. On a fast digital board, an unterminated trace reflects the edge and produces steps and overshoot: it is a string with a free end. Antennas. A half-wave antenna is, literally, a standing wave of current on a conductor. Ultrasound. Distance sensors measure the time of flight of an acoustic pulse; flow sensors use the Doppler effect. Crystals and SAW filters. They work with surface acoustic waves in a solid."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Modes of a string Stretch a thread between a vibrator and a pulley with weights. Vary the frequency until each mode appears and verify that \\(f_n = n \\cdot v/(2L)\\). Change the tension and check that \\(v = \\sqrt{T/\\mu}\\). Activity 2 · Speed of sound with a tube With a tube partially submerged in water and a tuning fork, look for the resonance positions. The distance between two of them is λ/2, and with the fork’s f you obtain the speed of sound. Activity 3 · Reflection in a coaxial cable Send a pulse down a few meters of coax with the end open, short-circuited and terminated in 50 Ω, and look at the reflection on the oscilloscope. It is the activity that closes the circle: the three rows of the reflection table, measured."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Believing the wave carries matter. It carries energy; the medium only oscillates. Thinking the frequency changes when the medium changes. λ changes, because v changes. Confusing the propagation speed with the speed of a particle of the medium. They are different things and of very different orders of magnitude. Believing sound travels faster if there is more pressure. In an ideal gas it depends on the temperature, not on the pressure. Adding intensities in decibels. Decibels are not added: powers are added. Miscounting the nodes: mode n has n antinodes and n+1 nodes including the ends."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment A 1.2 m string with \\(v = 120\\text{ m/s}\\). What is its fundamental frequency? \\(\\lambda_1 = 2L = 2.4\\text{ m}\\), so \\(f_1 = 120/2.4 =\\) \\(50\\text{ Hz}\\). The third harmonic is at 150 Hz. What is \\(\\lambda\\) for a 100 MHz signal in a coax with a velocity factor of 0.66? \\(v = 0.66 \\cdot 3 \\cdot 10^8 \\approx 2 \\cdot 10^8\\text{ m/s}\\), so \\(\\lambda = 2 \\cdot 10^8/10^8 =\\) \\(2\\text{ m}\\). A quarter wave is 50 cm. What happens to a wave when it is reflected at a fixed end? It returns inverted, with a 180° phase shift. It is the case of the short-circuited line. Two sounds of 440 and 443 Hz are played together. What is heard? A tone of about 441.5 Hz whose intensity pulses 3 times per second: the beating used to tune an instrument. If the power of a signal doubles, by how many decibels does it rise? \\(10 \\cdot \\log(2) \\approx\\) \\(3\\text{ dB}\\), in both acoustics and electronics. Why does a half-wave antenna resonate? Because its open ends impose current nodes, and with \\(L = \\lambda/2\\) the reflected wave returns in phase: a standing wave is established, just as on the string."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Mechanical waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-mecanicas#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading A. P. French. Vibrations and Waves. W. W. Norton, 1971 (Spanish edition, “Vibraciones y ondas”, Reverté, 1974). The best book on the subject for an engineer: waves, modes and superposition from scratch. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 1. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapters 16 to 18: mechanical waves, sound, superposition and standing waves. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. Chapter 2 shows that the transmission line is this same wave equation. Paul A. Tipler and Gene Mosca. Physics for Scientists and Engineers, Volume 1. 6th ed., W. H. Freeman, 2008 (Spanish edition, “Física para la ciencia y la tecnología”, Reverté, 2010). Good development of the Doppler effect and of the decibel scale."},{"t":"From Maxwell to the wave equation","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#helmholtz","x":"01 From Maxwell to the wave equation Combining the two curl equations of the previous unit in a source-free region, the electric field is governed by a single equation, the Helmholtz equation: \\[ \\nabla^2 \\mathbf{E} - \\gamma^2 \\mathbf{E} = 0 \\] \\[ \\gamma = \\alpha + j\\beta = \\sqrt{j\\omega\\mu\\,(\\sigma + j\\omega\\varepsilon)} \\] \\( \\gamma \\) is the propagation constant: its real part \\( \\alpha \\) is the attenuation, in nepers per meter, and its imaginary part \\( \\beta \\), the phase constant, in radians per meter. The simplest solution is the plane wave: a uniform field in every plane perpendicular to the direction of propagation, advancing as \\( e^{-\\gamma z} = e^{-\\alpha z} e^{-j\\beta z} \\). It is the model for the wave from a distant antenna: many wavelengths away from the source, any spherical wave looks locally flat."},{"t":"The wave in a lossless medium","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#sin-perdidas","x":"02 The wave in a lossless medium With \\( \\sigma = 0 \\), the wave is not attenuated and its parameters come directly from \\( \\mu \\) and \\( \\varepsilon \\): \\[ \\beta = \\omega\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon} \\] \\[ v_p = \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{\\mu\\varepsilon}} = \\frac{c}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}} \\] \\[ \\eta = \\sqrt{\\frac{\\mu}{\\varepsilon}} = \\frac{377\\ \\Omega}{\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r}} \\] \\( \\eta \\) is the intrinsic impedance of the medium: the ratio between the electric and the magnetic field of the wave. In vacuum it is 376.73 Ω. E and H are perpendicular to each other and to the direction of propagation, and are in phase. The power density is \\( |E|^2 / 2\\eta \\): with an amplitude of 1 V/m in air, 1.33 mW per square meter. The direction in which the electric field oscillates is the polarization of the wave."},{"t":"The wave in a lossy medium","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#con-perdidas","x":"03 The wave in a lossy medium With conductivity, \\( \\gamma \\) and \\( \\eta \\) become complex. The two limiting cases are the useful ones: Low-loss dielectric, \\( \\sigma \\ll \\omega\\varepsilon \\) Good conductor, \\( \\sigma \\gg \\omega\\varepsilon \\) Attenuation \\( \\alpha \\approx \\dfrac{\\sigma}{2}\\sqrt{\\mu/\\varepsilon} \\) \\( \\alpha \\approx \\beta \\approx 1/\\delta \\) Impedance Almost real, \\( \\approx \\sqrt{\\mu/\\varepsilon} \\) \\( (1 + j)/\\sigma\\delta \\), very small, at 45° Wavelength That of the lossless dielectric \\( 2\\pi\\delta \\): the wave vanishes within one wavelength Attenuation is expressed in decibels by multiplying by 8.686: one neper is 8.686 dB. In seawater at 1 kHz, \\( \\alpha = 0.126 \\) Np/m, about 1.1 dB per meter; at 1 GHz, 673 dB per meter."},{"t":"Reflection at normal incidence","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#reflexion","x":"04 Reflection at normal incidence When a wave arrives perpendicular to the boundary between two media, part of it is reflected and part is transmitted. The boundary conditions give the coefficients for the electric field: \\[ \\Gamma = \\frac{\\eta_2 - \\eta_1}{\\eta_2 + \\eta_1} \\] \\[ \\tau = 1 + \\Gamma \\] The fraction of power reflected is \\( |\\Gamma|^2 \\). From air to glass (\\( \\varepsilon_r = 2.25 \\)), \\( \\Gamma = -0.2 \\) and 4% is reflected; from air to water, 64%; from air to a metal, practically all of it. The incident and the reflected waves superpose and form a standing wave: fixed maxima and minima separated by half a wavelength. It is exactly the phenomenon that appears in badly terminated transmission lines, with the load impedance in the role of \\( \\eta_2 \\). Lab · a plane wave entering a medium A plane wave travels through air and strikes a medium perpendicularly. The attenuation, wavelength, impedance and reflection are calculated with the exact complex expressions, and the electric field inside the medium is plotted as a function of depth. seawater (σ = 4 S/m, εr = 81) fresh water (σ = 0.01 S/m, εr = 80) moist soil (σ = 0.01 S/m, εr = 15) dry soil (σ = 0.001 S/m, εr = 4) glass (lossless, εr = 2.25) Frequency ="},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case The phenomenon Radome of an antenna A thickness of half a wavelength in the material so that the reflection cancels Ground-penetrating radar The attenuation of the ground limits the depth; low frequencies are used to see deeper Microwave oven The water in food absorbs at 2.45 GHz and heats up Radio reception inside a building Reflection and attenuation by walls and glass Absorbing materials of an anechoic chamber An impedance that grows gradually up to \\( \\eta_0 \\) so as not to reflect"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Standing waves in air With a 10 GHz transmitter aimed at a metal plate and a detector that moves between them, locate the minima of the standing wave and measure the wavelength. Activity 2 · Attenuation by water Measure the signal of a 2.4 GHz link with water containers of different thickness between the antennas and estimate the attenuation per centimeter. Activity 3 · The phone in the cage Wrap a phone in aluminum foil and check that it loses signal. Then try with a mesh with large holes and relate the result to the wavelength."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Using \\( c/\\sqrt{\\varepsilon_r} \\) in a medium with large losses. The velocity and the wavelength change with the conductivity. Confusing nepers with decibels. \\( 1\\ \\text{Np} = 8.686\\ \\text{dB} \\). Forgetting that \\( \\Gamma \\) is for the field. The reflected power is \\( |\\Gamma|^2 \\). Assuming that a good conductor absorbs the wave. Almost all of it is reflected; the little that enters is attenuated within a few \\( \\delta \\)."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What intrinsic impedance does a lossless dielectric with \\( \\varepsilon_r = 4 \\) have? \\( 377/2 \\approx 188 \\) Ω. What fraction of the power is reflected from air to a 188 Ω medium? \\( \\Gamma = (188 - 377)/(188 + 377) \\approx -0.33 \\); 11% is reflected. How many decibels is 0.5 Np? \\( 0.5 \\times 8.686 \\approx 4.3 \\) dB. Why does ground-penetrating radar use low frequencies? Because the attenuation of the ground grows with frequency, and at low frequencies the wave reaches deeper."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Wave equations and plane waves","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/ondas-planas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading David K. Cheng. Field and Wave Electromagnetics. 2nd ed., Addison-Wesley, 1989. Plane waves in lossy media and reflection, with the notation of this page. Fawwaz T. Ulaby and Umberto Ravaioli. Fundamentals of Applied Electromagnetics. 7th ed., Pearson, 2015. Very graphic, with engineering applications in every chapter. Constantine A. Balanis. Advanced Engineering Electromagnetics. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2012. The complete treatment, for going deeper into media and oblique reflection."},{"t":"When light can be treated as rays","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#rayos","x":"01 When light can be treated as rays Light is an electromagnetic wave, but when the objects it meets are much larger than its wavelength —half a micrometer for visible light— it behaves as if it traveled in straight-line rays. That approximation is geometrical optics, and it is used to design lenses, mirrors, cameras and fibers. When that condition is not met, diffraction and interference appear, which already belong to physical optics. The limit is not arbitrary: it is what sets the maximum resolution of a microscope and the minimum size of a transistor made by photolithography. Refractive index \\[ n = \\frac{c}{v} \\] It is how many times slower light travels in that medium than in vacuum. Air 1.0003; water 1.33; ordinary glass 1.5; diamond 2.42. Always n ≥ 1."},{"t":"Reflection and refraction","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#reflexion","x":"02 Reflection and refraction Law Statement Reflection The angle of incidence equals the angle of reflection, measured from the normal Refraction (Snell) \\( n_{1}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin} \\theta _{1} = n_{2}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin} \\theta _{2} \\) On entering a denser medium light bends toward the normal; on leaving into a less dense one, it bends away. And if the angle of incidence exceeds a certain value, the light no longer exits: it is all reflected. That is the critical angle: \\[ \\operatorname{sin} \\, \\theta_c = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} \\quad (n_1 \\gt n_2) \\] For glass and air it comes to about 41°: above that, total internal reflection. Optical fiber A fiber is a glass core with an index only slightly higher than that of the cladding around it. Every ray that enters within the acceptance cone strikes the wall above the critical angle and is totally reflected, again and again, kilometer after kilometer, with almost no loss of energy. This is what makes fiber transmission possible."},{"t":"Mirrors and thin lenses","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#lentes","x":"03 Mirrors and thin lenses A thin lens or a spherical mirror is described by a single equation and a single definition of magnification: \\[ \\frac{1}{f} = \\frac{1}{s'} + \\frac{1}{s} \\quad m = -\\frac{s'}{s} \\] s is the object distance, s′ the image distance and f the focal length. The sign of m tells whether the image is upright or inverted. Three rays are enough to construct the image of any point, and two already suffice: The one that arrives parallel to the axis leaves passing through the focal point. The one that passes through the center of the lens is not deflected. The one that passes through the object focal point leaves parallel to the axis. Lab · ray tracing in a thin lens Move the object and see how the image changes. The three rays are drawn automatically and the equation is solved alongside. Converging lens Diverging lens Focal length 6.0 cm Object distance 15.0 cm Object height 3.0 cm"},{"t":"What image results in each case","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#imagenes","x":"04 What image results in each case Object at Image Application Beyond 2f Real, inverted and smaller Camera At 2f Real, inverted and the same size 1:1 copier Between f and 2f Real, inverted and larger Projector At the focal point None forms: the rays leave parallel Collimator, headlight Closer than f Virtual, upright and larger Magnifying glass Diverging lens Always virtual, upright and smaller Myopia correction, viewfinder Real and virtual A real image forms where the light actually converges: it can be captured on a screen or a sensor. A virtual image is located where the rays appear to come from when extended backward; it can be seen with the eye, but it cannot be projected. That is why a magnifying glass does not work as a projector. The power of a lens is measured in diopters, \\(D = 1/f\\) with \\(f\\) in meters. An eyeglass prescription of −2.5 diopters is a diverging lens with a 40 cm focal length."},{"t":"Instruments and aberrations","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#instrumentos","x":"05 Instruments and aberrations Camera. A lens forms the real image on the sensor; focusing means moving the lens so that s′ falls exactly on it. The f-number —the relative aperture— controls how much light enters and the depth of field. Magnifier and microscope. They increase the angle under which an object is seen. Telescope. An objective of long focal length and an eyepiece of short focal length: the magnification is their ratio. The eye. A lens of variable focal length in front of a fixed screen, the retina. Myopia is an eye that is too long, and it is corrected with a diverging lens. No real lens is perfect. Spherical aberration makes rays far from the axis focus at a different point; chromatic aberration makes each color focus differently because n depends on λ; and distortion bends the straight lines at the edge. Good lenses combine several elements of different glasses to compensate for them, and the rest is corrected by digital image processing."},{"t":"Optics in electronics","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#electronica","x":"06 Optics in electronics Image sensors. A CMOS sensor needs a lens that forms a real image on it: without geometrical optics there is no camera. Optical fiber. Numerical aperture, acceptance cone and modal dispersion all follow from Snell’s law. Optical sensors. Photoelectric gates, encoders, barcode readers and proximity sensors. Photolithography. Integrated circuits are made by projecting a mask onto a wafer: the minimum size is set by the wavelength used. Displays and projectors. Lenses, mirrors and diffusers calculated with these same rules. Lidar and time-of-flight measurement. A laser pulse, receiving optics and a fast photodiode."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#laboratorio","x":"07 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the focal length of a lens Focus the image of a distant object onto a screen: the lens-to-screen distance is f. Then repeat with the object at several distances and verify \\(1/f = 1/s + 1/s'\\) with a table of values. Activity 2 · Critical angle with a laser pointer Shine a laser from inside a container of water onto the surface, varying the angle until the refracted ray disappears. Compare the measured angle with \\(\\arcsin(1/1.33) = 48.8°\\). Activity 3 · Optical gate and encoder Build a gate with an infrared LED and a phototransistor, and use a slotted disk to count revolutions with a microcontroller. It is the most direct application of rectilinear propagation."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#errores","x":"08 Common mistakes Measuring angles from the surface instead of from the normal. Forgetting the sign convention and mixing real images with virtual ones. Believing that total reflection occurs when passing into a denser medium. It is the other way around: from the denser to the less dense. Confusing magnification with power. Magnification has no units; power is measured in diopters. Thinking that covering half a lens cuts half the image. The whole image is formed, but with half the light. Using geometrical optics where it does not apply, for example with apertures of the order of the wavelength."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#autoevaluacion","x":"09 Self-assessment An object is 15 cm from a converging lens with \\(f = 6\\text{ cm}\\). Where does the image form? \\(1/s' = 1/6 - 1/15 = 0.1\\), so \\(s' =\\) \\(10\\text{ cm}\\), with \\(m = -10/15 = -0.67\\): real, inverted and smaller. What is the critical angle between glass (\\(n = 1.5\\)) and air? \\(\\theta_c = \\arcsin(1/1.5) \\approx\\) \\(41.8°\\). What focal length does a −2.5 diopter lens have? \\(f = 1/(-2.5) = -0.4\\text{ m}\\): a 40 cm diverging lens. Why does a magnifying glass form a virtual image? Because the object is closer than the focal length: the rays leave diverging and the image is seen on the same side as the object, upright and enlarged. Light passes from air into water with a 30° angle of incidence. What is the refracted angle? \\(\\sin\\theta_2 = \\sin 30/1.33 = 0.376\\), so \\(\\theta_2 \\approx\\) \\(22.1°\\): it bends toward the normal. Why does an optical fiber not lose light through its walls? Because the rays strike above the critical angle between core and cladding, and total internal reflection returns them without any loss by refraction."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Geometrical optics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optica-geometrica#ampliar","x":"10 Further reading Eugene Hecht. Optics. 5th ed., Pearson, 2017 (Spanish edition, “Óptica”). The reference text; chapters 4 and 5 cover all of geometrical optics. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics, Volume 2. 9th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage Learning, 2014. Chapters 35 and 36, at the level of the course and with a well-explained sign convention. Gerd Keiser. Optical Fiber Communications. 5th ed., McGraw-Hill, 2015. From Snell to real fiber links: the bridge to telecommunications. Richard P. Feynman. The Feynman Lectures on Physics, Volume I. Addison-Wesley, 1963 (Spanish edition, “Física, volumen I”, Addison-Wesley / Pearson). Chapter 26 derives reflection and refraction from Fermat’s principle of least time."},{"t":"Photons and bands","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#luz","x":"01 Photons and bands A photon with energy greater than the band gap can be absorbed and create an electron-hole pair; a pair that recombines can emit a photon with the energy of the band gap. All of optoelectronics comes from those two sentences and from the relationship between energy and wavelength: \\[ E = \\frac{h\\,c}{\\lambda} \\qquad \\lambda\\,[\\text{nm}] \\approx \\frac{1240}{E\\,[\\text{eV}]} \\] Silicon, at 1.12 eV, absorbs up to about 1100 nm; GaAs, at 1.42 eV, emits at 870 nm; GaN, at 3.4 eV, in the near ultraviolet. The difference between absorbing and emitting lies in the type of band gap. In direct band gap semiconductors (GaAs, GaN, InP) the electron can fall into the hole emitting just a photon, and it does so in nanoseconds. In indirect ones, such as silicon, the transition also needs a lattice vibration, it is very improbable, and the electron almost always recombines through defects without emitting light, the mechanism of the transport unit. That is why silicon is good for detecting light and for solar cells, but not for making LEDs or lasers."},{"t":"The photodiode","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#fotodiodo","x":"02 The photodiode The pairs that light generates inside the depletion region of a PN junction, or within one diffusion length of it, are separated by the field and produce a current in the reverse direction proportional to the optical power. The constant is the responsivity: \\[ I_{ph} = \\mathcal{R}\\, P_{\\text{optical}} \\qquad \\mathcal{R} = \\frac{\\eta\\, q\\, \\lambda}{h\\, c} \\approx \\frac{\\eta\\, \\lambda\\,[\\text{nm}]}{1240} \\;\\text{A/W} \\] \\( \\eta \\) is the quantum efficiency: the fraction of photons that produce an electron in the circuit. A silicon photodiode reaches about 0.6 A/W near 900 nm. Mode or type How it is used Advantage Photovoltaic, unbiased Into a transimpedance amplifier, with zero voltage No dark current: the lowest noise, for measuring weak light Photoconductive, reverse biased With reverse voltage, which widens the depletion region Less capacitance and more speed PIN A wide intrinsic layer between the P and the N Almost all absorption occurs in a region with field: fast and efficient Avalanche (APD) Near breakdown, with internal multiplication Gain of tens to hundreds, for very weak signals"},{"t":"The solar cell","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#solar","x":"03 The solar cell A solar cell is a large photodiode that works in the fourth quadrant: light generates current and the junction, forward biased, delivers power to the load. Its curve is that of the diode shifted downward by the photocurrent: \\[ I = I_{ph} - I_S \\left( e^{(V + I R_s)/n V_T} - 1 \\right) \\] \\[ V_{oc} = n V_T \\ln \\left( \\frac{I_{ph}}{I_S} + 1 \\right) \\qquad FF = \\frac{P_{\\max}}{V_{oc}\\, I_{sc}} \\] \\( I_{sc} \\approx I_{ph} \\) is proportional to the irradiance; \\( V_{oc} \\) grows only with its logarithm. \\( R_s \\) is the series resistance of the contacts and the material. The efficiency of a single-junction cell has a theoretical ceiling, the Shockley–Queisser limit: photons with less energy than the band gap are not absorbed, and those with more lose the excess as heat. The optimum, near 1.34 eV, gives 33.7 %; for silicon the limit is about 29 %, and the best laboratory cells exceed 26 %. Multijunction cells with stacked layers of different band gaps pass 40 % and are used on satellites. Lab · the curve of a silicon cell A 156 × 156 mm silicon cell with 9 A short-circuit current and 0.64 V open-circuit voltage at 1000 W/m² and 25 °C, ideality factor 1.2 and 4 mΩ series resistance. You choose the irradiance and the cell temperature: the current and the power are plotted against the voltage, with the maximum power point, which is where a good controller makes it work. Irradiance = Cell temperature ="},{"t":"The LED","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#led","x":"04 The LED In an LED the junction is forward biased, carriers are injected on each side and, in a direct band gap material, a good part of the recombinations emit light with the energy of the band gap. That is why the color is set by the material, and the forward voltage is approximately the band gap divided by the electron charge: Color Material Wavelength Typical forward voltage Infrared GaAs, AlGaAs 850 to 940 nm 1.2 to 1.5 V Red and amber AlGaInP 590 to 630 nm 1.8 to 2.2 V Green InGaN 520 to 530 nm 2.8 to 3.4 V Blue InGaN 450 to 470 nm 2.8 to 3.4 V White Blue LED with a yellow phosphor Broad spectrum 2.8 to 3.4 V The gallium nitride blue LED, which made the white LED possible and with it solid-state lighting, earned the 2014 Nobel Prize in Physics for Akasaki, Amano and Nakamura. Like a diode, the LED is driven by current: its exponential curve means that a fixed voltage gives a very uncertain current, and that is why it always has a series resistor or a current source."},{"t":"The semiconductor laser","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#laser","x":"05 The semiconductor laser If so much current is injected that there are more electrons up than down in the junction (population inversion), a passing photon stimulates the emission of another identical one, in phase and in the same direction. With two mirrored faces forming a cavity, the light goes back and forth being amplified, and above a threshold current the emission becomes coherent and very narrow in wavelength. Below the threshold, the laser is a mediocre LED. Semiconductor lasers are found in optical drives, in printers and, above all, in fiber-optic communications, which use the three windows where the fiber loses little: 850, 1310 and 1550 nm. VCSELs, which emit through the surface of the wafer, light up the short links in data centers and the face sensors of phones."},{"t":"The optocoupler","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#optoacoplador","x":"06 The optocoupler An infrared LED and a phototransistor facing each other inside an opaque package transmit a signal with no electrical connection: the isolation is thousands of volts. The parameter that defines it is the current transfer ratio (CTR), the output current over the input current: in the PC817 it ranges from 50 to 600 % depending on the class. It separates the control side from the power side in switching power supplies, in motor drives and in PLC inputs."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#electronica","x":"07 Where it appears in electronics Case Device Photovoltaic installation Silicon cell panels and an inverter with maximum power point tracking Remote control and presence sensor Infrared LED and a photodiode with a filter Fiber-optic link Laser, fiber and a PIN or avalanche photodiode Isolated switching power supply Optocoupler in the feedback loop Digital camera Millions of photodiodes in a CMOS sensor Pulse oximeter Red and infrared LEDs and a photodiode that compares how much the blood absorbs"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#laboratorio","x":"08 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring Planck's constant with LEDs Measure the voltage at which LEDs of four colors begin to conduct and plot it against \\( 1/\\lambda \\). The slope is \\( h c / q \\): solve for \\( h \\). Activity 2 · Solar cell curve With a cell or a small panel in the sun, measure voltage and current across a variable load from short circuit to open circuit. Plot the curve and the power, locate the maximum power point and calculate the fill factor. Activity 3 · The CTR of an optocoupler With a PC817, measure the collector current for LED currents from 1 to 20 mA with 5 V at the output, and plot the CTR. Verify the class against the datasheet."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#errores","x":"09 Common mistakes Connecting an LED directly to a voltage. Without limiting the current, it is destroyed. Expecting light from a silicon junction. Its indirect band gap hardly emits at all. Making a solar cell work at an arbitrary fixed voltage. Power is lost; the maximum power point has to be tracked. Forgetting that the cell loses output with heat. The open-circuit voltage drops about 2 mV per degree. Reading responsivity as efficiency. 0.6 A/W is not 60 %: it depends on the wavelength."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#autoevaluacion","x":"10 Self-assessment Up to what wavelength does a 1.42 eV material absorb? \\( 1240 / 1.42 \\approx 873 \\) nm. Why are silicon LEDs not made? Because its band gap is indirect: recombination with emission of a photon is very improbable and the carriers recombine through defects, without emitting light. A photodiode with \\( \\eta = 0.8 \\) at 850 nm, what responsivity does it have? \\( 0.8 \\times 850 / 1240 \\approx 0.55 \\) A/W. If the irradiance drops by half, what happens to \\( I_{sc} \\) and \\( V_{oc} \\)? \\( I_{sc} \\) drops by half; \\( V_{oc} \\) drops only \\( n V_T \\ln 2 \\), about 20 mV. What does the CTR of an optocoupler measure? The ratio between the output current of the phototransistor and the LED current."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Photonics and optoelectronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/optoelectronica#ampliar","x":"11 Further reading E. Fred Schubert. Light-Emitting Diodes. 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2006. The reference on the LED: materials, efficiency, color and packaging. Jenny Nelson. The Physics of Solar Cells. Imperial College Press, 2003. The solar cell from the physics of the junction, with the Shockley–Queisser limit explained. Bahaa E. A. Saleh and Malvin Carl Teich. Fundamentals of Photonics. 3rd ed., Wiley, 2019. Photodetectors, semiconductor lasers and fibers in a single text, in great detail."},{"t":"The oscillation condition at radio frequency","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#condicion","x":"01 The oscillation condition at radio frequency An oscillator is a feedback amplifier that deliberately satisfies the Barkhausen criterion: loop gain of magnitude 1 and zero phase at a single frequency. At low frequency the network that selects the frequency is RC, like the Wien bridge in stability and compensation. At radio frequency, LC resonant circuits or crystals are used, which have a much higher quality factor: their phase changes very fast near resonance, and that fixes the frequency precisely and reduces noise. To start up, the loop gain has to be somewhat greater than 1; the amplitude is then limited by the nonlinearity of the transistor."},{"t":"LC oscillators","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#lc","x":"02 LC oscillators Oscillator Feedback network Frequency Colpitts Capacitive divider \\( C_1, C_2 \\) with an inductor \\( 1/\\big(2\\pi\\sqrt{L\\,C_1C_2/(C_1+C_2)}\\big) \\) Hartley Tapped inductor, or two inductors, with a capacitor \\( 1/\\big(2\\pi\\sqrt{(L_1+L_2)C}\\big) \\) Clapp Colpitts with a small capacitor in series with the inductor Set almost entirely by the series capacitor, less sensitive to the transistor In the Colpitts, the capacitive divider feeds back a fraction \\( C_1/C_2 \\) of the output; with a bipolar transistor, the start-up condition calls for approximately \\( g_m R \\ge C_2/C_1 \\), where \\( R \\) is the load resistance of the tank. With \\( L \\) = 1 μH and two 100 pF capacitors, it oscillates at 22.5 MHz. The basic circuits were presented in oscillators, in the Electronics Technician program."},{"t":"Phase noise: Leeson's model","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#ruido","x":"03 Phase noise: Leeson's model A real oscillator does not generate a perfect line: the transistor's noise modulates its phase, and the spectrum has skirts on either side of the carrier. David Leeson proposed in 1966 a model that shows what it depends on: \\[ \\mathcal{L}(\\Delta f) = 10\\log\\left[\\frac{2FkT}{P_s}\\, A\\left(1 + \\frac{f_c}{\\Delta f}\\right)\\right] \\] \\[ A = 1 + \\left(\\frac{f_0}{2Q_L\\,\\Delta f}\\right)^2 \\] \\( \\Delta f \\) is the offset from the carrier \\( f_0 \\), \\( Q_L \\) the loaded quality factor of the resonator, \\( P_s \\) the power in the resonator, \\( F \\) the noise factor of the amplifier and \\( f_c \\) the flicker noise corner. Within \\( f_0/2Q_L \\) the noise falls 20 dB per decade: doubling Q improves it by 6 dB. That is why reference oscillators use crystals, with Q of tens or hundreds of thousands, compared with a few hundred for an LC. The phase noise of the reference is what a synthesizer multiplies, as seen in synthesized generators."},{"t":"The quartz crystal","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#cristal","x":"04 The quartz crystal A quartz crystal is piezoelectric: it vibrates mechanically when a voltage is applied, and its mechanical resonance looks electrically like a series RLC circuit, the motional branch (\\( L_m, C_m, R_m \\)), in parallel with the capacitance of the electrodes \\( C_0 \\). It has two very close resonances: \\[ f_s = \\frac{1}{2\\pi\\sqrt{L_m C_m}} \\] \\[ f_p = f_s\\sqrt{1 + \\frac{C_m}{C_0}} \\] \\[ f_L \\approx f_s\\left(1 + \\frac{C_m}{2(C_0 + C_L)}\\right) \\] \\( C_m \\) is a few femtofarads and \\( C_0 \\) a few picofarads, so \\( f_p \\) is only a fraction of a percent above \\( f_s \\). In parallel-resonance oscillators the crystal operates between the two, as an inductor, and the frequency depends on the load capacitance \\( C_L \\) that the circuit presents to it. The manufacturer specifies the nominal frequency for a given \\( C_L \\). Lab · the crystal and its load capacitance A crystal with a series resonance at 10 MHz, specified for an 18 pF load. The curve is the reactance of the crystal between its two resonances; the yellow line, the reactance that the crystal has to cancel in order to oscillate with the chosen load. Their crossing is the operating frequency. Motional capacitance C m = Parallel capacitance C 0 = Circuit load capacitance C L = Besides the load, the frequency changes with temperature: in the AT cut, the most widely used, it follows a cubic with a few ppm between −20 and 70 °C. The temperature-compensated (TCXO) and oven-controlled (OCXO) oscillators of tim"},{"t":"In the lab","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Colpitts Build a Colpitts oscillator with a transistor, measure the frequency with a counter and compare it with the calculated one. Change the C 1 /C 2 ratio and observe the start-up. Activity 2 · Pulling a crystal In a Pierce oscillator with a logic gate, change the load capacitors and measure the shift in ppm. Activity 3 · Phase noise Compare with a spectrum analyzer the phase noise of an LC oscillator and a crystal oscillator of similar frequency."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Forgetting the stray capacitances in the crystal load. Those of the board and the IC add up to several picofarads. Driving the crystal with too much power. It ages faster and changes frequency; the drive level is specified. Designing with the loop gain exactly at 1. The oscillator will not start with tolerances; margin is needed. Loading the tank with the output. It lowers the Q and worsens the phase noise; a buffer is preferable."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment At what frequency does a Colpitts with L = 2 μH, C 1 = 220 pF and C 2 = 220 pF oscillate? \\( C_{eq} \\) = 110 pF: \\( 1/(2\\pi\\sqrt{2\\cdot10^{-6}\\cdot 110\\cdot10^{-12}}) \\approx 10.7 \\) MHz. According to Leeson, how much does the close-in phase noise improve when the resonator's Q is doubled? 6 dB. What shift does a crystal with C m = 20 fF and C 0 = 5 pF have between loads of 18 and 16 pF? About 41 ppm. What component makes it possible to tune a VCO? A varactor, a diode whose capacitance depends on the reverse voltage."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"RF oscillators","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciladores-rf#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Chris Bowick. RF Circuit Design. 2nd ed., Newnes, 2008. RF oscillators and the practical design of resonant circuits. Thomas H. Lee. The Design of CMOS Radio-Frequency Integrated Circuits. 2nd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2004. Oscillators and a clear treatment of phase noise. David B. Leeson. A Simple Model of Feedback Oscillator Noise Spectrum. Proceedings of the IEEE, 54(2), 1966. The paper with the phase noise model."},{"t":"Seeing the shape of a signal","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#por-que","x":"01 Seeing the shape of a signal The oscilloscope plots voltage as a function of time. No other instrument shows at a glance the shape, the peaks, the oscillations, the rise times and the transients. Today almost all oscilloscopes are digital, but their vertical channel, their trigger and their probes inherit the architecture of the analog one, and understanding it explains why they are configured the way they are. Practical use is presented in the technician program, in the oscilloscope."},{"t":"The cathode-ray tube","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#trc","x":"02 The cathode-ray tube In the analog oscilloscope, an electron gun emits a beam that is accelerated with several kilovolts and focused onto a phosphor-coated screen, which glows where the beam strikes it. Two pairs of plates deflect the beam: the vertical ones, with the amplified signal; the horizontal ones, with a ramp that sweeps it from left to right. The deflection is proportional to the voltage between plates, with a sensitivity of a few millimeters per volt, so the plates need amplifiers of tens or hundreds of volts. The brightness of the trace depends on how long the beam spends at each place: an analog scope naturally shows with more intensity what happens more often."},{"t":"The vertical channel","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#vertical","x":"03 The vertical channel The signal goes through a compensated attenuator that sets the volts per division, a preamplifier, a delay line that delays it by about a hundred nanoseconds so that the sweep has time to start and the edge that triggered it is visible, and the final amplifier that drives the plates. The input presents 1 MΩ in parallel with about 20 pF, and the coupling can be DC, AC (with a capacitor that blocks the DC) or ground, to locate the zero. The vertical channel is an amplifier with limited bandwidth, and that limits the rise time it can display: \\[ t_r \\approx \\frac{0.35}{BW} \\] \\[ t_{r,meas} = \\sqrt{t_{r,signal}^2 + t_{r,osc}^2} \\] For a first-order system, with the rise time from 10 to 90%. A 100 MHz oscilloscope has 3.5 ns. Cascaded rise times add in quadrature: to measure an edge with an error below 5%, the oscilloscope has to be a bit more than three times faster (about 3.2: with exactly 3 the error is 5.4%)."},{"t":"Timebase and trigger","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#horizontal","x":"04 Timebase and trigger The timebase generates a linear ramp that sweeps the beam in a time set by the seconds-per-division control. For the image to stay still, each sweep has to start at the same point of the signal: that is decided by the trigger, which starts the ramp when the signal crosses a level with a chosen slope. In normal mode it sweeps only if there is a trigger; in auto mode, it sweeps anyway after a time, to show the trace even when there is no signal; in single mode, it sweeps just once. The holdoff prevents re-triggering for a time, to stabilize complex signals. In X-Y mode the ramp is replaced by a second signal: two sine waves form Lissajous figures, from which the frequency ratio and the phase shift are read."},{"t":"The compensated attenuator probe","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#puntas","x":"05 The compensated attenuator probe The 10:1 probe has a 9 MΩ resistor at its tip which, together with the megohm input, divides by ten and multiplies by ten the resistance seen by the circuit. But the input also has capacitance, and the cable’s adds more: without correction, the resistive divider would become a low-pass filter. The solution is an adjustable capacitor in parallel with the 9 MΩ, which forms a capacitive divider with the same ratio: \\[ R_p\\, C_p = R_{in}\\, C_{in} \\] When the two time constants are equal, the divider divides by ten at all frequencies. The adjustment is made with the oscilloscope’s calibrator square wave: the flat top has to come out flat. Lab · compensating a 10:1 probe A 9 MΩ probe with an adjustable capacitor \\( C_p \\), on a 1 MΩ input with 100 pF between the oscilloscope and the cable. The signal is the 0 to 5 V, 1 kHz square wave from the calibrator; the screen is set to 1 V/div and 0.2 ms/div, with the probe factor already applied. Compensation capacitance \\( C_p \\) = Even when compensated, the probe loads the circuit: it presents 10 MΩ in parallel with about 10 pF. At 100 MHz that capacitance is a reactance of only 160 Ω. For fast signals in high-impedance circuits, active probes are used, with an amplifier at the tip and less than 1 pF."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Compensating the probes Connect each probe to the calibrator, adjust the trimmer until the square wave looks flat and record how it looks overcompensated and undercompensated. Activity 2 · The bandwidth limit Measure the rise time of a fast pulse generator with oscilloscopes of different bandwidth and verify the sum in quadrature. Activity 3 · Lissajous In X-Y mode, measure the phase shift of an RC network at several frequencies with the Lissajous ellipse and compare with the calculation."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Not compensating the probe when changing it to another channel or oscilloscope. Each input has its own capacitance. Using the probe in 1:1 for fast signals. In that position the bandwidth drops to a few megahertz. Measuring rise times close to those of the oscilloscope without correcting. Connecting the probe ground to a point that is not ground. The oscilloscope ground is tied to the installation’s earth: a short circuit results."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What rise time does a 200 MHz oscilloscope have? \\( 0.35 / 200 \\) MHz = 1.75 ns. A 5 ns edge is measured with a 3.5 ns oscilloscope. What is read? \\( \\sqrt{25 + 12.25} \\approx 6.1 \\) ns: 22% more. What capacitance compensates a 9 MΩ probe on 1 MΩ with 20 pF? \\( C_p = 1 \\cdot 20 / 9 \\approx 2.2 \\) pF. How does the calibrator square wave look with the probe overcompensated? With spikes on the edges that decay toward the flat top: the capacitive divider lets more than a tenth through on fast changes."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The analog oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-analogico#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Ian Hickman. Oscilloscopes: How to Use Them, How They Work. 5th ed., Newnes, 2001. The oscilloscope from the inside, from the tube to digital ones, with probes and triggering. Tektronix. XYZs of Oscilloscopes. Tektronix, primer. The manufacturer’s introduction: vertical and horizontal systems, triggering and specifications. Albert D. Helfrick and William D. Cooper. Modern Electronic Instrumentation and Measurement Techniques (Spanish edition: “Instrumentación electrónica moderna y técnicas de medición”). Prentice Hall, 1990. The cathode-ray oscilloscope and its circuits."},{"t":"Three numbers that define a digital oscilloscope","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#arquitectura","x":"01 Three numbers that define a digital oscilloscope The digital oscilloscope of Measurements I was described by its bandwidth and its sampling rate. An advanced instrument is judged by three numbers that interact: the analog front-end bandwidth, the maximum sampling rate of the converter and the memory depth, the number of samples it stores in one acquisition. With sin(x)/x interpolation, it is necessary to sample at about 2.5 times the bandwidth to reconstruct a full-bandwidth signal well. The memory is the one that tends to be forgotten. The screen shows ten horizontal divisions; to fill them, the oscilloscope needs to store samples during that whole time: \\[ f_s = \\min\\!\\left(f_{s,\\max},\\ \\frac{M}{10\\, t_{div}}\\right) \\] \\( M \\) is the memory depth and \\( t_{div} \\) the timebase. With 10,000 points and 1 ms/div, the sampling rate drops to 1 MS/s, even though the converter can do 5 GS/s: a 100 MHz signal is displayed aliased. Lab · memory, timebase and sampling rate A 500 MHz, 5 GS/s oscilloscope. As the timebase is lengthened, the actual sampling rate depends on the memory. A 100.03 MHz signal is observed: if half the sampling rate falls below it, an alias appears. 10 k points of memory 100 k points 1 M points 10 M points 100 M points Timebase ="},{"t":"Advanced triggering and segmented memory","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#disparo","x":"02 Advanced triggering and segmented memory Finding a rare event is harder than seeing it. Advanced oscilloscopes trigger on conditions: a pulse narrower or wider than a value, a runt pulse that does not reach a logic level, a violation of setup and hold times between data and clock, or a particular field of a decoded serial frame. Segmented memory divides the memory into thousands of short acquisitions, one for each trigger, and stores the time of each one: it is useful for recording bursts separated by long dead times without wasting memory. Another important specification is the update rate, in waveforms per second. Between one acquisition and the next the instrument processes and does not see the signal: with 1000 waveforms per second and a 1 μs window, it is blind 99.9% of the time. High-rate instruments, with hundreds of thousands per second, find intermittent faults much sooner."},{"t":"Measurements, FFT and vertical resolution","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#analisis","x":"03 Measurements, FFT and vertical resolution The automatic measurements (rise time, frequency, RMS value) are calculated on the samples, and their accuracy depends on the number of points per edge. The FFT converts the record into a spectrum with a frequency resolution equal to the inverse of the recorded time: deep memory means fine resolution. The 8-bit converter gives 256 levels; in high-resolution mode, the instrument samples faster than necessary and averages neighboring samples, gaining half a bit for each doubling of the averaging, at the expense of bandwidth. The effects of sampling were studied in aliasing."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Alias due to memory Observe a 50 MHz signal with minimum memory and increasing timebases. Note the sampling rate reported by the instrument and the point at which the waveform stops being the real one. Activity 2 · Segmented memory Capture bursts of a serial communication with segmented memory and measure the time between them using the time stamps. Activity 3 · Runt pulses Generate an occasional runt pulse with an arbitrary function generator and find it with the runt-pulse trigger and with infinite persistence."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Looking only at the maximum sampling rate. With long timebases, the memory reduces it. Trusting the screen without looking at the actual sampling rate. An alias can look like a stable signal. Hunting for rare faults with a low update rate. The instrument does not see the signal most of the time. Using high resolution with fast signals. Averaging reduces the bandwidth."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What sampling rate does a 1 M-point oscilloscope use at 100 μs/div? \\( 10^6/(10 \\cdot 10^{-4}) = 1 \\) GS/s. What resolution does the FFT of a 10 ms record have? 100 Hz. How many effective bits are gained in high resolution by averaging 16 samples? About 2 bits: half a bit for each doubling. What is segmented memory for? For recording many short events separated by long dead times, without spending memory on the dead times."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The advanced digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-avanzado#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Tektronix. XYZs of Oscilloscopes. Tektronix, introductory note. The concepts of sampling, memory and triggering explained by a manufacturer. Keysight Technologies. Evaluating Oscilloscope Sample Rates vs. Sampling Fidelity. Application note, Keysight. Why sampling at twice the bandwidth is not enough. Clyde F. Coombs Jr. (ed.). Electronic Instrument Handbook. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. The digital oscilloscope and its acquisition modes."},{"t":"From the beam to the memory","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#arquitectura","x":"01 From the beam to the memory A digital storage oscilloscope (DSO) keeps the vertical channel of the analog scope —attenuator, amplifier, bandwidth— but instead of deflecting a beam, it digitizes the signal with a fast ADC and stores the samples in an acquisition memory. The trigger decides which part of that memory is displayed, and a processor builds the image, calculates measurements and stores the waveform. The advantage is enormous: you can see what happened before the trigger, capture a single event, measure automatically, calculate the FFT and decode serial buses. The price is that there is now sampling, with all its consequences: the signal you see is a reconstruction from samples, and it may not resemble the real one."},{"t":"Sampling rate and memory","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#muestreo","x":"02 Sampling rate and memory The maximum sampling rate appears in the specification, but the one actually used depends on the timebase, because the memory has to be enough to fill the ten divisions of the screen: \\[ f_s = \\min\\left( f_{s,max},\\ \\frac{N}{10 \\cdot t_{div}} \\right) \\] \\( N \\) is the number of points in the acquisition memory. With 10,000 points and 1 ms/div, the oscilloscope samples at only 1 MS/s, even if its maximum is 1 GS/s. More memory sustains a high sampling rate at slow timebases. To reconstruct a sine wave properly, more than two samples per period are needed, according to the sampling theorem. Oscilloscopes interpolate between samples with the \\( \\sin(x)/x \\) function and in practice need about 2.5 samples per period of the highest frequency; without interpolation, joining points with straight lines, about ten are needed. If the sampling rate falls below twice the signal frequency, an alias appears: a stable waveform, of lower frequency, that does not exist. Lab · the sampling rate that is actually used An oscilloscope with a 1 GS/s maximum. You choose the signal, the timebase and the memory length; the screen shows the real signal in gray and what the oscilloscope reconstructs from its samples, in green. 1 MHz sine wave 10.1 MHz sine wave 49 MHz sine wave 10 ns/div 100 ns/div 1 µs/div 10 µs/div 100 µs/div 1000-point memory 10,000-point memory 100,000-point memory 1,000,000-point memory There are two defenses against aliasing: look first with the fastes"},{"t":"Acquisition modes","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#modos","x":"03 Acquisition modes Mode What it does What for Normal One sample per interval General use Peak detect Maximum and minimum of each interval, sampling at the maximum rate Glitches and aliasing at slow timebases High resolution Averages the samples of each interval Less noise and more effective bits on slow signals Average Averages several successive acquisitions Reduces random noise by \\( \\sqrt{n} \\), with repetitive signals Segmented memory Stores only the segments around each trigger Widely spaced bursts, such as bus packets"},{"t":"Vertical resolution and bandwidth","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#vertical","x":"04 Vertical resolution and bandwidth Most oscilloscopes use 8-bit converters: 256 levels over the whole screen, about 32 per division. To avoid losing resolution, the signal has to fill almost the whole height: a signal that occupies one division is digitized with barely 5 bits. Recent models have 10 or 12 bits, useful for seeing small details on large signals, such as the ripple of a power supply. The frequency response of a digital oscilloscope is usually steeper than that of an analog one, to make use of the sampling rate without letting aliases through. That is why the relationship between rise time and bandwidth is \\( t_r \\approx 0.4/BW \\) to \\( 0.45/BW \\) instead of 0.35."},{"t":"Triggering, measurements and analysis","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#funciones","x":"05 Triggering, measurements and analysis Digital triggering goes beyond the edge: it can wait for a pulse wider or narrower than a value, a pulse that did not reach the logic level (runt), a pattern on several channels or a frame with certain data on a serial bus. Automatic measurements —frequency, rise time, RMS value— are calculated on the samples, and their accuracy depends on there being enough samples per edge and on the calculation spanning a whole number of periods. The FFT turns the oscilloscope into a basic spectrum analyzer, with the limitation of its 8 bits."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#laboratorio","x":"06 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Causing an alias With a 10 MHz generator, reduce the oscilloscope’s memory and lengthen the timebase until a low-frequency signal appears. Verify the apparent frequency with the formula and eliminate it with peak detect. Activity 2 · Averaging Measure a noisy 1 mV signal in normal mode and averaging 16 and 256 acquisitions. Estimate how much the noise drops. Activity 3 · Decoding a bus Capture an I²C transaction, configure the decoding and trigger on a specific address."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Trusting a stable waveform at a slow timebase. It may be an alias. Using a small signal on the screen. Bits of resolution are lost. Measuring a rise time with few samples per edge. Interpolation invents the shape. Leaving the memory at the minimum. It forces the sampling rate down at slow timebases."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment At what rate does a 2 GS/s oscilloscope with 20,000 points sample at 50 µs/div? \\( 20\\,000 / (10 \\cdot 50\\ \\mu\\text{s}) = 40 \\) MS/s. What apparent frequency does a 10.1 MHz sine wave sampled at 10 MS/s show? 100 kHz, the distance to the nearest multiple of the sampling rate. How many quantization levels does a signal use that occupies two divisions with an 8-bit ADC and eight divisions? About 64, the equivalent of 6 bits. How much does random noise drop when averaging 100 acquisitions? Tenfold: 20 dB."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The digital oscilloscope","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/osciloscopio-digital#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Tektronix. XYZs of Oscilloscopes. Tektronix, primer. Sampling, memory, acquisition modes and triggering in digital oscilloscopes. Keysight Technologies. Evaluating Oscilloscope Sample Rates vs. Sampling Fidelity. Application note 1587. How many samples per period are really needed, with measurements. Ian Hickman. Oscilloscopes: How to Use Them, How They Work. 5th ed., Newnes, 2001. Digital oscilloscopes compared with analog ones."},{"t":"Thermistors","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#termistores","x":"01 Thermistors A thermistor is a semiconductor resistor, made of sintered metal oxides, whose resistance changes strongly with temperature. NTCs lower their resistance when heated, by about 4 % per degree at room temperature, ten times more than a metal; they are used to measure temperature and to limit the inrush current of power supplies. PTCs raise their resistance abruptly above a transition temperature, and serve as protection and as self-regulating heaters. Their resistance follows, over a limited range, the β equation: \\[ R(T) = R_{25}\\,\\exp\\!\\left[\\beta\\left(\\frac{1}{T} - \\frac{1}{298.15\\ \\mathrm{K}}\\right)\\right] \\] \\[ \\frac{1}{T} = A + B \\ln R + C\\,(\\ln R)^3 \\] The second is the Steinhart–Hart equation, with three coefficients that are fitted by measuring three temperatures; over a wide range it is much more accurate than the β equation. The measurement current heats the thermistor: it has to be kept small compared with its dissipation constant, in mW/K. The curve is highly nonlinear. Connected in a divider with a fixed resistor \\( R_s \\), the output voltage is an S-shaped curve, with an inflection point where it is almost straight. Placing the inflection at the center of the measurement range \\( T_m \\) gives the resistor: \\[ R_s = R(T_m)\\,\\frac{\\beta - 2T_m}{\\beta + 2T_m} \\] With a 10 kΩ NTC and \\( \\beta = 3950\\ \\text{K} \\), for a range of 0 to 70 °C, centered at 35 °C, \\( R_s \\) ≈ 4.75 kΩ results. Sensor conditioning in general was covered in sensor conditioning. "},{"t":"Protection devices","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#proteccion","x":"02 Protection devices Device How it acts Use Fuse Melts by \\( I^2 t \\); fast or time-delay Overcurrent and short circuit, one time only Resettable PTC (polymeric) Goes to high resistance when heated and recovers on cooling USB ports, batteries, moderate overloads Varistor (MOV) Zinc oxide that conducts above a voltage; degrades with each surge Mains overvoltages TVS suppressor Large-area avalanche diode, very fast Electrostatic discharges and transients on data and power lines Gas discharge tube (GDT) Arc in a gas; large energy, but slow and with a high breakdown voltage Telephone and antenna lines, first stage of protection Protections are combined in stages: a discharge tube for the large energy, a separating resistance or inductance, and a TVS close to the circuit to clamp the voltage precisely."},{"t":"Relays and optocouplers","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#reles","x":"03 Relays and optocouplers An electromechanical relay galvanically isolates the control circuit from the power circuit and has a very low contact resistance, but it is slow, has bounce and a limited mechanical life. Its contacts are rated for current and voltage, and the DC breaking capacity is much lower than the AC one, because the arc does not extinguish by itself. The coil needs a freewheeling diode so as not to destroy the transistor that drives it. A solid-state relay, with a triac or MOS transistors and an optocoupler, has no wear or bounce, but it dissipates and has leakage current. An optocoupler transmits a signal across an isolation barrier with an LED and a phototransistor, and is specified by its current transfer ratio (CTR), which falls as the LED ages."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · NTC calibration Measure the resistance of an NTC at three temperatures with a bath and a reference thermometer, and compute the Steinhart–Hart coefficients. Activity 2 · Self-heating Measure the voltage across an NTC with different currents and detect the self-heating error. Activity 3 · Relay coil Observe on the oscilloscope the overvoltage when the coil of a relay is switched off, with and without a diode."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Using the β equation over the whole range. Outside the interval where it was fitted, the error reaches several degrees. Forgetting the self-heating of the thermistor. A high measurement current puts it above the temperature to be measured. Trusting a varistor forever. Each surge degrades it and it can end up short-circuited. Switching DC with a relay rated for AC. The arc does not extinguish and burns the contacts."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What resistance does a 10 kΩ NTC with \\( \\beta = 3950\\ \\text{K} \\) have at 70 °C? About 1.76 kΩ. What fixed resistor linearizes the divider best for measuring between 0 and 70 °C? One close to 4.75 kΩ, the one that places the inflection at 35 °C; in the E24 series, 4.7 or 5.1 kΩ. Why is a gas discharge tube combined with a TVS? The tube absorbs the large energy but lets a high spike through while it ignites; the TVS clamps the voltage quickly and precisely. What is the diode in parallel with a relay coil for? To give a path to the coil current when it is switched off and avoid the overvoltage."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Other devices used in electronics","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/otros-dispositivos#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Ramón Pallás Areny. Sensores y acondicionadores de señal. 4th ed., Marcombo, 2003 (in Spanish). Thermistors, their linearization and their conditioning. Jacob Fraden. Handbook of Modern Sensors. 5th ed., Springer, 2016. Temperature sensors and the physics of each one. Charles A. Harper (ed.). Passive Electronic Component Handbook. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1997. Thermistors, varistors, relays and protection devices."},{"t":"The LCR meter","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#lcr","x":"01 The LCR meter The bridges of Electronic Measurements I are balanced by hand. A modern LCR meter does the same automatically with a self-balancing bridge: it applies a sinusoidal voltage of known frequency to the component, takes the other terminal to a virtual ground with an amplifier, measures the current with a range resistor and calculates the complex impedance with two synchronous detectors, in phase and in quadrature: \\[ Z = \\frac{V}{I} = R + jX \\] From \\( R \\) and \\( X \\) the instrument deduces the value the user asks for according to the chosen model: series or parallel capacitance or inductance, and the dissipation factor \\( D = R/|X| \\) or the quality factor \\( Q = 1/D \\). A real component is not ideal, and the number the instrument shows depends on the model and on the test frequency. For the same component, the series and parallel models give different values: \\[ C_p = \\frac{C_s}{1 + D^2} \\] With small losses, \\( D \\ll 1 \\), the two models coincide. By convention the series model is used for low impedances (large capacitors, small inductors) and the parallel model for high impedances."},{"t":"The real capacitor","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#real","x":"02 The real capacitor Besides its capacitance, a capacitor has an equivalent series resistance (ESR), from the electrodes, the dielectric and the electrolyte, and a series inductance (ESL), from its leads and its construction. Its impedance falls with frequency down to the self-resonance, where it equals only the ESR, and above that it grows like that of an inductor: \\[ Z(f) = ESR + j\\left(2\\pi f\\, ESL - \\frac{1}{2\\pi f C}\\right) \\] \\[ f_r = \\frac{1}{2\\pi \\sqrt{ESL \\cdot C}} \\] A 10 μF electrolytic with 10 nH of ESL resonates at 503 kHz. Above that, it does not decouple: that is why small ceramic capacitors are placed in parallel with the large ones on boards. Lab · what an LCR meter reads on a real capacitor The impedance of a capacitor with ESR and ESL as a function of frequency. The dot marks the meter’s test frequency; the data are what it would show in the series and parallel models. Nominal capacitance = ESR = ESL = Test frequency 100 Hz 1 kHz 10 kHz 100 kHz 1 MHz"},{"t":"Connection and compensation","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#conexion","x":"03 Connection and compensation The cables and the fixture add their own impedance. For low impedances, four terminals are used, with the current and the voltage on separate pairs, as in the Kelvin method; for high impedances, shields that take the stray capacitances to ground. Before measuring, compensation is performed: one measurement with the terminals open and another shorted, which the instrument subtracts. Without it, a 1 pF capacitor or a 10 mΩ resistor are read with large errors."},{"t":"Active devices: the curve tracer","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#activos","x":"04 Active devices: the curve tracer For transistors and diodes, the curve tracer applies a stepped voltage to the base or gate and a sweep to the collector or drain, and draws the family of output curves. From them one reads the current gain, the output resistance, the saturation voltage and the breakdown voltage. Semiconductor parameter analyzers, with source-measure units (SMUs), do the same with picoampere precision and make it possible to measure leakage currents and junction capacitances. The models being measured are those of the bipolar transistor and the MOSFET, in Electronic Devices."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#laboratorio","x":"05 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Sweeping an electrolytic Measure an electrolytic capacitor with an LCR meter at all its test frequencies, in series model, and estimate the ESR and the ESL. Compare with the datasheet. Activity 2 · Compensation Measure a 10 pF capacitor with and without open-circuit compensation, and a 0.1 Ω resistor with and without short-circuit compensation. Activity 3 · Transistor curves Use a curve tracer to obtain the output family of a bipolar transistor and calculate β and the Early voltage."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Measuring a capacitor without noting the test frequency. The value changes with it, and near resonance it stops making sense. Forgetting the compensation. The cables can add more than the component. Confusing the series model with the parallel one. With large losses, they give very different values. Measuring in circuit. Components in parallel change the reading."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment Where does a 100 nF capacitor with 5 nH of ESL resonate? \\( 1/(2\\pi\\sqrt{5\\cdot10^{-9} \\cdot 10^{-7}}) \\approx 7.1 \\) MHz. A meter reads \\( C_s = 1\\ \\mu\\text{F} \\) with \\( D = 0.5 \\). What would it read in parallel? \\( 1/(1 + 0.25) = 0.8 \\) μF. Why is four-terminal measurement used for low resistances? So that the drop in the current leads does not enter the voltage measurement. What does a curve tracer measure? The families of output curves of an active device, sweeping the output voltage for several values of the input."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Measuring device parameters","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parametros-dispositivos#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Keysight Technologies. Impedance Measurement Handbook. 6th ed., Keysight, 2016. The reference guide on LCR meters, models, fixtures and compensation. Albert D. Helfrick and William D. Cooper. Modern Electronic Instrumentation and Measurement Techniques. Prentice Hall, 1990. Spanish edition: “Instrumentación electrónica moderna y técnicas de medición”, Prentice Hall Hispanoamericana, 1991. Bridges and component measuring instruments. Clyde F. Coombs Jr. (ed.). Electronic Instrument Handbook. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. Chapters by instrument, among them the impedance meter and the curve tracer."},{"t":"Two symmetries","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#definicion","x":"01 Two symmetries A signal is even if mirroring it in time does not change it, and odd if mirroring it is equivalent to changing its sign: \\[ \\text{even: } x(-t) = x(t) \\qquad\\qquad \\text{odd: } x(-t) = -x(t) \\] In discrete time it is the same, with \\( n \\) in place of \\( t \\). The cosine is even, the sine is odd, and an arbitrary signal is neither one nor the other. From this comes a minor observation: every odd signal is zero at the origin, because \\( x(0) = -x(0) \\) forces \\( x(0) = 0 \\). The interesting thing is not classifying signals, but that these two symmetries split any signal in two, and that this split says concrete things about the spectrum and about the distortion of an amplifier."},{"t":"Every signal is the sum of an even one and an odd one","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#descomposicion","x":"02 Every signal is the sum of an even one and an odd one And in only one way. The recipe is so simple it looks like a trick, but it is verified in one line: \\[ \\mathcal{E}v\\{x(t)\\} = \\frac{x(t) + x(-t)}{2}, \\qquad \\mathcal{O}d\\{x(t)\\} = \\frac{x(t) - x(-t)}{2} \\] The first is even by construction —replacing \\( t \\) with \\( -t \\) leaves the same two terms— and the second is odd for the same reason. And added together they give \\( x(t) \\), because the \\( x(-t) \\) terms cancel. The example to keep at hand is the step. Its even part equals \\( 1/2 \\) everywhere, and its odd part is half the sign function: \\[ u(t) = \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}}_{\\text{even}} + \\underbrace{\\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sgn}(t)}_{\\text{odd}} \\] A step is a DC level plus an antisymmetric jump. This decomposition shows up when computing its Fourier transform. Lab · splitting a signal in two Above, the signal and its mirror image; below, the even part and the odd part. Check that added together they give back the original, and see how the energy is divided. x(t) = u(t) · the step x(t) = e^(−t)·u(t) x(t) = pulse between 0 and 2 x(t) = clipped ramp x(t) = cos(2t) x(t) = sin(2t) Show the mirror image"},{"t":"The rules for products and integrals","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#propiedades","x":"03 The rules for products and integrals Operation Result Looks like even × even even \\( (+)(+) = + \\) odd × odd even \\( (-)(-) = + \\) even × odd odd \\( (+)(-) = - \\) And over a symmetric interval, the integral of an odd signal is zero, while that of an even one is twice the integral over half: \\[ \\int_{-a}^{a} x_{\\text{odd}}(t)\\,dt = 0, \\qquad \\int_{-a}^{a} x_{\\text{even}}(t)\\,dt = 2\\int_{0}^{a} x_{\\text{even}}(t)\\,dt \\] Those two lines are what make half of the coefficients of a Fourier series vanish before computing anything, and what let you save half of the work when there is symmetry. The energy is divided too Since the product of the even part and the odd part is odd, its integral over the whole axis vanishes. The cross term disappears and what remains is \\( E = E_{\\text{even}} + E_{\\text{odd}} \\): the energy of the signal is the sum of the energies of its two parts, with no interference between them. The lab verifies it number by number."},{"t":"What symmetry does to the spectrum","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#fourier","x":"04 What symmetry does to the spectrum This is where it stops being a curiosity. The symmetry of a periodic signal determines which harmonics it can have: If the signal is… Its Fourier series… Example Even Has only cosines: \\( b_n = 0 \\) Centered triangle wave Odd Has only sines: \\( a_n = 0 \\) Centered sawtooth Half-wave: \\( x(t \\pm T/2) = -x(t) \\) Only odd harmonics Symmetric square wave Real, in general \\( X(-j\\omega) = X^{*}(j\\omega) \\) Any measurable signal The last row is called Hermitian symmetry and it is the reason for something you see every day: on a spectrum analyzer only the positive frequencies are plotted. The negative part is not left out for convenience, but because it adds no information: for a real signal, the magnitude of the spectrum is even and the phase is odd. Why a push-pull sounds different The nonlinearity of an amplifier is expanded as \\( y = a_1x + a_2x^2 + a_3x^3 + \\cdots \\). The even terms generate even harmonics —the second above all— and the odd ones, odd harmonics. A push-pull stage has odd symmetry by construction: the two halves process opposite half-cycles, and when subtracted they cancel all the even terms. That is why a push-pull has almost no second harmonic and its residual distortion is third-order. A single-stage class A amplifier, on the other hand, lets the second harmonic through, which is the one associated with a “warm” sound."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Exercise 1 · Decomposing by hand For \\( x(t) = e^{-t}u(t) \\), obtain \\( \\mathcal{E}v\\{x\\} \\) and \\( \\mathcal{O}d\\{x\\} \\) and check that they equal \\( \\frac{1}{2}e^{-|t|} \\) and \\( \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sgn}(t)e^{-|t|} \\). Check that they add up to the original. Exercise 2 · The harmonics of a push-pull Measure with a spectrum analyzer the distortion of a class A stage and of a push-pull stage, with the same input signal and the same output power. Compare the amplitude of the second harmonic in each case and explain the difference through symmetry. Exercise 3 · The square wave has no even harmonics Generate a symmetric square wave and measure its spectrum. Check that only the odd harmonics appear and that they fall off as \\( 1/n \\). Then unbalance the duty cycle and watch how the even ones appear."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing every signal is even or odd. Almost none is: most have both parts. Forgetting that odd × odd gives even. It is the rule of signs, and it is used all the time in Fourier analysis. Confusing half-wave symmetry with odd symmetry. They are different conditions: the first eliminates even harmonics, the second eliminates cosines. Thinking the negative spectrum is an artifact. It exists; what happens is that for real signals it is determined by the positive one. Assuming \\( x(0) \\) can be anything in an odd signal. It has to be zero."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How do you get the even part of a signal? \\( \\mathcal{E}v\\{x\\} = \\frac{1}{2}\\big(x(t) + x(-t)\\big) \\). The odd one comes out with a minus. What is the even part of the step? The constant \\( 1/2 \\). And the odd part is \\( \\frac{1}{2}\\operatorname{sgn}(t) \\). What is the integral of an odd signal from \\( -a \\) to a? Zero: what is on one side cancels what is on the other. Which harmonics does a signal with half-wave symmetry have? Only the odd ones. It is the case of the symmetric square wave. Why does the spectrum analyzer show only positive frequencies? Because of Hermitian symmetry: for a real signal, the magnitude is even and the phase odd, so the negative half adds nothing. Why does a push-pull cancel the even harmonics? Because its transfer characteristic is odd by construction, and the even terms of the expansion are subtracted between the two halves."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Even and odd signals","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pares-impares#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The even-odd decomposition and its role in the symmetry properties of Fourier analysis. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Circuitos microelectrónicos”), Oxford University Press, 2016. The harmonic distortion of output stages, with the push-pull cancellation. Simon Haykin and Barry Van Veen. Signals and Systems. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Limusa Wiley, 2001. The symmetries of Fourier series, tabulated with their consequences for the coefficients."},{"t":"Energy does not depend on where you look at it","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#enunciado","x":"01 Energy does not depend on where you look at it A signal has a certain energy. That energy can be computed by summing its square over time, or by summing the square of its spectrum over frequency, and the result is the same. That is Parseval’s relation: \\[ \\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |x(t)|^2\\,dt = \\frac{1}{2\\pi}\\int_{-\\infty}^{\\infty} |X(j\\omega)|^2\\,d\\omega \\] For isolated, finite-energy signals. The \\( 1/2\\pi \\) disappears if you work in hertz instead of radians per second. And for periodic signals, where what makes sense is the average power, the version is even more convenient because the integral becomes a sum: \\[ P = \\frac{1}{T}\\int_{T}|x(t)|^2\\,dt = \\sum_{k=-\\infty}^{\\infty}|a_k|^2 \\] The total power is the sum of the powers of the harmonics. Each one contributes its own share, with no cross terms. The absence of cross terms is a direct consequence of orthogonality: when you square the sum of harmonics, the products between different harmonics average to zero and only the squares survive. It is the same Pythagorean theorem, in infinitely many dimensions."},{"t":"How much energy each harmonic contributes","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#lab","x":"02 How much energy each harmonic contributes The practical use of Parseval is not to verify an equality: it is to be able to distribute the energy among the frequencies and ask how many are needed. The lab computes the two integrals, compares them, and shows the accumulation harmonic by harmonic. Lab · how the power is distributed The signal on top; below, the power of each harmonic and the cumulative total. See how many harmonics are needed to gather 99 %, and how that changes with the waveform. pure sinusoid square triangular sawtooth pulse, adjustable duty cycle clipped sinusoid Show the cumulative total Duty cycle / clipping = 0.50"},{"t":"Harmonic distortion comes from here","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#thd","x":"03 Harmonic distortion comes from here If the total power is the sum of the powers of the harmonics, then you can ask what fraction is not in the fundamental. That is the total harmonic distortion: \\[ \\text{THD} = \\frac{\\sqrt{\\sum_{n \\ge 2} A_n^2}}{A_1} \\] The square root of the sum of the squares of all the harmonics, divided by the fundamental. It is expressed as a percentage or in decibels. A perfect amplifier delivers a pure sinusoid: all the power is in the fundamental and the THD is zero. Any nonlinearity distributes energy to the harmonics, and that percentage is what a distortion analyzer measures. Signal THD Comment Ideal sinusoid 0 % All the power in the fundamental Decent audio amplifier < 0.1 % The harmonics are 60 dB down Triangular wave 12 % Its harmonics fall off as \\( 1/n^2 \\) Square wave 48.3 % They fall off as \\( 1/n \\): there is a great deal outside The last row gives a number worth remembering because it serves as a reference: a square wave has a THD of 48.3 %, among the highest found in practice. If a distortion analyzer reads something close to that, the signal is completely clipped."},{"t":"Where else it is used","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#usos","x":"04 Where else it is used Problem How Parseval solves it Noise at the output of a filter The spectral density is integrated times \\( |H(j\\omega)|^2 \\) instead of simulating in time Occupied bandwidth You look for the band that contains 99 % of the power Choosing how many harmonics to compute You stop when the cumulative total reaches the target Signal-to-noise ratio It is a ratio between two energies, and it can be measured in either of the two domains Audio and image compression The coefficients that contribute little energy are discarded The last row is a simple idea with enormous consequences. If most of the energy of a signal is in a few coefficients, storing only those preserves almost the entire signal. That is, in essence, what JPEG and MP3 do: transform, keep what has energy and throw away the rest. The 99 % is not always close by How much you can trim depends on how the harmonics fall off. Those of a triangular wave fall off as \\( 1/n^2 \\), so their powers fall off as \\( 1/n^4 \\) and with three or four terms you already gather almost everything. Those of a square wave fall off as \\( 1/n \\), with powers as \\( 1/n^2 \\), and many more are needed. And a narrow pulse spreads the energy over a multitude of harmonics: that is why it occupies so much bandwidth."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying Parseval by hand For a square wave of amplitude 1, compute the power in time —which is 1— and sum \\( \\sum (4/n\\pi)^2/2 \\) for the first ten odd harmonics. Check that the sum approaches 1 and estimate how much is missing. Activity 2 · Measuring THD Using the spectrum analyzer, measure the amplitude of the first five harmonics at the output of an amplifier, compute the THD and compare it with the one indicated by the distortion analyzer. Repeat while raising the signal until it clips. Activity 3 · The 99 % bandwidth For a 1 μs pulse train at 10 kHz, determine how many harmonics are needed to gather 99 % of the power and what bandwidth they represent. Contrast with the rule \\( B \\approx 1/t_r \\)."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Adding amplitudes instead of powers. Parseval sums squares; amplitudes are not added. Forgetting the \\( 1/2\\pi \\) in the integral version, or carrying it along when working in hertz. Counting only one half of the spectrum. In the two-sided version the negative frequencies are included. Computing THD with a signal clipped by the instrument itself. You end up measuring the instrument. Assuming that a few harmonics are always enough. It depends on how quickly they fall off."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What does Parseval say, in one line? That the energy computed in time and the energy computed in frequency are the same. Why are there no cross terms between harmonics? Because of orthogonality: the average of the product of two different harmonics is zero. What is the THD of a square wave? 48.3 %. It is the reference value for a completely clipped signal. Why does a triangular wave need fewer harmonics than a square wave? Because its amplitudes fall off as \\( 1/n^2 \\) and its powers as \\( 1/n^4 \\): the cumulative total reaches 99 % much sooner. How is the noise at the output of a filter calculated? By integrating the noise spectral density multiplied by \\( |H(j\\omega)|^2 \\) over the whole band. How is Parseval related to JPEG? It makes it possible to know which coefficients contribute little energy, so they can be discarded without it being too noticeable."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Parseval’s relation","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/parseval#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1997. Parseval in both versions, with the derivation from orthogonality. C. D. Motchenbacher and J. A. Connelly. Low-Noise Electronic System Design. Wiley, 1993. The calculation of total noise by integrating spectral density, which is Parseval applied every day. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Circuitos microelectrónicos”), Oxford University Press, 2015. Harmonic distortion in amplifiers, with its measurement and its causes."},{"t":"Resistors","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#resistores","x":"01 Resistors The ideal model of passive components was covered in passive components, in Circuit Theory I; here what matters is real components and how they are chosen. In a resistor what matters is the value, the tolerance, the temperature coefficient (TCR, in ppm/K), the power rating, the maximum voltage, the noise and the long-term stability: Technology Characteristics Carbon film Cheap, 5 %, high and negative TCR; falling out of use Metal film 1 % or better, TCR of 15 to 100 ppm/K, low noise Thick film (SMD chip) The most widely used; 1 % to 5 %, TCR of 100 to 200 ppm/K Thin film (SMD chip) Precision of 0.1 %, TCR of 5 to 25 ppm/K Wirewound Power and precision; inductive Metal foil Maximum stability, TCR close to 1 ppm/K Values follow the standard series of IEC 60063, with logarithmic steps: E12 (10 %) has 12 values per decade, E24 (5 %) 24 and E96 (1 %) 96. The rated power holds up to a temperature, typically 70 °C, and above it is reduced linearly. SMD chips are designated by their size in inches: 0402, 0603, 0805, 1206."},{"t":"Capacitors","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#capacitores","x":"02 Capacitors The dielectric decides almost everything in a capacitor: the capacitance per volume, the stability, the losses and the life. Type Characteristics Class 1 ceramic (C0G, NP0) Stable: 0 ± 30 ppm/K, no voltage effect; up to tens of nF Class 2 ceramic (X7R, X5R) X7R varies ±15 % between −55 and 125 °C, and loses capacitance with the applied DC voltage Y5V, Z5U ceramic A lot of capacitance, but varies from +22 to −82 % with temperature Film (polypropylene, polyester) Low losses and self-healing; filters, audio, snubber networks Aluminum electrolytic A lot of cheap capacitance; polarized, high ESR, limited life Tantalum and polymer Compact and low ESR; tantalum ones fail short-circuited if overstressed The electrolyte of an aluminum capacitor slowly evaporates, and its useful life depends on temperature. As a rule of thumb, it doubles for every 10 °C below the rated temperature: \\[ L = L_0 \\cdot 2^{(T_0 - T)/10} \\] A 2000-hour capacitor at 105 °C operating at 65 °C lasts about 32,000 hours. The ripple current heats it from the inside and has to be added. It is often the component that limits the life of a power supply."},{"t":"Inductors and transformers","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#inductores","x":"03 Inductors and transformers A commercial inductor is specified by its inductance, its DC resistance, its saturation current, where the inductance drops by a given percentage, its thermal current and its self-resonant frequency, above which it behaves like a capacitor. Core materials were covered in magnetic materials. General-purpose transformers, mains ones, are chosen for their rating in VA, their voltages, their regulation between no load and full load, their insulation class and their electrical safety."},{"t":"Tolerances: worst case and statistics","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#tolerancias","x":"04 Tolerances: worst case and statistics The output of a circuit depends on all of its components, and each has a tolerance and drifts with temperature. There are two ways to analyze it: the worst case, which combines all the extremes and guarantees compliance, but oversizes; and the statistical analysis or Monte Carlo, which draws thousands of circuits and estimates what fraction complies. \\[ V_o = V_i\\,\\frac{R_2}{R_1 + R_2} \\] In a divider, the worst case is \\( R_2 \\) at its maximum and \\( R_1 \\) at its minimum. If both resistors come from the same integrated network, their variations track each other and the ratio is much more stable than each value. Lab · Monte Carlo of a divider 4000 dividers of 10 V with two 10 kΩ resistors, each drawn uniformly within its tolerance and its temperature coefficient. The histogram shows the output; the red lines, the worst case. Tolerance = Temperature coefficient = Temperature change ΔT ="},{"t":"In the lab","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Real distribution Measure fifty resistors from the same lot and plot the histogram. Does it fill the whole tolerance or is it concentrated? Activity 2 · Ceramic capacitors Measure a 10 μF X7R capacitor with the LCR meter applying different bias voltages and compare with the manufacturer's curve. Activity 3 · Drift Heat a divider with a heat gun and measure the variation of its output, with discrete resistors and with an integrated network."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Using the nominal value of an X5R or X7R ceramic. With the applied DC voltage it can lose more than half of its capacitance. Forgetting power derating. A 1/4 W resistor does not dissipate 1/4 W inside a hot enclosure. Designing with the tolerance alone. Drift with temperature and aging add to it. Placing an electrolytic next to the heat sink. Every 10 °C more takes away half of its life."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment How many values per decade does the E24 series have? Twenty-four. A 1000 h electrolytic at 105 °C operates at 65 °C. How long does it last, according to the rule of thumb? About 16,000 hours. What is the worst-case error of a 1:2 divider with 1 % resistors? ±1 % of the output: ±50 mV on 5 V. What ceramic capacitor is used in a precision oscillator or filter? A class 1 one, C0G or NP0."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"General-purpose passive components","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/pasivos-uso-general#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Charles A. Harper (ed.). Passive Electronic Component Handbook. 2nd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1997. The technology of resistors, capacitors and inductors. Paul Horowitz and Winfield Hill. The Art of Electronics. 3rd ed., Cambridge University Press, 2015. The opening chapter and the appendices on real components and how to choose them. Henry W. Ott. Electromagnetic Compatibility Engineering. Wiley, 2009. The behavior of real passives at high frequency."},{"t":"The planning hierarchy","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#planificacion","x":"01 The planning hierarchy Planning production means deciding what, how much and when to manufacture with the available resources. It is done in levels, from the general to the particular: Level Horizon What it decides Aggregate plan Months to a year Total output by family, staffing, shifts, subcontracting and inventory Master production schedule Weeks to months How many units of each finished product and in which week Material requirements planning (MRP) Weeks Which components to order or make, how many and when Shop scheduling Days and hours Which order goes to which machine and in what sequence MRP starts from the master schedule and the bill of materials —each unit takes so many boards, each board so many components— and, subtracting inventory and adding lead times, calculates the necessary orders: this is the explosion of the bill. Integrated with purchasing, finance and sales, it became the ERP systems. Shop scheduling uses Gantt charts and priority rules, such as serving first the order with the shortest processing time or the one with the nearest due date. Just in time, by contrast, does not push production from a plan but pulls it from demand, with kanban."},{"t":"Inventories","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#inventarios","x":"02 Inventories Inventories —raw materials, work in process and finished goods— buffer the differences between the pace of purchasing, of production and of sales, but they tie up money, take up space, deteriorate or become obsolete, and hide problems. In electronics the risk of obsolescence is high: a discontinued component can stop a line. ABC classification ranks items by their annual value: typically 20 % of them, the A items, account for about 80 % of the value and deserve the strictest control."},{"t":"The economic order quantity and the reorder point","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#lote","x":"03 The economic order quantity and the reorder point With a constant annual demand \\( D \\), a cost \\( S \\) for each order and a cost \\( H \\) of holding one unit in inventory for a year, the total annual cost as a function of the lot size \\( Q \\) and its minimum, Harris’s economic order quantity (1913), are: \\[ CT(Q) = \\frac{D}{Q}\\,S + \\frac{Q}{2}\\,H \\] \\[ Q^* = \\sqrt{\\frac{2 D S}{H}} \\] At the optimum, the ordering cost and the holding cost are equal. The order is placed when inventory falls to the reorder point, the demand during the lead time \\( L \\) plus a safety stock \\( z\\,\\sigma_d \\sqrt{L} \\), which covers the variability of daily demand with a service level given by \\( z \\). Lab · economic order quantity The curves are the annual ordering cost (violet), holding cost (green) and total cost (blue) as a function of the lot size. The yellow dot is the chosen lot. For the reorder point, 250 working days, a lead time of 10 days, a standard deviation of daily demand of 20 % and a service level of 95 % (\\(z = 1.645\\)) are assumed. Annual demand D = Cost per order S = Holding cost H = Chosen lot Q ="},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Bill of materials Build the multilevel bill of materials of a laboratory unit and carry out the explosion for one hundred units. Activity 2 · ABC classification With the laboratory component inventory and its prices, classify the items and plot the Pareto curve. Activity 3 · Scheduling Schedule five orders on two machines with different priority rules and compare the delays on a Gantt chart."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the safety stock. With variable demand, ordering exactly at the expected demand leaves you out of stock half of the time. Applying the economic order quantity with highly irregular demand. The model assumes constant demand. Controlling all items equally. ABC classification concentrates the effort where the money is. Planning materials without considering capacity. An MRP can order more than the machines can make."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What is the economic order quantity with \\(D = 12\\,000\\), \\(S = 100\\) and \\(H = 4\\)? \\( \\sqrt{2 \\cdot 12\\,000 \\cdot 100 / 4} \\approx 775 \\) units. How many orders per year does that imply? About 15.5. What happens to the economic order quantity if demand quadruples? It doubles, because it depends on the square root of demand. What is the explosion of the bill of materials? The calculation of the quantities of each component needed to produce the master schedule."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Planning and inventories","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/planificacion-inventarios#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Jay Heizer and Barry Render. Principles of Operations Management. Pearson. (Spanish edition: “Principios de administración de operaciones”.) Planning, MRP and inventory models. Richard B. Chase, F. Robert Jacobs and Nicholas J. Aquilano. Operations and Supply Chain Management. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Administración de operaciones: producción y cadena de suministros”.) Production and inventory planning with cases. Steven Nahmias. Production and Operations Analysis. McGraw-Hill. (Spanish edition: “Análisis de la producción y las operaciones”.) The mathematical models of inventory and planning."},{"t":"Why an electronics engineer needs complex numbers","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#porque","x":"01 Why an electronics engineer needs complex numbers The word “imaginary” is a historical misfortune: it suggests that they do not exist. In electronics, the complex plane is the place where the most concrete things of the whole degree live. Object in the complex plane What it is in electronics A point in polar form A phasor: amplitude and phase of a sinusoidal signal A number \\( R + jX \\) An impedance: resistance and reactance Multiplying by \\( e^{j\\theta} \\) Phase-shifting a signal by \\( \\theta \\) radians A point in the left half-plane A stable pole: the response dies out The unit circle The edge of stability of a discrete system The circle \\( |\\Gamma| = \\text{const} \\) A circle of the Smith chart This whole course is built on that plane: poles and zeros, the Laplace transform, the Z-transform and the frequency response are readings of points and regions inside it."},{"t":"The three ways of writing the same thing","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#formas","x":"02 The three ways of writing the same thing \\[ z = a + jb = r(\\cos\\theta + j\\,\\operatorname{sin}\\theta) = r\\,e^{j\\theta} \\] Rectangular, trigonometric and exponential. In engineering you write \\( j \\) and not \\( i \\), because \\( i \\) is already the current. For Use Because Adding and subtracting Rectangular The real and imaginary parts are added separately Multiplying and dividing Polar or exponential The moduli are multiplied and the angles are added Raising to a power Exponential \\( z^n = r^n e^{jn\\theta} \\), De Moivre’s formula Differentiating and integrating Exponential The exponential is its own derivative, up to a factor Euler’s formula \\[ e^{j\\theta} = \\cos\\theta + j\\,\\operatorname{sin}\\theta \\qquad \\cos\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} + e^{-j\\theta}}{2} \\qquad \\operatorname{sin}\\theta = \\frac{e^{j\\theta} - e^{-j\\theta}}{2j} \\] It is the bridge between what oscillates and what grows: it turns sines and cosines into exponentials, which are far easier to differentiate, integrate and multiply. The whole phasor method used in alternating current comes from here. Lab · the complex plane Click or tap the plane to move the point, or use the sliders. The operations are drawn on the same plane. Just the number z Conjugate of z Multiply by w Inverse 1/z Square it Cube roots of z Mark a region Modulus |z| = 1.50 Argument θ = 40 ° |z − 1| < 1 · a disk 1 < |z| < 2 · an annulus Re(z) > 0 · right half-plane Re(z) < 0 · left half-plane, the stable zone |z| < 1 · unit disk, discrete-time sta"},{"t":"What each operation does in the plane","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#operaciones","x":"03 What each operation does in the plane Every operation with complex numbers is a geometric motion. Seeing it that way avoids memorizing formulas: Operation In the plane Where it appears Adding Placing the vectors head to tail Adding two out-of-phase voltages Multiplying by \\( r\\,e^{j\\theta} \\) Scaling by \\( r \\) and rotating by \\( \\theta \\) Passing through a network that attenuates and shifts phase Multiplying by \\( j \\) Rotating 90° without changing the size Differentiating a sinusoid; inductive reactance Conjugating Reflecting across the real axis Impedance matching: \\( Z_L = Z_S^* \\) Inverting \\( 1/z \\) Inverting the modulus and changing the sign of the angle Going from impedance to admittance Squaring Doubling the angle and squaring the modulus A mixer, which generates twice the frequency The n roots A complex number has \\( n \\) nth roots, spread over the vertices of a regular polygon inscribed in a circle of radius \\( \\sqrt[n]{r} \\): \\[ z_k = \\sqrt[n]{r}\\ e^{\\,j\\left(\\frac{\\theta + 2\\pi k}{n}\\right)}, \\qquad k = 0, 1, \\ldots, n-1 \\] Those roots are the ones that appear when you solve the characteristic equation of a system, and their position in the plane decides whether the response oscillates, dies out or grows."},{"t":"Regions: where an inequality draws a figure","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#regiones","x":"04 Regions: where an inequality draws a figure An inequality between complex numbers does not define an interval, as with the reals, but a region of the plane. Recognizing them by heart saves a lot of work: Condition Region What it means in the degree \\( |z - z_0| = r \\) Circle centered at \\( z_0 \\) A locus of constant modulus \\( |z - z_0| \\lt r \\) Open disk Neighborhood of a point: the basis of limits \\( r_1 \\lt |z| \\lt r_2 \\) Annulus Region of convergence of the Z-transform \\( \\operatorname{Re}(z) \\lt 0 \\) Left half-plane Stable zone of Laplace \\( |z| \\lt 1 \\) Unit disk Stable zone of the Z-transform \\( |\\arg z| \\lt \\alpha \\) Angular sector Minimum damping of a system The two highlighted rows are the reason this course starts with complex variable: the question “is this system stable?” is answered by looking at where its poles fall in the plane."},{"t":"The phasor, which is all of this together","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#fasores","x":"05 The phasor, which is all of this together A sinusoidal signal \\( v(t) = V_p \\cos(\\omega t + \\varphi) \\) can be written as the real part of a rotating complex exponential: \\[ v(t) = \\operatorname{Re}\\left\\{ V_p\\,e^{j(\\omega t + \\varphi)} \\right\\} = \\operatorname{Re}\\left\\{ \\underbrace{V_p e^{j\\varphi}}_{\\text{phasor}} \\cdot e^{j\\omega t} \\right\\} \\] The phasor stores amplitude and phase; \\( e^{j\\omega t} \\) is the rotation, which is common to all the signals in the circuit and cancels out. With that, a circuit with derivatives and integrals becomes an algebraic one: differentiating is multiplying by \\( j\\omega \\) and integrating is dividing by \\( j\\omega \\). From there come \\( Z_L = j\\omega L \\) and \\( Z_C = 1/(j\\omega C) \\), and Ohm’s law works again, now with complex numbers. The phasor assumes sinusoidal steady state It holds when everything oscillates at the same frequency and the transient has already died out. For switch-on, for a pulse or for an arbitrary signal, you need the more general tool studied later: Laplace, which is this same idea with an exponential that also grows or dies out, \\( e^{st} \\) with \\( s = \\sigma + j\\omega \\)."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring a phasor With a generator and a two-channel oscilloscope, measure the amplitude and phase shift in an RC circuit. Draw the input phasor and the output phasor on graph paper and check the angle with \\( \\varphi = -\\arctan(\\omega RC) \\). Activity 2 · The roots, on the screen Compute by hand the five fifth roots of unity and plot them. Check that they form a regular pentagon and that their sum is zero. Activity 3 · Impedance and admittance For a series RLC at three frequencies, compute \\( Z \\) in rectangular and in polar form, and its inverse \\( Y = 1/Z \\). Verify that inverting reflects the point across the unit circle and changes the sign of the angle, as in the lab on this page."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Confusing \\( j \\) with \\( i \\). In electronics \\( i \\) is current; the imaginary unit is \\( j \\). Adding in polar form. To add you have to convert to rectangular; only the product and the quotient are convenient in polar. Losing the quadrant when computing the angle with \\( \\arctan(b/a) \\): you have to look at the signs of \\( a \\) and \\( b \\), or use the two-argument function. Mixing degrees and radians in the same calculation. Forgetting that the nth root has n results and keeping only one. Writing the phasor with the peak value when you had been working in RMS, or the other way around."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment Write \\( z = 3 + j4 \\) in polar form. \\( r = \\sqrt{9+16} = 5 \\) and \\( \\theta = \\arctan(4/3) \\approx 53.13^\\circ \\), that is, \\( 5\\,e^{j53.13^\\circ} \\). What does multiplying a phasor by \\( j \\) do to it? It rotates it 90° counterclockwise without changing its modulus. It is what differentiating a sinusoid does. What region is \\( 1 \\lt |z| \\lt 2 \\)? An annulus centered at the origin, between radii 1 and 2. It is the typical shape of a region of convergence in the Z-transform. Where do the poles of a continuous-time system have to be for it to be stable? In the left half-plane, with negative real part: that way the exponential \\( e^{st} \\) dies out instead of growing. How many cube roots does 8 have and where are they? Three, of modulus 2, spaced 120° apart: \\( 2 \\), \\( 2e^{j120^\\circ} \\) and \\( 2e^{j240^\\circ} \\). Only the first is real. Why does the conjugate appear in impedance matching? Because maximum power transfer occurs with \\( Z_L = Z_S^* \\): the same resistance and the opposite reactance, so that they cancel."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"The complex plane and its regions","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/plano-complejo#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Murray R. Spiegel. Complex Variables. Schaum’s Outline Series (Spanish edition, “Variable compleja”), McGraw-Hill, 2011. Concise theory and hundreds of solved problems: the classic companion for this part of the course. Erwin Kreyszig. Advanced Engineering Mathematics. Wiley (Spanish edition, “Matemáticas avanzadas para ingeniería, volumen 2”, 3rd ed.), Limusa Wiley, 2003. Complex variable from the engineer’s point of view, with good application examples. Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Pearson, 1997. The book for the whole course; worth having from day one. Charles K. Alexander and Matthew N. O. Sadiku. Fundamentals of Electric Circuits. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Fundamentos de circuitos eléctricos”), McGraw-Hill, 2013. The chapter on phasors, to see these complex numbers at work measuring real circuits."},{"t":"The direction in which the field oscillates","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#estados","x":"01 The direction in which the field oscillates The fact that the electromagnetic wave is transverse leaves a freedom that longitudinal waves do not have: the electric field can oscillate in any direction in the plane perpendicular to the propagation. That direction is the polarization, and sound does not have it because it oscillates in the same direction in which it travels. State What the field does How it is achieved Linear Always oscillates in the same plane A polarizer, or a single-element antenna Circular Rotates, describing a circle Two equal components shifted by 90° Elliptical Rotates, describing an ellipse The general case: any amplitudes or phase shift Unpolarized Changes randomly all the time Any thermal source: the Sun, a lamp The last row is the natural state of almost all light: a lamp emits photons from atoms that are not coordinated with each other, and the result averages over all directions. Polarizing it is filtering, and that is why it always costs energy. Circular polarization is not a detail In satellite communications circular polarization is used because the relative orientation between the satellite and the ground antenna changes, and with linear polarization it would have to be tracked. Also a right-hand circular wave and a left-hand one are independent: two channels can be transmitted on the same frequency. And in the ionosphere, Faraday rotation turns the plane of a linear wave unpredictably, something that does not affect a circular one."},{"t":"Malus’s law","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#malus","x":"02 Malus’s law A polarizer lets through only the component of the field parallel to its axis. Since the intensity goes with the square of the field, the fraction that passes is: \\[ I = I_0\\cos^2\\theta \\] With \\( \\theta \\) the angle between the incoming polarization and the axis of the polarizer. If the light enters unpolarized, the first polarizer lets through exactly half, whatever its orientation: it is the average of \\( \\cos^2 \\) over all angles. Lab · cascaded polarizers Three polarizers with adjustable axes. Start with the first and the third crossed at 90°: nothing gets through. Then insert the middle one at 45° and see what happens. The incoming light is unpolarized Use the middle polarizer Axis of the first = 0° Axis of the second = 45° Axis of the third = 90° Why the middle one lets light through It seems contradictory that adding a filter could increase what gets through, and it is not. The middle polarizer does not merely filter: it reorients. What comes out of it is polarized at 45°, and that direction does have a component along the axis of the third. Without it, what reaches the third is perpendicular to its axis and has none. It is the cleanest demonstration that a polarizer projects a vector, rather than simply letting through “the light that was already there.”"},{"t":"Polarizing without a polarizer","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#brewster","x":"03 Polarizing without a polarizer Reflection polarizes. When light strikes a transparent surface at the Brewster angle, what is reflected is completely polarized in the plane parallel to the surface: \\[ \\tan\\theta_B = \\frac{n_2}{n_1} \\] For air and glass it gives about 56°, and for air and water, 53°. That is the basis of polarized sunglasses: the glare from asphalt or water is mostly horizontal, so a vertical filter removes it and lets the rest through. Phenomenon What it does Where it appears Brewster reflection Polarizes what is reflected Polarized sunglasses, laser windows Rayleigh scattering Polarizes the sky at 90° from the Sun Photography filters; orientation of some insects Birefringence Two indices depending on the polarization Quarter-wave plates, calcite Optical activity Rotates the plane of polarization Measuring sugar concentration The screen you are looking at An LCD is pure polarization. Behind it there is a white lamp, in front two crossed polarizers —which by themselves would give black— and in between a layer of liquid crystal whose molecules rotate the plane of polarization. With no voltage, the layer rotates it 90° and the light passes through both polarizers: pixel on. With voltage, the molecules align, stop rotating anything and the second polarizer blocks: pixel off. Each pixel is a Malus experiment, and that is why the screen of a laptop goes dark when viewed through rotated polarized sunglasses."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Verifying Malus With two polarizers, a stable source and a photodiode with an amplifier, measure the intensity every ten degrees of rotation of the second. Plot it against \\( \\cos^2\\theta \\) and verify that it gives a straight line. Activity 2 · The third one in the middle Cross two polarizers until extinction and measure. Insert a third between them and sweep it from 0 to 90°, measuring. Locate the maximum and compare it with the 12.5 % predicted by theory. Activity 3 · Brewster angle Reflect a beam off a glass plate while varying the angle and observe the reflection through a polarizer. Find the extinction angle and from it obtain the refractive index of the glass."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing that a polarizer “lets through the light that was already in its direction.” It projects: it transmits the component. Expecting the first one to let through more than half. With unpolarized light it is always 50 %, in any orientation. Thinking that adding a filter cannot increase what gets through. The middle one proves otherwise. Using Malus with unpolarized light. Malus holds from the second polarizer onward. Confusing circular polarization with unpolarized light. Circular is perfectly defined; it just rotates. Forgetting that polarization is transverse. Sound is not polarized: it oscillates in the direction in which it travels."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How much light passes through the first polarizer? Half, if it enters unpolarized, whatever its orientation. What fraction passes through three polarizers at 0°, 45° and 90°? One eighth: \\( \\tfrac12 \\times \\cos^2 45° \\times \\cos^2 45° = 12.5\\ \\% \\) of the light that entered. Why does the middle one let light through if the other two are crossed? Because it reorients: what comes out of it is at 45°, and that direction does have a component along the axis of the third. What is the Brewster angle? The angle of incidence at which the reflected light is totally polarized. Its tangent is the ratio of the indices. Why do satellites use circular polarization? Because it does not depend on the relative orientation, and the ionosphere rotates the plane of a linear wave unpredictably. How does an LCD pixel work? Two crossed polarizers with liquid crystal in between: with no voltage it rotates the polarization 90° and light passes; with voltage it does not rotate it and the second polarizer blocks it."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Polarization","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polarizacion#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Eugene Hecht. Optics. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Óptica”), Pearson, 2017. The whole of chapter 8: states of polarization, Malus, Brewster, birefringence and retarder plates. Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed. (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”), Cengage, 2019. The introductory treatment, with the exercises on cascaded polarizers. Ernst Lueder. Liquid Crystal Displays: Addressing Schemes and Electro-Optical Effects. 2nd ed., Wiley, 2010. How a display is built from two polarizers and a layer of liquid crystal."},{"t":"Why there is technology policy","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#por-que","x":"01 Why there is technology policy No country buys development off a supermarket shelf. What can be bought are products; the capacity to design, maintain and improve them is built, and that building takes decades and decisions. Technology policy is the set of public decisions about what gets researched, what is produced locally, what is imported and who pays the bill. The underlying question Should a country produce what it already knows how to make cheaply —comparative advantages— or build capabilities in sectors where it is not yet competitive, betting that it will become so? Comparative advantages are built: nobody was born making semiconductors. The discussion is about how much it costs to build them, who finances it and for how long. For an electronic engineer this is not abstract: it determines whether you will work designing or assembling, whether there are laboratories where a product can be certified, whether funding exists for a development, and whether your knowledge has somewhere to be applied without emigrating."},{"t":"Sábato’s triangle","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#sabato","x":"02 Sábato’s triangle In 1968, the Argentine physicist Jorge Sábato and the political scientist Natalio Botana published the most cited model in the region. It says that a country’s technological capacity does not depend on having good scientists or good factories separately, but on the relationships among three vertices: Lab · the triangle, vertex by vertex Tap each vertex or each side to see what it represents, and then try the cases: the diagnosis changes depending on which relationship is weak. — Choose a case to diagnose — A country that assembles products designed abroad Plenty of published research, little transfer A national satellite developed by a state-owned company A small electronics company that wants to certify a product Sábato distinguished three types of relationships: intra-vertex (how each one is organized internally), inter-vertex (the sides of the triangle) and extra-vertex (the links with the outside world). His thesis: underdeveloped countries tend to have reasonable vertices and weak sides. Good scientists who do not talk to industry; industry that imports technology instead of asking for it domestically; a State that funds without coordinating."},{"t":"Development models and their effects","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#modelos","x":"03 Development models and their effects Model Idea Risk Import substitution Produce locally what was previously bought abroad, protecting the domestic market Without a competitiveness requirement, a country can end up making expensive, outdated goods Openness and specialization Produce what you are already competitive in and import the rest Capabilities are lost that later take decades to rebuild Sector promotion Tax benefits or credit for a chosen sector If the benefit does not require local design in return, assembly is funded with no transfer Public procurement as an engine The State buys from local suppliers and thereby creates a market Requires technical capacity in the buyer to specify and control properly The assembly trap Putting together equipment from imported kits creates jobs and saves foreign currency in the short term, but if the regime does not require incorporating design, product engineering and local suppliers, no installed capacity remains when the benefit ends. The industrial promotion regime of Tierra del Fuego —in force since 1972 under Law 19,640— is the Argentine case where that debate was most intense, with strong arguments on both sides: jobs and productive sovereignty on one, fiscal cost and little local integration on the other."},{"t":"The Argentine system, in concrete terms","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#argentina","x":"04 The Argentine system, in concrete terms Argentina has a science and technology system that is comparatively dense for the region: Institution What it does Where it touches electronics CONICET Argentina’s National Scientific and Technical Research Council: research in all disciplines, with its own institutes and in universities Materials, microelectronics, signal processing INTI The National Institute of Industrial Technology: industrial technology, testing and certification Laboratories for electromagnetic compatibility, electrical safety, metrology CNEA The National Atomic Energy Commission: nuclear energy and its associated technologies Nuclear instrumentation, control electronics, detectors CONAE The National Space Activities Commission: space activities Satellites, communication systems, telemetry Agencia I+D+i The national R&D&I Agency: funds projects through competitive grants Subsidies and loans for technological development in companies Universities They educate, and they also do research and transfer The UTN, with its network of regional faculties, is a clear case of a link with industry The underlying legal framework is Law 25,467 on Science, Technology and Innovation (2001), which organizes the system and defines responsibilities. Multi-year plans and later funding laws were built on it. Two cases worth knowing INVAP, a state-owned company from Río Negro province born of an agreement with the CNEA, designs and exports nuclear research reactors, radars and satell"},{"t":"The regional dimension","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#regional","x":"05 The regional dimension No country in the region has, by itself, the market that would justify a state-of-the-art semiconductor fab: a modern plant costs tens of billions of dollars. Hence the importance of regional integration —common standards, joint purchasing, shared projects— and of choosing well the niches where it is possible to compete: scientific instrumentation, electronics for agriculture, embedded systems, control software, engineering services. The realistic strategy is not “make everything” but to position yourself in the higher-value links: designing, integrating, certifying and maintaining, even if the wafer comes from abroad. That requires trained engineers, testing laboratories and continuity in the rules."},{"t":"Activities","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#laboratorio","x":"06 Activities Activity 1 · Diagnosis with the triangle Choose an electronic product made in Argentina and map its triangle: what each vertex does, which side is weakest and what specific measure would strengthen it. Activity 2 · Tracking down a policy instrument Find a currently open call for funding technological development, read the terms and summarize: who can apply, what it funds, what counterpart it requires and what outcome is expected. Activity 3 · Debate with data Prepare, in two groups, the arguments for and against the Tierra del Fuego promotion regime, with figures on employment, fiscal cost and local integration. Each group must be able to state the best version of the opposing argument."},{"t":"Ideas worth revisiting","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#errores","x":"07 Ideas worth revisiting “Good scientists are enough.” Sábato showed that without a link to production, knowledge does not turn into capacity. “Technology is just imported, end of story.” The artifact is imported; the capacity to maintain it, adapt it and improve it does not come in the box. Confusing assembling with manufacturing. The difference lies in where the design is done. Assuming that technology policy is only money. Public procurement, standards, certification and training weigh as much as funding. Measuring success only in publications or only in revenue: the triangle asks you to look at both and, above all, at the relationship between them."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What are the three vertices of Sábato’s triangle? Government, the productive structure and the scientific-technological infrastructure. What does it mean for the sides to be weak? That the vertices exist but are not linked: research does not reach production, industry does not demand local knowledge and the State funds without coordinating. What is the risk of industrial promotion with no local design requirement? That the assembly of imported kits becomes entrenched: there are jobs while the benefit lasts, but no technological capacity is left installed. Name two institutions of the Argentine system and what they contribute to electronics. For example, the INTI with electromagnetic compatibility and electrical safety testing, and the CONAE with the development of satellite systems. Why do people speak of “built” comparative advantages? Because competitiveness in a sector is not a natural given: it is built with investment, training and time. No country was born making semiconductors."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Policies for national and regional development","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/politicas-desarrollo#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading Jorge A. Sábato and Natalio Botana. La ciencia y la tecnología en el desarrollo futuro de América Latina. 1968; reprinted in several anthologies (in Spanish). The original text of the triangle, short and still relevant. Oscar Varsavsky. Ciencia, política y cientificismo. CEAL, 1969 (in Spanish). An uncomfortable and fundamental critique: what research is for and for whom it is done in a peripheral country. Amílcar Herrera. Ciencia y política en América Latina. Siglo XXI, 1971 (in Spanish). Distinguishes explicit science policy from implicit science policy, which is the one that really rules. Jorge Katz and Bernardo Kosacoff. El proceso de industrialización en la Argentina. CEPAL / CEAL (in Spanish). Argentine industrial history with data, useful for discussing the models in section 3."},{"t":"The two numbers that define a circuit","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#que","x":"01 The two numbers that define a circuit A lumped-parameter transfer function is always a quotient of polynomials, and every polynomial can be factored. So: \\[ H(s) = K\\,\\frac{(s - z_1)(s - z_2)\\cdots(s - z_m)}{(s - p_1)(s - p_2)\\cdots(s - p_n)} \\] The \\( z_i \\) are the zeros: there \\( H \\) is zero. The \\( p_k \\) are the poles: there \\( H \\) goes to infinity. That factorization says something powerful: a constant and a handful of points of the plane contain all the information about the circuit. The frequency response, the step response, the stability and even the settling time come from reading where those points are. How to read each one The real part of a pole tells how fast that component of the response dies out: a pole at \\( \\sigma \\) contributes \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\). The imaginary part tells at what frequency it oscillates. A real pole gives an exponential; a conjugate pair, a damped sinusoid."},{"t":"The frequency response, read with a ruler","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#geometria","x":"02 The frequency response, read with a ruler To obtain the frequency response, \\( H \\) is evaluated on the imaginary axis, that is, at \\( s = j\\omega \\). Taking the modulus and phase of the factorization: \\[ |H(j\\omega)| = |K|\\,\\frac{\\prod_i |\\,j\\omega - z_i\\,|}{\\prod_k |\\,j\\omega - p_k\\,|}, \\qquad \\angle H(j\\omega) = \\sum_i \\angle(j\\omega - z_i) - \\sum_k \\angle(j\\omega - p_k) \\] Each factor \\( j\\omega - p_k \\) is the vector that goes from the pole to the operating point on the axis. The magnitude of the response is the product of the distances to the zeros divided by the product of the distances to the poles. With a ruler and a protractor on the diagram you can estimate the Bode plot without computing anything. From this come the two rules that an electronics engineer uses all the time: bringing a pole closer to the imaginary axis raises a peak at that frequency, because the denominator distance becomes small; and a zero on the axis produces a null, because the numerator vanishes. Lab · the diagram and its response On the left, the \\( s \\)-plane with the poles (×) and zeros (○), and the vectors going to the operating point. On the right, the magnitude in decibels and the phase. Click or tap the plane to move the pole pair. First-order low-pass Second-order low-pass Second-order high-pass Band-pass Notch · band-reject All-pass Show the vectors Operating frequency ω = 1.00 rad/s Q = 0.71"},{"t":"Stability: a single line divides the plane","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#estabilidad","x":"03 Stability: a single line divides the plane The natural response of a system is the sum of the terms \\( e^{p_k t} \\), one per pole. Whether it dies out or not depends only on the sign of the real part: Where the pole is What it contributes to the response Result \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) \\lt 0 \\) \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\) with \\( \\sigma \\lt 0 \\) Dies out · stable \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) = 0 \\), simple Oscillation of constant amplitude Marginally stable \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) = 0 \\), double \\( t\\cos\\omega t \\), grows Unstable \\( \\operatorname{Re}(p) \\gt 0 \\) \\( e^{\\sigma t} \\) with \\( \\sigma \\gt 0 \\) Grows without bound · unstable Zeros do not affect stability: they can be anywhere. They do affect the shape of the response, and a zero in the right half-plane —what is called non-minimum phase — makes the output start off in the wrong direction before correcting. This is what happens in a boost converter and is one of the reasons they are hard to compensate. The oscillator lives on the line A sinusoidal oscillator needs a pair of poles exactly on the imaginary axis. Since that cannot be sustained with real components, they are designed slightly to the right and an amplitude control is added —a small lamp, a JFET, a limiter— that pushes them toward the axis when the amplitude grows. The circuit settles by itself onto the line."},{"t":"The cases you have to recognize by heart","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#casos","x":"04 The cases you have to recognize by heart Function Poles and zeros What it does \\( \\dfrac{1}{s + \\omega_0} \\) One real pole Low-pass, −20 dB/dec, −45° at \\( \\omega_0 \\) \\( \\dfrac{s}{s + \\omega_0} \\) A zero at the origin First-order high-pass \\( \\dfrac{\\omega_0^2}{s^2 + \\frac{\\omega_0}{Q}s + \\omega_0^2} \\) Conjugate pair Low-pass with a peak if \\( Q \\gt 0.707 \\) \\( \\dfrac{s^2 + \\omega_0^2}{s^2 + \\frac{\\omega_0}{Q}s + \\omega_0^2} \\) Zeros on the axis Notch: exact null at \\( \\omega_0 \\) \\( \\dfrac{s - a}{s + a} \\) Zero and pole in mirror image All-pass: flat magnitude, only the phase changes The all-pass deserves attention because it is counterintuitive: it has a zero in the right half-plane and yet is perfectly stable, because the pole is on the left. Its magnitude is 1 at all frequencies and all it does is delay. It is used to equalize delays and to correct phase without touching the amplitude."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#laboratorio","x":"05 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the peak and deducing Q Build a series RLC with variable \\( R \\) and measure the frequency response. Locate the peak, measure its height relative to the passband and solve for \\( Q \\). Compare with \\( Q = \\frac{1}{R}\\sqrt{L/C} \\) and locate the poles in the plane. Activity 2 · The power-line notch Design a notch at 50 Hz with \\( Q = 10 \\), compute the positions of its two zeros and two poles, and verify in the lab how many decibels of attenuation it gives at 50 Hz and how much it affects 45 Hz and 55 Hz. Activity 3 · The phase of the all-pass Build a first-order all-pass with an operational amplifier and measure magnitude and phase between \\( 0.1\\,a \\) and \\( 10\\,a \\). Check that the magnitude stays flat while the phase sweeps 180°."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#errores","x":"06 Common mistakes Believing that a zero in the right half-plane makes the system unstable. Stability is decided by the poles, nothing else. Forgetting the zeros at infinity. If the denominator has a higher degree than the numerator, there are zeros at infinity, and that fixes the final slope of the Bode plot. Confusing \\( \\omega_0 \\) with the frequency of the peak. The peak is at \\( \\omega_0\\sqrt{1 - 1/(2Q^2)} \\), slightly below. Drawing a conjugate pair without its mirror partner. With real coefficients, complex poles always come in pairs. Canceling an unstable pole with a zero. On paper they cancel; in the real circuit the cancellation is never exact and the unstable mode is still there."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#autoevaluacion","x":"07 Self-assessment What is a pole and what is a zero? A zero is a value of \\( s \\) where \\( H \\) vanishes; a pole, where it becomes infinite. They are the roots of the numerator and of the denominator. How do you estimate \\( |H(j\\omega)| \\) by looking at the diagram? Product of the distances to the zeros divided by the product of the distances to the poles, all times \\( |K| \\). A pole at \\( -0.1 \\pm j10 \\): what response does it give? An oscillation of 10 rad/s that decays with a time constant of 10 s. It is a high \\( Q \\), around 50: in frequency it will show a pronounced peak. Why does the all-pass have constant magnitude? Because the zero and the pole are mirror images across the imaginary axis: for each \\( j\\omega \\) the two distances are equal and the quotient is 1. What does bringing a pole closer to the imaginary axis do to the circuit? It raises a peak at that frequency and makes the time response take longer to die out. On the axis, it oscillates without damping. What is a non-minimum-phase system? One with zeros in the right half-plane. It is stable, but its output starts off in the opposite direction before heading to the final value."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Poles and zeros","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/polos-y-ceros#ampliar","x":"08 Further reading Alan V. Oppenheim and Alan S. Willsky. Signals and Systems. 2nd ed. (Spanish edition, “Señales y sistemas”), Prentice Hall, 1998. The Laplace chapter, with the relationship between pole location and time response. Adel S. Sedra and Kenneth C. Smith. Microelectronic Circuits. 7th ed. (Spanish edition, “Circuitos microelectrónicos”), Oxford University Press, 2016. The filters chapter: each classic response with its pole-zero diagram alongside. Katsuhiko Ogata. Modern Control Engineering. 5th ed. (Spanish edition, “Ingeniería de control moderna”), Pearson, 2010. The root locus, which is the study of how the poles move as a gain is varied."},{"t":"The power that travels along the line","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#potencia","x":"01 The power that travels along the line In a transmission line the power travels in two waves: the incident one, which goes from the generator to the load, and the reflected one, which comes back. Since the characteristic impedance is real in a low-loss line, each wave carries its own power and what the load receives is the difference: \\[ P_{inc} = \\frac{|V^+|^2}{2 Z_0} \\] \\[ P_{ref} = |\\Gamma_L|^2\\, P_{inc} \\] \\[ P_L = P_{inc}\\,\\left(1 - |\\Gamma_L|^2\\right) \\] With peak amplitudes. A directional power meter, the instrument of every transmitter, measures the incident and the reflected power separately; the SWR comes from their ratio. With an SWR of 2, 11% of the incident power is reflected; with an SWR of 3, 25%. That power is not necessarily lost: it goes back toward the generator and, if the generator is not matched, it is reflected again toward the load. What is lost is what the line itself dissipates on each trip."},{"t":"The losses of the line","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#perdidas","x":"02 The losses of the line A real line attenuates the wave as it advances. The attenuation is given in decibels per 100 m, with the line matched, and it has two causes with different dependence on frequency: \\[ \\alpha_{dB} \\approx k_1 \\sqrt{f} + k_2\\, f \\] The first term is the resistance of the conductors, which grows with the square root of frequency because of the skin effect; the second is the dielectric, which dissipates in proportion to frequency and dominates at microwaves. Cable (50 Ω) Diameter At 146 MHz At 435 MHz At 2.4 GHz RG-58 5 mm about 20 dB/100 m about 36 dB/100 m about 100 dB/100 m RG-213 10 mm about 9 dB/100 m about 16 dB/100 m about 44 dB/100 m LMR-400 (foam) 10 mm about 5 dB/100 m about 8.5 dB/100 m about 22 dB/100 m The values are typical; each manufacturer publishes its own table. A thicker cable loses less because the current is spread over more surface, and a foam dielectric loses less than a solid one. With 3 dB of loss, half of the power arrives; with 10 dB, one tenth."},{"t":"Mismatch and losses together","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#desadaptacion","x":"03 Mismatch and losses together If the line has losses and the load is also mismatched, the reflected wave is attenuated too on its trip back. This has two consequences. The first: the total loss is greater than that of the matched line, because the power that goes and comes back travels along the cable more than once. With \\( a \\) the attenuation of the matched line as a power ratio (\\( a = 10^{A/10} \\)): \\[ |\\Gamma_{in}| = \\frac{|\\Gamma_L|}{a} \\] \\[ \\frac{P_{in}}{P_L} = \\frac{a^2 - |\\Gamma_L|^2}{a\\,(1 - |\\Gamma_L|^2)} \\] The first expression gives the reflection seen from the transmitter, attenuated twice. The second is the total loss in the line, between the net power that goes in and the power the load receives; with \\( \\Gamma_L = 0 \\) it reduces to \\( a \\). The second consequence is misleading: the cable improves the SWR that the transmitter measures. An antenna with an SWR of 3 at the end of 20 m of RG-58 at 146 MHz looks like SWR 1.5 from the equipment. The transmitter is satisfied, but less than a third of the power reaches the antenna. That is why the SWR is measured, when possible, at the antenna feed point. Lab · how much power reaches the antenna A transmitter delivers 100 W net to a 50 Ω cable that ends in an antenna. The graph shows the power of the incident wave and of the reflected one along the cable, from the transmitter (on the left) to the antenna. The attenuations are typical catalog values. RG-58 RG-213 LMR-400 28 MHz 146 MHz 435 MHz 900 MHz 2.4 GHz Cabl"},{"t":"How much power a cable can take","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#limites","x":"04 How much power a cable can take A cable has two power limits. The thermal one: what it dissipates heats it, and the dielectric softens above a certain temperature; since the losses grow with frequency, the power rating drops with it. The voltage one: with standing waves, the voltage maxima are \\( (1 + |\\Gamma_L|) \\) times those of the matched line, and the dielectric can break down. Manufacturers give the power rating with the line matched and a derating factor for each SWR. For a transmitter, mismatch is also a risk: the reflected power returns to the output stage and the transistors can receive voltages higher than the design values. That is why solid-state transmitters automatically reduce their power when the SWR exceeds a threshold."},{"t":"Where it appears in electronics","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#electronica","x":"05 Where it appears in electronics Case What decides the useful power Amateur radio station The length and type of cable up to the antenna, more than the SWR at the equipment Cellular base station Thick foam cables, or the radio mounted next to the antenna to eliminate the cable Television antenna with amplifier The amplifier goes on the mast, ahead of the cable losses Broadcast transmitter SWR protection and a permanent directional meter The complete link, with the gain of the antennas and the path loss, is put together in the unit on antennas; the cable losses enter that budget by subtracting decibels, just as in the microwave links of the technician program."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#laboratorio","x":"06 In the lab Activity 1 · Measuring the attenuation of a cable With a generator and a power meter, measure the output power of a roll of RG-58 loaded with 50 Ω at several frequencies. Plot the decibels per 100 m against frequency and separate the square-root term from the linear one. Activity 2 · The misleading SWR Terminate the same roll in 150 Ω and measure the SWR at its input with a directional meter. Repeat with a short section and compare with the SWR of the load. Activity 3 · Choosing the cable For an antenna 30 m from the equipment at 435 MHz, compare in catalogs the cost per meter and the power that arrives with RG-58, RG-213 and a foam cable."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#errores","x":"07 Common mistakes Trusting the SWR measured at the equipment. A very lossy cable artificially improves it. Believing that all the reflected power is lost. What is lost is what the line and the generator dissipate, not the reflected power itself. Using the attenuation at one frequency for another. It grows with frequency, and at 2.4 GHz a thin cable is useless. Ignoring the voltage limit at high SWR. A cable that withstands the matched power can break down with a badly mismatched load."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#autoevaluacion","x":"08 Self-assessment What fraction of the incident power is reflected at SWR 3? \\( |\\Gamma| = 0.5 \\), so \\( |\\Gamma|^2 = 25 \\)%. How much power reaches the matched load through a cable with 6 dB of loss if 100 W go in? \\( 100 / 10^{0.6} \\approx 25 \\) W. An antenna with SWR 3 is at the end of a cable with 4 dB of loss. What SWR does the transmitter measure? \\( |\\Gamma_{in}| = 0.5 / 10^{0.4} \\approx 0.2 \\), and the SWR is \\( 1.2 / 0.8 = 1.5 \\). Why does the attenuation of a coaxial cable grow faster than the square root of frequency at microwaves? Because the dielectric loss grows in proportion to frequency and comes to dominate over that of the conductors."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Power in transmission lines","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-lineas#ampliar","x":"09 Further reading H. Ward Silver (ed.). The ARRL Antenna Book. ARRL, periodic editions. Its chapter on transmission lines covers losses with SWR using examples from real stations. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2011. Power in lossy lines and the rigorous definition of return loss and mismatch loss. Walter C. Johnson. Transmission Lines and Networks. McGraw-Hill, 1950. A classic on real lines, with the attenuation of conductors and dielectrics derived step by step."},{"t":"Why power is measured and not voltage","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#porque","x":"01 Why power is measured and not voltage At radio frequency the voltage changes along a line with standing waves, and it depends on where it is measured. The power traveling toward the load, on the other hand, is well defined. That is why RF instruments measure power, in watts or in dBm, with a sensor that absorbs the signal and a meter that reads its output. The relationship between voltage, current and power on lines was covered in power on lines, in Transmission Media."},{"t":"The sensors","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#sensores","x":"02 The sensors Sensor Principle Typical range Feature Thermistor (bolometer) The power heats a thermistor in a bridge, which is rebalanced with DC −20 to +10 dBm DC substitution: traceable, slow; today used in standards Thermocouple The power heats a thermoelectric junction −30 to +20 dBm Measures the true RMS value of any waveform Diode Rectifies the signal; in the square-law region the output is proportional to the power −70 to +20 dBm Very sensitive and fast; outside the square-law region it depends on the waveform Below about −20 dBm, the current of a detector diode is proportional to the square of the voltage, that is, to the power, for any modulation. Above that it begins to behave as a peak detector, and with modulated signals the result depends on the waveform. Modern diode sensors combine several paths or correct in software, and peak power sensors sample the envelope to measure radar pulses or bursts of digital communications."},{"t":"Calibration factor and mismatch uncertainty","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#desadaptacion","x":"03 Calibration factor and mismatch uncertainty No sensor absorbs all the power it receives: part is reflected and part is lost before reaching the sensing element. The manufacturer measures its calibration factor as a function of frequency, and the meter applies it. What cannot be corrected without measuring phases is the interaction between the source and the sensor: the multiple reflections between the two add or subtract depending on the phase, and leave a mismatch uncertainty: \\[ M_u = 20 \\log_{10}\\big(1 \\pm |\\Gamma_s|\\,|\\Gamma_L|\\big)\\ \\ \\mathrm{dB} \\] \\[ |\\Gamma| = \\frac{\\mathrm{SWR} - 1}{\\mathrm{SWR} + 1} \\] \\( \\Gamma_s \\) is the reflection coefficient of the source and \\( \\Gamma_L \\) that of the sensor. With a source of SWR 1.5 and a sensor of 1.2, the uncertainty is ±0.16 dB, more than that of many meters. In practice it is often the largest component of the uncertainty budget. Lab · mismatch uncertainty The reflections between the source and the sensor. The chart shows the measured power relative to the true one as a function of the phase between the two reflections, which is not known: the band between the maximum and the minimum is the uncertainty. Source SWR = Sensor SWR = The way to reduce it is to improve the match on whichever side can be improved: a 10 dB attenuator between the source and the sensor improves the match seen by the sensor, at the cost of level. Mismatch uncertainty also affects the uncertainty budget of any RF measurement."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Square-law region Use a diode sensor to measure a continuous signal and a 100% AM-modulated one at several levels, and observe where the reading stops matching the true power. Activity 2 · Mismatch Measure the power of a generator through a cable of variable length with a poorly matched load in the middle, and observe how the reading changes with the length. Activity 3 · Attenuator Repeat the measurement with a 10 dB attenuator at the generator output and compare the scatter."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Forgetting the calibration factor for the working frequency. It can change by several percent across the band. Measuring modulated signals with a diode outside its square-law region. The reading depends on the waveform. Ignoring the mismatch. It is often the largest source of uncertainty. Exceeding the maximum power of the sensor. Diode sensors are damaged a few dBm above their range."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment What reflection coefficient corresponds to an SWR of 2? 1/3. What is the mismatch uncertainty with an SWR of 2 at the source and 1.3 at the sensor? \\( |\\Gamma_s||\\Gamma_L| = 0.333 \\cdot 0.130 = 0.043 \\): from +0.37 to −0.39 dB. Which sensor would you use to measure the RMS value of a heavily modulated signal? A thermocouple, or a diode in its square-law region. Why does an attenuator reduce the mismatch uncertainty? Because the reflections returning toward the source pass through it twice and arrive attenuated: the sensor sees a better-matched source."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"RF power measurement","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/potencia-rf#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Keysight Technologies. Fundamentals of RF and Microwave Power Measurements. Application Note 1449, in four parts, Keysight. The sensors, the meters and the calculation of the uncertainty. David M. Pozar. Microwave Engineering. 4th ed., Wiley, 2012. The reflection coefficient and mismatch in microwave networks. Clyde F. Coombs Jr. (ed.). Electronic Instrument Handbook. 3rd ed., McGraw-Hill, 1999. RF power meters and their sensors."},{"t":"Accidents have causes","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#causas","x":"01 Accidents have causes Prevention starts from a simple idea: an accident is not bad luck but the result of causes that can be identified and eliminated. Three models shaped the way we think about it: Model Central idea Consequence for prevention Heinrich’s domino (1931) A chain of factors ends in the injury; unsafe acts and conditions are the central domino Removing one domino stops the chain Bird’s pyramid (1969) For every serious injury there are many minor ones, plus property damage and hundreds of incidents without consequences Incidents are warnings: recording them makes it possible to act sooner Reason’s Swiss cheese (1990) Every barrier has holes; the accident occurs when they line up Several independent barriers, and a search for the latent failures of the organization Reason’s model changed the focus: the operator’s error is usually the last failure in a series that began in design, maintenance or organizational decisions. Blaming the person does not eliminate those latent failures."},{"t":"Analytical and operational techniques","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#tecnicas","x":"02 Analytical and operational techniques Prevention techniques are divided into analytical ones, which look for the causes, and operational ones, which act on them: Safety inspections: planned walk-throughs with checklists to detect hazardous conditions before they cause an accident. Accident and incident investigation: the causal tree, developed in France in the 1970s, starts from the event and asks, for each antecedent, what was necessary and sufficient for it to occur, until it reaches facts that can be corrected. It looks for facts, not culprits. Job analysis: dividing the task into steps and listing the hazards and controls for each one. Accident statistics: measuring in order to compare periods and sectors, and to see whether the measures work. Training, signage, standards and procedures: the operational techniques covered in industrial hygiene and safety and in the unit on safe work procedures."},{"t":"Accident rates","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#indices","x":"03 Accident rates Counting accidents is not enough: a large company has more accidents than a small one even if it is safer. The rates relate them to the exposure: \\[ I_I = \\frac{\\text{lost-time accidents}}{\\text{workers}} \\times 10^3 \\] \\[ I_F = \\frac{\\text{lost-time accidents}}{\\text{hours worked}} \\times 10^6 \\] \\[ I_G = \\frac{\\text{workdays lost}}{\\text{hours worked}} \\times 10^3 \\] The incidence rate \\( I_I \\) counts accidents per thousand workers; the frequency rate \\( I_F \\), per million hours, corrects for the length of the working day; the severity rate \\( I_G \\), workdays lost per thousand hours. The average duration of the absences is the ratio of workdays lost to accidents. Lab · rates and pyramid of a plant One year of data from a plant. On the right, the rates; in the chart, Bird’s pyramid scaled to the recorded injuries, using the 1: 10: 30: 600 ratio of the original study, which is only indicative: each activity has its own. Workers = Hours per worker in the year = Lost-time accidents = Workdays lost = The Superintendency of Occupational Risks publishes incidence rates by economic activity, which make it possible to compare a plant with its sector. A low rate with many unrecorded incidents does not indicate safety, but a lack of information."},{"t":"In the laboratory","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#laboratorio","x":"04 In the laboratory Activity 1 · Causal tree Take an accident described in a public report and build its causal tree as a group. Identify which of the facts could have been corrected and with what measure. Activity 2 · Inspection Design a checklist for an electronics laboratory, apply it and classify the findings by severity. Activity 3 · Incident log Propose a short form for recording no-injury incidents in the workshop and discuss how to prevent it from being used to look for culprits."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Ending the investigation at human error. It is the starting point for asking why it was possible. Comparing numbers of accidents between companies of different sizes. Rates are what get compared. Ignoring incidents. They are the base of the pyramid and the cheapest information for prevention. Taking Bird’s ratio as a law. It is an average from one study; what matters is the shape, not the numbers."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment A company of 400 workers had 10 lost-time accidents. What is its incidence rate? 25 per thousand workers. If those workers each worked 1800 hours, what is the frequency rate? \\( 10/720\\,000 \\times 10^6 \\approx 13.9 \\). What does the Swiss cheese model propose for prevention? Several independent barriers, and correcting the latent failures of the organization, not just the last error. What question guides the construction of a causal tree? For each fact: what was necessary and sufficient for it to occur."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"Techniques for preventing accidents","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/prevencion-accidentes#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading James Reason. Human Error. Cambridge University Press, 1990. The origin of the Swiss cheese model and the distinction between active and latent failures. Sidney Dekker. The Field Guide to Understanding 'Human Error'. 3rd ed., CRC Press, 2014. How to investigate accidents without stopping at individual blame. Frank E. Bird and George L. Germain. Practical Loss Control Leadership. International Loss Control Institute, 1985. The accident pyramid and loss control."},{"t":"The First Law: energy is conserved","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#primero","x":"01 The First Law: energy is conserved The internal energy of a system changes in two ways, and only two: the heat that enters and the work that is done. Written like that it seems little, and it is the most general conservation principle there is. \\[ \\Delta U = Q - W \\] With the usual convention: \\( Q \\) positive when heat enters the system, \\( W \\) positive when the system does work on its surroundings. In a complete cycle the system returns to its initial state, so \\( \\Delta U = 0 \\) and all the net heat has been converted into net work. Process What is held constant Consequence Isothermal The temperature \\( \\Delta U = 0 \\), so \\( Q = W \\) Isobaric The pressure \\( W = P\\Delta V \\) Isochoric The volume \\( W = 0 \\), all the heat goes into internal energy Adiabatic No heat exchange \\( Q = 0 \\), the work comes out of \\( U \\) For electronic equipment in steady state the balance is trivial and therefore useful: there is no mechanical work and no accumulation, so all the electrical power that goes in comes out as heat. A 500 W power supply feeding a computer heats the room with 500 W, no matter what it is computing. That is the starting point of any room-cooling calculation."},{"t":"The Second Law: conserving is not enough","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#segundo","x":"02 The Second Law: conserving is not enough The First Law does not forbid a cold cup from heating up by itself by cooling the air: energy would be conserved all the same. What forbids it is the Second, which introduces a quantity that never decreases in an isolated system: \\[ dS = \\frac{\\delta Q_{rev}}{T} \\qquad\\qquad \\Delta S_{universe} \\ge 0 \\] Entropy is what gives time its direction. All spontaneous processes —heat flowing down, a gas expanding, things mixing— go in the direction of increasing it. From this comes the limit that no machine can exceed. A heat engine that takes heat from the hot reservoir, at \\( T_c \\), and delivers it to the cold one, at \\( T_f \\), cannot have an efficiency greater than: \\[ \\eta_{Carnot} = 1 - \\frac{T_f}{T_c} \\] With temperatures in kelvin. It does not depend on the fluid, the design or the technology: it is a ceiling imposed by physics. And it would only be reached with reversible processes, which take infinite time. Lab · efficiency and its limits The same pair of temperatures, four different machines. The diagram shows the energy flows to scale. Look at the Peltier: it is the only one that does not come anywhere near its own Carnot limit, and there lies its whole story. heat engine · converts heat into work refrigerator · removes heat from the cold side heat pump · heats Peltier cell · solid-state cooling Cold reservoir temperature = 0 °C Hot reservoir temperature = 25 °C Why a heat sink cannot cool A heat sink is a passive exchanger: it"},{"t":"The thermodynamic cost of erasing a bit","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#landauer","x":"03 The thermodynamic cost of erasing a bit Here thermodynamics meets computing in a surprising way. Erasing information is an irreversible process: two possible states become one, and that loss of uncertainty is a decrease in the entropy of the system. For the universe not to violate the Second Law, that entropy has to appear in the surroundings as heat: \\[ E_{\\min} = k T \\ln 2 \\approx 2.9\\times10^{-21}\\ \\mathrm{J} \\ \\text{ at 300 K} \\] Landauer's limit, 1961. It is the minimum energy dissipated when erasing a bit, and it does not depend on the technology: it holds for transistors, for relays and for anything that gets invented. That is about three zeptojoules. A current transistor switches spending on the order of a femtojoule, so today's electronics is about three hundred thousand times above the limit. There is room, but the room is not infinite, and that floor explains why reversible computing is researched: if an operation does not destroy information, in principle it has no minimum energy cost. The connection with noise The same \\( kT \\) that appears here is the one in the diode equation, in Arrhenius and in thermal noise \\( \\overline{v^2} = 4kTRB \\). It is no coincidence: in all four cases what is being measured is how much energy thermal agitation brings, and that number —26 meV at room temperature— is the one that sets the scale for almost all of electronics."},{"t":"In the lab","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#laboratorio","x":"04 In the lab Activity 1 · Everything ends up as heat Power a resistor of known power rating inside an insulated container with water and record the temperature rise against time. Calculate the absorbed energy and compare it with the electrical energy delivered. Discuss where the difference went. Activity 2 · A real Peltier Power a Peltier cell with a heat sink on the hot face and measure the temperature of both faces and the electrical power consumed. Calculate the real COP and compare it with the Carnot COP for that same temperature difference. Activity 3 · Efficiency of a power supply Measure input and output power of a switching power supply with a variable load. Plot the efficiency against the load and calculate how much heat the enclosure has to remove in the worst case."},{"t":"Common mistakes","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#errores","x":"05 Common mistakes Believing the Carnot efficiency can be reached. It requires reversible processes, which take infinite time. Using Celsius in the Carnot formula. The temperatures go in kelvin, and with Celsius the result means nothing. Thinking a heat sink cools. It only transports heat, and for that it needs a temperature difference in its favor. Confusing COP with efficiency. The COP of a refrigerator is greater than 1 without violating anything: it is not an efficiency. Believing entropy never decreases. It decreases locally all the time; what does not decrease is that of the universe. Assuming an efficient device does not heat up. All the power that goes in and does not come out as useful signal ends up as heat."},{"t":"Self-assessment","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#autoevaluacion","x":"06 Self-assessment How much heat does a computer that consumes 500 W dissipate? 500 W. There is no mechanical work and no accumulation: all the electrical power ends up as heat. Which quantity gives time its direction? Entropy: it never decreases in an isolated system, and all spontaneous processes increase it. What does the Carnot efficiency depend on? Only on the two temperatures, in kelvin. Not on the fluid or the design. Why can a COP be 4? Because it is not an efficiency: it measures heat moved per unit of work, and moving heat costs less than creating it. How much energy does it cost to erase a bit? At least \\( kT\\ln 2 \\), about 2.9 zeptojoules at 300 K. That is Landauer's limit. Can you cool something below ambient with a heat sink? No. It would require heat to flow uphill by itself. For that you need a machine that consumes work."},{"t":"Further reading","d":"First and Second Laws","u":"/en/publicaciones/mapa-ingenieria/temas/principios-termodinamica#ampliar","x":"07 Further reading Raymond A. Serway and John W. Jewett. Physics for Scientists and Engineers with Modern Physics. 10th ed., Cengage, 2019 (Spanish edition, “Física para ciencias e ingeniería”). First and second laws, heat engines, the Carnot cycle and entropy. Enrico Fermi. Thermodynamics. Dover, 1956 (Spanish edition, “Termodinámica”, Eudeba, reissue). One hundred and fifty pages that explain the whole subject. It remains unsurpassed as a first serious reading. Charles H. Bennett. The Thermodynamics of Computation — a Review. International Journal of Theoretical Physics, vol. 21, 1982. Where Landauer's limit comes from and what reversible computing means."}]);
